/* Chapter 12 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":12,"title":"Participatory Modes: Shirkah and its Variants","part":"part-iii","partRoman":"III","pages":[307,346],"minutes":90,"estimates":{"f1":90,"f2":85},"difficulty":"Advanced","difficultyAlt":"advanced","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch12-1","text":"Define Shirkah and distinguish Shirkatulmilk from Shirkatul‘aqd and its sub-types (Mufawadah, ‘Inan, A‘mal, Wujooh, Mudarabah).","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-2","text":"State the basic rules of Musharakah: partners, capital, management, profit and loss, guarantees and termination.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-3","text":"Explain the concept and rules of Mudarabah: capital, types, work, profit/loss and termination.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-4","text":"Distinguish Mudarabah from Musharakah on seven grounds.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-5","text":"Apply Shirkah principles to joint stock companies and share trading.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-6","text":"Explain modern application of Shirkah on the deposit and asset sides and in securitisation (Boxes 12.2–12.3).","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-7","text":"Explain Diminishing Musharakah in Shirkatulmilk and Shirkatul‘aqd, its procedure and a housing case (Boxes 12.4–12.5).","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-8","text":"Define Shirkah and distinguish Shirkatulmilk (partnership by ownership) from Shirkatul'aqd (partnership by contract) and its sub-types.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-9","text":"State the conditions governing partners, capital, management, and profit/loss treatment in Musharakah (Shirkah al 'Inan).","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-10","text":"Explain Mudarabah's structure, the nature of Mudarabah capital, and how profit/loss is treated and the contract terminated.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-11","text":"Distinguish Mudarabah from Musharakah on investment source, management rights, loss-bearing, liability, and asset ownership.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-12","text":"Explain how modern joint stock companies map onto Shirkah al 'Inan principles, and the rules on trading shares of mixed-asset companies.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-13","text":"Describe how Shirkah is applied on both the deposits and assets sides of Islamic banking, and its use in securitization.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch12-14","text":"Walk through the Diminishing Musharakah structure - the three independent subcontracts (partnership, lease, sale) - and apply it to housing and trade finance scenarios.","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"Shirkah-based modes are what the majority of jurists regard as the most desirable form of Islamic finance: the financier shares real profit and real loss. Almost every exam paper asks for the golden rule — profit as agreed, loss by capital — and for the differences between Musharakah, Mudarabah and Diminishing Musharakah.","whyExpanded":"Participatory (profit-and-loss-sharing) modes are widely regarded by Islamic economists as the truest alternative to interest, since they directly link return to genuine risk-sharing rather than a fixed debt claim. Musharakah and Mudarabah underpin both sides of Islamic banking - deposit mobilization (via Mudarabah pools) and asset-side financing (via Musharakah, Mudarabah, and their most practically important modern derivative, Diminishing Musharakah, which today finances the bulk of Islamic housing and fixed-asset transactions worldwide).","overview":"The chapter sets out the classical law of partnership (Shirkah) — its legality, forms and definition — then the detailed rules of Musharakah and Mudarabah, their differences, and how the same principles govern modern joint stock companies. It then shows how Islamic banks use Shirkah on the deposit side (Mudarabah pools) and the asset side (trade, project and running finance, TFCs, Sukuk), and closes with Diminishing Musharakah for fixed-asset and housing finance, a worked housing case, and accounting treatment.","summary":"Shirkah — combining capital, labour or creditworthiness to share profit and bear loss — is legitimate by Qur’an, Sunnah and consensus, and Shirkah al ‘Inan and Mudarabah enjoy unanimous acceptance. Partnership may be in ownership (Shirkatulmilk, not for profit, co-owners not agents) or by contract (Shirkatul‘aqd: ‘Inan, A‘mal, Wujooh, Mufawadah). In Musharakah capital is preferably money (goods at market value, never debt) and commingled; partners are mutual agents and trustees, so none may guarantee another's capital or profit, though security against negligence and an independent third-party guarantee are allowed. Profit follows agreement as a percentage of actual profit (a declared sleeping partner not above his capital ratio), while loss always follows capital. In Mudarabah the financier bears loss and the Mudarib his effort; debt cannot be capital; restrictions bind the Mudarib; profit exists only after capital is intact. Joint stock companies are Shirkah with limited liability; preference shares are non-compliant and liquid-capital shares trade only at par. On the deposit side banks run Mudarabah pools with disclosed weightages; on the asset side Musharakah suits trade, project and running finance and securitisation (Sitara TFCs). Diminishing Musharakah combines separate partnership, lease and sale contracts; a pre-agreed unit price is allowed only in Shirkatulmilk, making it the practical mode for housing and fixed assets. The author notes that banks underuse Musharakah mainly because Murabaha and leasing are easier.","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 12 establishes Shirkah as the broad Islamic partnership concept underlying both Musharakah (all partners contribute capital and can manage) and Mudarabah (one party provides capital, the other manages, and bears no financial loss). It works through the detailed rules governing partner eligibility, capital, management, and the central profit/loss asymmetry - profit ratios are freely negotiable, but loss must always track capital investment exactly. Modern joint stock companies are shown to be Shirkah al 'Inan with limited liability grafted on, and Shirkah principles are applied across Islamic banking's deposits side (Mudarabah-based pools), assets side (trade and project financing), and securitization (TFCs, PTCs, Sukuk). The chapter closes with Diminishing Musharakah - the most practically important modern derivative - detailing how its three independent subcontracts (partnership, lease, sale) must be kept legally separate, and how the sale-pricing rules differ sharply between housing-style (Shirkatulmilk, pre-fixed price allowed) and trade-style (Shirkatul'aqd, only market-value price, only the financier's promise binding) Diminishing Musharakah.","summarySection":"12.10","takeaways":["Profit as agreed; loss strictly by capital.","No lump-sum profit and no profit as a percentage of capital.","Partners and Mudaribs are trustees: liable only for negligence, misconduct or breach.","Debt cannot be Musharakah or Mudarabah capital.","Seven differences between Musharakah and Mudarabah.","Preference shares and profit-redeemed shares are non-compliant.","Depositors = Rabbul-mal, bank = Mudarib; weightages disclosed.","DM: pre-agreed unit price only in Shirkatulmilk; separate contracts.","Shirkatulmilk (ownership partnership, no agency) and Shirkatul'aqd (contract partnership, mutual agency) are the two fundamental partnership categories; Shirkah al 'Inan and Mudarabah are the only two forms enjoying universal jurist consensus.","The central Musharakah rule: profit-sharing ratio is freely negotiable by agreement (can reflect work contribution), but loss must always be shared exactly per capital investment ratio - the maxim 'profit is based on agreement, loss is always subject to the ratio of investment.'","Mudarabah differs from Musharakah on seven key dimensions: investment source, management rights, loss-bearing, liability scope, profit distribution timing, asset ownership, and dissolution valuation requirements.","Modern joint stock companies are treated as Shirkah al 'Inan with limited liability as the key exception; preference shares are not Shari'ah-compliant, and share tradability depends on whether company capital remains liquid (face value only) or has converted to nonliquid assets (any agreed market price).","Diminishing Musharakah requires its constituent subcontracts (partnership, lease, sale) to be entered separately and remain legally independent, never conditional on one another.","The critical Diminishing Musharakah distinction: in Shirkatulmilk-based DM (e.g. housing), a pre-agreed purchase price IS permissible; in Shirkatul'aqd-based DM (e.g. trade), the price cannot be pre-fixed, and only the financier's promise to sell is binding.","Construction/renovation DM arrangements require a one-year gap after the last disbursement before the bank begins selling units back to the client, specifically to avoid Bai' al 'Inah.","Shirkah-based Sukuk, TFCs, and PTCs generally carry lower moral hazard than direct Musharakah investment, and Musharakah's underuse by Islamic banks stems mainly from documentation, taxation, and effort gaps rather than any inherent Shari'ah limitation."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you classify Shirkah into its forms with examples?","Can you state the rules on Musharakah capital and management?","Can you apply the profit/loss rules including the sleeping partner rule?","Can you list the four conditions for third-party guarantee?","Can you tabulate Musharakah vs Mudarabah?","Can you explain share-trading rules for companies with liquid and mixed assets?","Can you describe running Musharakah and the Sitara TFCs?","Can you compute a DM housing schedule like Box 12.5?"],"topics":[{"id":"t12.1","section":"12.1","title":"Introduction: Participatory (PLS) Modes","pages":[307,308],"tier":"core","concepts":["shirkah","musharakah","mudarabah","pls"],"intuition":"If every Islamic mode involves some risk, what makes partnership modes special?","simpleExplanation":"Islamic modes are asset-based and require taking risk and responsibility. Participatory or profit-and-loss sharing (PLS) modes — Musharakah and Mudarabah, both based on Shirkah — involve direct sharing of profit and loss, and the majority of jurists regard them as the most desirable modes. In Musharakah all partners contribute capital and may work; in Mudarabah one party provides funds and the other manages, and the financier bears the loss while the manager loses his labour.","academicExplanation":["Taking risk and responsibility is a precondition for the legality of profit in any business, so non-participatory modes also carry business risk; Shirkah-based modes, however, involve direct participation in profits and losses.","A partnership may be in the right of ownership (Shirkatulmilk), where a profit motive need not exist, or contractual (Shirkatul‘aqd), where partners agree to run a joint business, share profit in a pre-agreed ratio and bear loss to the extent of investment. A variant is Mudarabah, where one party provides capital and the other manages.","Partnership business was widely practised before Islam; the Prophet (pbuh) traded in partnership before prophethood and Islam approved it. The author notes that Muslim practice appears to have influenced Christian merchants in Europe.","Contemporary jurists use ‘Musharakah’ in a limited sense (all partners provide funds and may work) and in a specific sense (a Mudarib adds his own capital to the Rabbul-mal’s — also permissible).","Modern Shirkah takes the form of partnerships, joint stock companies and cooperatives; the Islamic law of partnership differs from modern law in its religious character. The chapter covers Musharakah, Mudarabah and Diminishing Musharakah, the last being the latest development of Islamic jurisprudence."],"examExplanation":"PLS modes = Musharakah and Mudarabah, based on Shirkah; considered most desirable by the majority of jurists. Musharakah: all contribute capital and may work. Mudarabah: capital from one side, work from the other; financier bears loss, Mudarib loses labour.","keyPoints":["All Islamic modes involve risk; PLS modes involve direct sharing of profit and loss.","Shirkatulmilk vs Shirkatul‘aqd.","Musharakah (limited sense) and Musharakah–Mudarabah combination (specific sense)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Participatory (PLS) modes","definition":"Shirkah-based modes — Musharakah and Mudarabah — in which the parties directly share actual profit and loss."}],"examples":[{"title":"Participatory vs fixed-return finance","kind":"practice","text":"A bank puts 5 million into a client’s trading venture. Under Murabaha it would earn a fixed margin whatever the venture earned. Under Musharakah it earns an agreed share of the actual profit, or bears its share of any loss (pp. 307–308)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t12.2","t4.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Mudarabah, who bears a financial loss that occurs without negligence?","options":["The Mudarib","The financier (Rabbul-mal)","Both equally","The bank's depositors only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The financier bears the loss; the Mudarib loses his labour (p. 308)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.1","sectionTitle":"Introduction","title":"Participatory Modes and the Concept of Shirkah","covers":["t12.1"],"overview":"Introduces Shirkah as the umbrella concept behind Musharakah and Mudarabah, and frames participatory (profit/loss-sharing) modes as the most desirable Islamic financing category.","simpleExplanation":"Shirkah just means 'partnership' - combining resources (money, labor, or even just creditworthiness) with someone else to do business and share the results. Two main partnership-based financing tools come from this concept: Musharakah (everyone puts in capital and can work) and Mudarabah (one side puts in money, the other side manages). These are considered the most genuinely 'Islamic' way to finance things because everyone directly shares in the real profit or loss, rather than one side getting a guaranteed fixed return.","academicExplanation":"Islamic modes are asset-based and require real economic activity plus risk/liability. Participatory or profit/loss-sharing (PLS) modes are widely considered the most desirable by the majority of Islamic finance jurists - though this does not mean non-participatory modes (discussed in prior chapters) involve no business risk; risk-bearing is a precondition for profit legitimacy in ANY mode. What distinguishes Shirkah-based participatory modes specifically is DIRECT participation in profit AND loss by the parties. Two contracts built on the Shirkah concept enable PLS: Mudarabah and Musharakah. Partnership may be Shirkatulmilk (ownership-based, no inherent profit motive required) or Shirkatul'aqd (contract-based, partners agree to conduct joint business for profit, sharing profit at a pre-agreed ratio and loss per each partner's investment). A further variant has one partner providing capital while another manages the business (Mudarabah). Partnership-based business predates Islam - the Prophet (pbuh) himself conducted business on a partnership basis before his prophethood, and many Companions continued the practice; the practice's prevalence among Arabs and Muslims is credited with introducing it into Europe via Muslim contact. In classical Fiqh, joint business is discussed under the broad heading of Shirkah, with the majority of classical jurists treating Mudarabah as a TYPE of Shirkah (broadly defined), though Fiqh books also discuss Mudarabah separately. Contemporary jurists use 'Musharakah' in both a broad sense (contractual partnership where all partners provide funds, not necessarily equally, with a right to work) and a specific sense (an amalgam of Musharakah and Mudarabah, where a Mudarib also employs his own capital alongside the Rabbul-mal's). The key structural difference: in Musharakah, ALL parties contribute funds AND work; in Mudarabah, ONE party contributes funds while the other acts as entrepreneur, sharing profit per a predetermined ratio, with the FINANCIER bearing all loss (the entrepreneur's only 'loss' being his already-expended, uncompensated labour). Modern Shirkah manifests as partnerships, joint stock companies, cooperative societies, and (in a looser sense) pools, cartels, trusts, and syndicates - all treated differently under modern law per their objectives/nature, unlike Islamic partnership law's distinguishing religious character. The chapter covers three main sets of rules: Musharakah, Mudarabah, and Diminishing Musharakah (the last being the newest development, built on broad Shirkah principles).","examExplanation":"Shirkah = the umbrella partnership concept; Mudarabah is treated by most classical jurists as a TYPE of Shirkah (broadly). Musharakah (contemporary usage) = all partners contribute funds + have right to work; Mudarabah = ONE party (Rabbul-mal) contributes funds, ONE party (Mudarib) manages, financier bears ALL loss (entrepreneur only loses his uncompensated labour). Partnership-based business predates Islam and was practiced by the Prophet (pbuh) himself before prophethood. Chapter structure: Musharakah, Mudarabah, Diminishing Musharakah (the newest, Shirkah-derived innovation).","keyPoints":["Risk-bearing is a precondition for profit legitimacy in ANY Islamic financing mode - what distinguishes Shirkah-based (participatory) modes specifically is DIRECT participation in both profit and loss.","Mudarabah is treated by the majority of classical jurists as a TYPE of Shirkah in the broad sense, even though it is also discussed separately in Fiqh literature.","Musharakah (contemporary usage): all partners contribute funds and have a right to work. Mudarabah: one party (Rabbul-mal) contributes funds only; the other (Mudarib) manages the business, with the FINANCIER bearing all loss.","Partnership-based business predates Islam; the Prophet (pbuh) himself conducted business on a partnership basis before his prophethood, and the practice's prevalence among Muslims is credited with introducing it into Europe.","The chapter covers three main rule-sets: Musharakah, Mudarabah, and Diminishing Musharakah - the last being the most recent contemporary development, built on broad Shirkah principles."],"definitions":[{"term":"Shirkah","definition":"The broad Islamic legal concept of partnership - combining capital, labour, or creditworthiness between two or more people, with similar (not necessarily equal) rights and liabilities, to share profit/loss according to their proportionate ownership."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Risk-bearing is a precondition for the legality of profit in any Islamic business mode - a principle that applies broadly, not only to participatory modes."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think only PLS/participatory modes involve genuine risk in Islamic finance, implying trade/lease modes are somehow 'riskless.' The chapter clarifies that risk-bearing is a precondition for ANY mode's profit legitimacy - Shirkah-based modes are distinguished by DIRECT profit/loss participation, not by being the only risk-bearing modes."],"distinctions":["Shirkatulmilk (ownership-based partnership, no inherent profit motive) vs Shirkatul'aqd (contract-based partnership, formed specifically for profit)."],"relatedConcepts":["Musharakah","Mudarabah","Diminishing Musharakah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.1","pages":[307,308]}}]},{"id":"t12.2","section":"12.2","title":"Legality, Forms and Definition of Partnership","pages":[308,312],"tier":"core","concepts":["shirkah"],"intuition":"What is the minimum that two people must combine to be ‘partners’ in Islamic law?","simpleExplanation":"Shirkah is legitimate by Qur’an, Sunnah and consensus; Shirkah al ‘Inan and Mudarabah are accepted by all jurists. The author defines Shirkah as a business where two or more people combine capital, labour or creditworthiness, with similar rights and liabilities, to share profit (or yield or appreciation) and bear loss according to proportionate ownership — so capital is not needed in every structure.","academicExplanation":["Legality: Qur’an 4:12 and 38:24 and the Hadith Qudsi ‘So long as the two partners remain honest to each other, I am the third’; consensus of jurists.","Jurists divide Shirkah into Shirkatulmilk (partnership in ownership) and Shirkatul‘aqd (partnership by contract); with these two forms, traditional Shirkah is the main source of rules for Musharakah, Mudarabah and Diminishing Musharakah today.","‘Profit’ in this definition arises from purchase, sale, hire or wages; it excludes amounts arising from marriage, divorce, maintenance, penalties and fines.","Musharakah and Mudarabah contracts may be for a specific project, a redeemable investment (Diminishing Musharakah) or continuous for the life of the business.","Whether Mudarabah is a form of Shirkah: some Maliki and Hanbali jurists say yes; the Hanafis say no, because the Mudarib is not liable for loss and is only an agent until profit arises. The author considers the difference to reflect analysis of business conditions rather than Shirkah principles."],"examExplanation":"Legality: Qur’an, Sunnah, Ijma‘a; ‘Inan and Mudarabah accepted by all. Definition: combining capital, labour or creditworthiness with similar rights/liabilities to share profit and bear loss by ownership. Two broad categories: Shirkatulmilk and Shirkatul‘aqd.","keyPoints":["Capital is not essential in every Shirkah (A‘mal, Wujooh).","Profit here excludes fines, penalties and family maintenance receipts.","Musharakah may be project-specific, redeemable (diminishing) or continuous.","A Musharakah can be for a specific project, redeemable (Diminishing Musharakah) or continuous, running for as long as the business operates (p. 312)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Shirkah","definition":"A business in which two or more persons combine capital, labour or creditworthiness, with similar rights and liabilities, to share profit, yield or appreciation and bear any loss according to proportionate ownership."}],"examples":[{"title":"Is Mudarabah a kind of Shirkah?","kind":"textbook","text":"A Mudarib has traded for a month with no profit yet, and the goods have fallen in value. For the Hanafis he is still only an agent and bears no loss, which is why they do not count Mudarabah as Shirkah; some Malikis and Hanbalis do (p. 312)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t12.2.1","t12.2.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which two forms of Shirkah enjoy acceptance by all jurists?","options":["Mufawadah and Wujooh","Shirkah al ‘Inan and Mudarabah","A‘mal and Mufawadah","Wujooh and Mudarabah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 308 and p. 311."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.2-12.2.2","sectionTitle":"Legality, Forms and Definition of Partnership / Partnership in Ownership / Partnership by Contract","title":"Shirkatulmilk vs Shirkatul'aqd: The Two Broad Categories of Partnership","covers":["t12.2","t12.2.1","t12.2.2"],"overview":"Defines Shirkah and distinguishes the two fundamental categories - Shirkatulmilk (ownership-based, no agency between partners) and Shirkatul'aqd (contract-based, partners are mutual agents).","simpleExplanation":"There are two very different kinds of 'partnership' in Islamic law. Shirkatulmilk just means jointly owning something - like two people who inherit a house together - without any agency relationship between them; neither can act on the other's behalf. Shirkatul'aqd is a real contractual partnership formed on purpose to do business together and share profit, where each partner acts as the other's agent.","academicExplanation":"Shirkah's legality rests on the Qur'an, Sunnah, and jurist consensus - Shirkah al 'Inan and Mudarabah specifically enjoy universal jurist acceptance. Shirkah is defined as a business where two or more people combine capital, labour, or creditworthiness (capital is not always necessary), with similar rights/liabilities, to share profit/yield/value-appreciation and loss per proportionate ownership. 'Profit' in this context (per Islamic law) arises from purchase, sale, hire, or wages, EXCLUDING income from marriage/divorce contracts, spousal/child subsistence payments, or penalties/fines. Shirkatulmilk (partnership by ownership): the basic element is mixed ownership, either OPTIONAL (e.g. joint purchase, joint gift acceptance, joint bequest acceptance - not inherently for profit-sharing) or COMPULSIVE (e.g. commingled/undistinguishable capital, or joint inheritance). Critically, in Shirkatulmilk, partners are NOT agents of each other - each is a 'stranger' with no right to use the absent partner's property or liability for it, and cannot even use his OWN share if it harms the other's interest. A partner CAN sell his own share to the other partner or to third parties without the other's consent, EXCEPT where property is mixed/associated (in which case selling the other's share to a third party requires permission). If one partner uses joint property, the other may demand rental for his portion. Revenue distribution always follows the ownership proportion. Shirkatul'aqd (partnership by contract): the main, most commonly used form, created via offer and acceptance; AAOIFI defines it as an agreement to combine assets, labour, or liabilities for profit. In sharp contrast to Shirkatulmilk, in Shirkatul'aqd, partners ARE agents of each other, and one partner cannot sell his share without others' consent, nor guarantee the capital/profit of other partners. It subdivides into: Shirkatulamwal (capital-based, all partners invest capital, collective ownership per capital ratio), Shirkatula'mal (labour/service-based, partners jointly render services and share fees per an agreed ratio, each contributing their own resources if needed), and Shirkatulwujooh (creditworthiness-based, partners access market credit using their own credibility and sell the resulting commodity to share profit at an agreed ratio). In Shirkah generally, all partners' rights and liabilities should be SIMILAR (not necessarily equal), grounded in the principle that one who shares profit must also bear risk (based on the Prophet's saying that earnings are concomitant to risks).","examExplanation":"Shirkah = broad partnership concept (Qur'an/Sunnah/Ijma'a basis); 'profit' excludes marriage/divorce/subsistence/penalty income. Shirkatulmilk (ownership partnership): optional (joint purchase/gift/bequest) or compulsive (commingled capital, joint inheritance); NO agency between partners - each is a legal 'stranger' to the other's share; revenue always follows ownership proportion. Shirkatul'aqd (contract partnership): created via offer/acceptance; partners ARE mutual agents, cannot sell shares without consent, cannot guarantee others' capital/profit; subdivides into Shirkatulamwal (capital), Shirkatula'mal (labour/services), Shirkatulwujooh (creditworthiness).","keyPoints":["Shirkah's 'profit' basis (per Islamic law) is limited to income from purchase, sale, hire, or wages - excluding marriage/divorce, spousal/child subsistence, or penalty/fine income.","The core distinction between the two categories: in Shirkatulmilk (ownership partnership), partners are NOT agents of each other (each is a legal 'stranger' to the other's share); in Shirkatul'aqd (contract partnership), partners ARE mutual agents.","Shirkatulmilk can be optional (joint purchase, gift, or bequest acceptance) or compulsive (commingled/undistinguishable capital, or joint inheritance) - and revenue distribution always follows the exact ownership proportion.","Shirkatul'aqd subdivides by subject matter into Shirkatulamwal (capital-based), Shirkatula'mal (labour/service-based), and Shirkatulwujooh (creditworthiness-based).","The foundational principle across all Shirkah forms: a person who shares in profits must also bear the corresponding risk, grounded in the Prophet's (pbuh) saying that earnings are concomitant to risks."],"definitions":[{"term":"Shirkatulmilk","definition":"Partnership by ownership - two or more people jointly own something, either by choice (optional) or by circumstance (compulsive, e.g. commingled capital or joint inheritance), without any agency relationship between them."},{"term":"Shirkatul'aqd","definition":"Partnership by contract - an agreement (via offer and acceptance) between two or more persons to combine assets, labour, or liabilities for the purpose of making profit, in which partners act as each other's agents."},{"term":"Shirkatulamwal","definition":"A capital-based contractual partnership where all partners invest capital into a commercial enterprise, coming under collective ownership per their capital ratio."},{"term":"Shirkatula'mal","definition":"A labour/service-based contractual partnership where partners jointly render services to customers and share the resulting fee per an agreed ratio."},{"term":"Shirkatulwujooh","definition":"A creditworthiness-based contractual partnership where partners jointly purchase goods on credit using their personal credibility and sell them, sharing the resulting profit per an agreed ratio."}],"conditions":["In Shirkatulmilk, a partner cannot use even his own share of jointly owned property if doing so would harm the other partner's interest.","In Shirkatul'aqd, no partner can sell his share without the other partners' consent, nor guarantee the capital or profit of the other partners."],"principles":["A person who shares in profits must also bear the corresponding risk - the foundational principle underlying all Shirkah forms."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume all forms of 'partnership' involve mutual agency. This is only true for Shirkatul'aqd (contract-based); in Shirkatulmilk (ownership-based), partners are explicitly NOT agents of each other."],"distinctions":["Shirkatulmilk (no agency between co-owners, not inherently profit-motivated) vs Shirkatul'aqd (partners are mutual agents, formed specifically for profit-sharing business)."],"relatedConcepts":["Musharakah","Mudarabah","Wakalah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2-12.2.2","pages":[308,309,310]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Maliki and some Hanbali jurists","b":"Hanafi jurists","text":"Some Maliki and Hanbali jurists treat Mudarabah as a form of Shirkah. The Hanafis do not: in Shirkah the partners are liable for losses from the start, while the Mudarib is not a partner, and is not liable for losses, until profit arises. Before then he is an agent (p. 312)."}]},{"id":"t12.2.1","section":"12.2.1","title":"Partnership in Ownership (Shirkatulmilk)","pages":[309,309],"tier":"core","concepts":["shirkatulmilk","shirkah"],"intuition":"Two brothers inherit a house. Are they business partners?","simpleExplanation":"Shirkatulmilk is joint ownership of one thing — by choice (joint purchase, gift or bequest accepted together) or compulsorily (inheritance, or goods mixed beyond separation). It is not basically for sharing profit. Co-owners are not agents of each other; a co-owner may sell his own share (except in mixed property), and if one uses the joint property the other may demand rent for his share. Revenue is always shared by ownership proportion.","academicExplanation":["Optional Shirkatulmilk: two persons jointly purchase a specific article, or accept it jointly as a gift or bequest. Compulsive: property becomes united without their act and cannot be distinguished, or is jointly inherited.","Unlike other forms, co-owners are not agents of one another: in the other's absence a partner has no right to use the absent partner's property, nor is he responsible for liabilities of that share; he cannot use even his own share to the detriment of the other.","A co-owner may sell his share to the other partner, or to others without consent — except in association or mixture of property, where the partner's permission is needed.","If one partner uses the joint property, the other may demand rental for his part — the basis for leasing a share in Diminishing Musharakah. Revenue is distributed strictly by ownership proportion."],"examExplanation":"Shirkatulmilk: joint ownership (optional — purchase/gift/bequest; compulsive — inheritance/mixing). Not for profit; co-owners not agents; may sell own share; can charge rent to the co-owner using the property; revenue by ownership ratio.","keyPoints":["Rent may be charged to the co-owner who uses the property.","Revenue always follows ownership proportion."],"definitions":[{"term":"Shirkatulmilk","definition":"Partnership in ownership — joint ownership of property arising by choice or compulsion, not basically for profit sharing."}],"examples":[{"title":"Co-owners are not agents","kind":"textbook","text":"Two brothers inherit a house. It is Shirkatulmilk: neither is the other’s agent. If one lives in the whole house, he owes the other rent for using his share (p. 309)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t12.8","t12.9.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Shirkatulmilk, are the co-owners agents of each other?","options":["Yes, always","No — each is a stranger in respect of the other's share","Only if they are relatives","Only in inheritance cases"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 309."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t12.2.2","section":"12.2.2","title":"Partnership by Contract (Shirkatul‘aqd) and its Kinds","pages":[309,312],"tier":"core","concepts":["shirkatulaqd","shirkah-al-inan","shirkah"],"intuition":"What changes when partners sign a contract to do business together?","simpleExplanation":"Shirkatul‘aqd is created by offer and acceptance to combine assets, labour or liabilities for profit (AAOIFI). Partners are agents of each other; a partner cannot sell his share without consent and cannot guarantee another's capital or profit. Kinds: Shirkatulamwal (capital), Shirkatula‘mal (labour/crafts), Shirkatulwujooh (creditworthiness), Mufawadah (universal, equal) and ‘Inan (general). Shirkah al ‘Inan and Mudarabah are the most important.","academicExplanation":["Basic principle: whoever shares in profit must bear risk — based on the Hadith that earnings are concomitant with risk. Partners' rights and liabilities must be similar, though not necessarily equal.","Shirkah al-Mufawadah: partners equal in property, privileges and religious persuasion; supported only by Hanafis; cumbersome and factually nonexistent.","Shirkah al ‘Inan: any two persons become partners in a particular business or in all commerce; each is agent, not surety, of the other; consensus of all jurists; nearest to modern business partnership.","Shirkatula‘mal (San‘ai, Taqabbul, Abdan): partnership in labour or crafts — e.g. doctors, teachers, transporters, farmers — sharing earnings.","Shirkatul Wujooh: joint credit purchase and sale on joint account with agreed liability ratios. Shafi‘i: unlawful; Maliki: invalid for chance unless obligations are clarified first; Hanafi and Hanbali: valid. Loss follows liability undertaken; if liability was not stipulated, each is liable for his own credit and the working partner gets wages, not profit.","Conditions of ‘Inan: capital in any proportion; management rights may be disproportionate to capital; profit may be disproportionate by agreement; loss in proportion to capital; each partner an agent; no partner indemnifies another's acts. Partners may sell, trade, deposit, pledge and give capital on Mudarabah; a loan taken for the business binds all partners jointly.","Shafi‘i, Jafari and Zahiri jurists (e.g. Ibn Hazm) accept only ‘Inan and Mudarabah; Hanafi and Maliki jurists accept a broader range."],"examExplanation":"Shirkatul‘aqd = contract (offer/acceptance), partners mutual agents, no guarantee of capital/profit. Kinds: Amwal, A‘mal, Wujooh, Mufawadah (Hanafi only), ‘Inan (consensus). ‘Inan: profit by agreement, loss by capital.","keyPoints":["‘Inan: agent (Wakil) but not surety (Kafil).","Wujooh: views differ; loss by liability undertaken.","Mufawadah: equality in everything; practically nonexistent."],"definitions":[{"term":"Shirkatul‘aqd","definition":"Partnership by contract — an agreement to combine assets, labour or liabilities for profit (AAOIFI)."},{"term":"Shirkah al ‘Inan","definition":"General partnership in which each partner is the agent (not surety) of the others; profit by agreement, loss by capital."},{"term":"Shirkatula‘mal","definition":"Partnership in labour or crafts (also San‘ai, Taqabbul, Abdan) sharing earnings from services."},{"term":"Shirkatul Wujooh","definition":"Partnership in creditworthiness: joint purchase on credit and sale on joint account with agreed liability ratios."},{"term":"Shirkah al-Mufawadah","definition":"Universal partnership requiring equality in property, privileges and religious persuasion; supported only by Hanafi jurists."}],"examples":[{"title":"Partnership in labour","kind":"textbook","text":"Two doctors agree to run a clinic together and share their fees in an agreed ratio, without pooling capital. This is Shirkatula‘mal (partnership in work), also called Shirkah al Abdan (p. 310)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t12.3","t12.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Shirkah al ‘Inan, a partner is:","options":["Agent and surety of the others","Agent but not surety (Wakil, not Kafil)","Neither agent nor surety","Surety only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 310 and p. 315."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.2.2","sectionTitle":"Partnership by Contract (continued): Sub-types","title":"Sub-types of Contractual Partnership: Mufawadah, 'Inan, A'mal, Wujooh, and Mudarabah","covers":["t12.2.2"],"overview":"Surveys the classical sub-types of Shirkatul'aqd, with special focus on Shirkah al 'Inan (the most practically relevant, universally accepted form) and its six conditions.","simpleExplanation":"There are several named types of contract partnership. 'Universal Partnership' (Mufawadah) requires totally equal partners in everything - it's so demanding almost nobody actually uses it, and only Hanafi jurists even recognize it. 'General Partnership' (Shirkah al 'Inan) is the practical, everyday kind - unequal contributions are fine, and it's accepted by ALL schools; this is basically what modern business partnerships look like. There's also partnership in labor/skills (two professionals teaming up) and partnership in creditworthiness (buying on credit using combined reputation, then splitting the profit) - though scholars disagree on whether the creditworthiness type is even valid.","academicExplanation":"Contractual partnership subdivides by subject matter: Shirkah-al-Mufawadah (Universal Partnership) - per Hanafi jurists, requires two persons EQUAL in property, privileges, and religious persuasion, sharing everything equally; so cumbersome it is 'factually nonexistent' in practice, advocated ONLY by Hanafi jurists (Shafi'e, Ahmad ibn Hanbal, Malik, and Jafari jurists reject it). Shirkah al 'Inan (General Partnership) - involves collective capital where partners become agents (not sureties) of each other; enjoys UNANIMOUS consensus among all schools, is the most important/practically relevant form, and is closest to the modern business partnership concept - the chapter focuses primarily on this form. Its conditions: (1) capital can be invested in any proportion; (2) power of appropriation/participation may be disproportionate to capital invested; (3) profit may be divided unequally, disproportionate to capital, per the partners' agreement; (4) loss must be shared strictly proportionate to capital invested; (5) each partner is an agent to the others; (6) no partner is responsible for indemnifying the acts/omissions of other partners (though views differ on this last point). Shirkatula'mal (Sana'i', partnership in labour/crafts, also called Shirkah Taqabbul or Shirka al Abdan) - two persons partner to work jointly and share earnings; classical examples include partnerships between doctors, teachers, miners, transport owners, and farmers. Shirkatul Wujooh (partnership in creditworthiness) - two persons jointly purchase goods on personal credit (without immediate payment) and sell on joint account, undertaking obligations/liability per agreed percentages. Imam Shafi'e deems it unlawful; Maliki jurists view it as containing an element of random chance (invalid) UNLESS the obligation extent is clarified before the contract (e.g. joint credit purchase of a SPECIFIC commodity, sold at profit); Hanafi and Hanbali jurists validate it. If liability extent isn't stipulated upfront, each partner is individually responsible for credit he personally took, and the working partner gets only wages (not a profit share). Of all these, Shirkah al 'Inan and Mudarabah are the most popular, enjoying full Ijma'a; Shafi'e, Jafari, and Zahirite (Ibn Hazm) jurists recognize ONLY these two as lawful joint-venture forms, while Hanafi and Maliki jurists accept a broader range. Regarding rights/liabilities under Shirkah al 'Inan: partners may sell partnership assets, trade with them, deposit or pledge them, or hand them to a third party on a Mudarabah basis; if any partner takes a loan for the joint business, ALL partners become jointly liable to repay it. A Musharakah (or Mudarabah) contract can be for a specific project until completion, or a redeemable investment (Diminishing Musharakah) by a financial institution; if it runs the full life of an ongoing business with no midway termination, it is a 'continuous Musharakah.' On whether Mudarabah IS a form of Shirkah: some Maliki/Hanbali jurists treat it as such; Hanafi jurists treat it as distinct, reasoning that Shirkah partners become immediately liable to losses once the business starts or capital is combined, while in Mudarabah, the working party is NOT liable for losses until profits actually arise - before profit realization, the working party's position is more like an agent, even though the Mudarabah contract is already effective.","examExplanation":"Sub-types of Shirkatul'aqd: Mufawadah (Universal Partnership - totally equal partners, Hanafi-only, practically nonexistent); Shirkah al 'Inan (General Partnership - unequal capital OK, universal Ijma'a, most practically relevant, 6 conditions); Shirkatula'mal (labour/craft partnership); Shirkatul Wujooh (creditworthiness partnership - disputed: Shafi'e rejects, Maliki conditionally accepts if obligation extent is pre-clarified, Hanafi/Hanbali validate). Shirkah al 'Inan's 6 conditions: unequal capital OK; disproportionate management power OK; disproportionate profit-sharing OK (per agreement); LOSS must be strictly proportionate to capital; mutual agency; no indemnification duty for others' acts (disputed). Hanafi view on Mudarabah-vs-Shirkah: Mudarib is only liable once profit arises (before that, more like an agent), unlike a true Shirkah partner who is immediately liable upon capital combination.","keyPoints":["Shirkah-al-Mufawadah (Universal Partnership) requires totally equal partners in property, privileges, and religion - so demanding it is practically nonexistent, and is recognized only by Hanafi jurists (rejected by Shafi'e, Hanbali, Maliki, Jafari schools).","Shirkah al 'Inan (General Partnership) enjoys unanimous consensus among ALL schools, allows disproportionate capital/management/profit-sharing, and is the form most relevant to modern business partnerships and the chapter's primary focus.","Shirkah al 'Inan's six core conditions: capital in any proportion, disproportionate management power allowed, disproportionate profit-sharing allowed (per agreement), loss STRICTLY proportionate to capital, mutual agency, and (disputed) no indemnification duty for other partners' acts.","Shirkatul Wujooh (creditworthiness partnership) is disputed: Imam Shafi'e deems it unlawful; Maliki jurists view it as containing an element of chance (invalid) unless obligation shares are clarified upfront; Hanafi and Hanbali jurists validate it.","Only Shirkah al 'Inan and Mudarabah enjoy full Ijma'a (unanimous consensus) - Shafi'e, Jafari, and Zahirite jurists recognize ONLY these two as lawful joint-venture forms.","Hanafi jurists treat Mudarabah as distinct from Shirkah because the working party (Mudarib) is not liable for losses until profit actually arises - before that point, he functions more like an agent than a full partner."],"definitions":[{"term":"Shirkah-al-Mufawadah","definition":"'Universal Partnership' per Hanafi jurists - requiring two totally equal partners in property, privileges, and religious persuasion, sharing everything equally; so demanding it is practically nonexistent and recognized only by the Hanafi school."},{"term":"Shirkah al 'Inan","definition":"'General Partnership' - the most important and universally accepted form of contractual partnership, allowing disproportionate capital contributions, management power, and profit-sharing among partners, with loss strictly proportionate to capital invested."}],"conditions":["In Shirkah al 'Inan, loss must be shared strictly in proportion to each partner's capital investment, though profit-sharing and management power may be disproportionate by agreement.","In Shirkatul Wujooh, per the Maliki view, the extent of each partner's obligation must be clarified before the contract is effected for it to be valid."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think all four sub-types of Shirkatul'aqd are equally accepted. Only Shirkah al 'Inan and Mudarabah enjoy universal Ijma'a; Mufawadah is essentially unused (Hanafi-only, impractical), and Shirkatul Wujooh is actively disputed among schools."],"distinctions":["Shirkah al 'Inan (unequal contributions fine, loss strictly proportionate to capital, universal acceptance) vs Shirkah-al-Mufawadah (totally equal partners required, Hanafi-only, practically unused)."],"relatedConcepts":["Mudarabah","Wakalah","Diminishing Musharakah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","pages":[310,311,312]}}],"table":{"caption":"Kinds of contractual partnership (Section 12.2.2)","head":["Kind","Subject matter","Juristic status (as described)"],"rows":[["Shirkatulamwal / ‘Inan","Capital of partners","Consensus of all jurists"],["Shirkatula‘mal","Labour, skill, services","Recognised (examples: doctors, teachers)"],["Shirkatul Wujooh","Creditworthiness","Valid for Hanafi/Hanbali; Maliki conditional; Shafi‘i unlawful"],["Mufawadah","Everything equally","Hanafi only; practically nonexistent"],["Mudarabah","Capital of one, labour of other","Consensus of all jurists"]]}},{"id":"t12.3","section":"12.3","title":"Basic Rules of Musharakah","pages":[312,312],"tier":"core","concepts":["musharakah"],"intuition":"Which rules turn a joint venture into a valid Musharakah?","simpleExplanation":"The chapter's rules concern a general partnership with joint capital (Shirkatulamwal-cum-‘Inan). All the conditions of any valid contract — free consent without deception, misrepresentation or duress — apply, plus specific rules on partners, capital, management, profit and loss, guarantees and termination.","academicExplanation":["Rules discussed relate mainly to Shirkatulamwal-cum-Shirkah al ‘Inan. General contract conditions (free consent, no deception, misrepresentation or duress) must be met, together with the specific conditions in Sections 12.3.1–12.3.6.","Section 12.3 sets out the rules of a general partnership run with the partners’ joint capital (Shirkatulamwal combined with Shirkah al ‘Inan), the form most relevant to banks. Every condition of a valid contract applies first: free consent, without deception, misrepresentation or duress. On top of these come rules specific to Shirkah: who may be a partner (12.3.1), what may form the capital (12.3.2), how partners relate to one another and manage the business (12.3.3), how profit and loss are shared (12.3.4), what can and cannot be guaranteed (12.3.5), and how the partnership ends (12.3.6). One theme runs through all of them. A partner’s capital is at risk and cannot be protected by a fixed return or a guarantee from a co-partner (pp. 312–320)."],"examExplanation":"Musharakah rules: general valid-contract conditions + rules on partners, capital, management, P&L, guarantees, termination.","keyPoints":["Six rule areas: partners, capital, management, profit/loss, guarantees, termination.","General contract conditions (free consent, no deception, misrepresentation or duress) apply to Shirkah before its special rules (p. 312)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Checking a Musharakah proposal","kind":"practice","text":"Before approving, test: are both partners competent to contract; is the capital money (or goods valued in money), not a debt; is profit a percentage of actual profit; will loss follow capital; is no partner’s capital guaranteed by the other? (pp. 312–320)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t12.3.1","t12.3.2","t12.3.3","t12.3.4","t12.3.5","t12.3.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which type of partnership do the chapter's basic Musharakah rules mainly address?","options":["Shirkatulmilk","Shirkatulamwal-cum-Shirkah al ‘Inan","Mufawadah","Wujooh"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 312."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t12.3.1","section":"12.3.1","title":"Conditions with Respect to Partners","pages":[312,313],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["musharakah"],"intuition":"Can a bank be partners with a non-Muslim or a conventional bank?","simpleExplanation":"Partners may be individuals or legal persons. Individuals must be free and of sound mind; minors (unless permitted by a guardian), the insane, the intoxicated and those inhibited by a court (e.g. insolvency) cannot validly be partners because partnership involves mutual agency. Musharakah with non-Muslims and conventional banks is allowed for Shari’ah-acceptable operations with assurances of compliance.","academicExplanation":["Since partnership comprehends mutual agency, anyone restricted from exercising it cannot act as a true partner. Jafari scholars list five inhibitions: minority, stupidity, insanity, fatal disease and insolvency.","Indebtedness: Abu Hanifa did not allow inhibition of a debtor (it restrains him from improving his condition), though he may be imprisoned for non-payment; later Hanafi law (Abu Yusuf, Muhammad) allows inhibition if creditors demand. Inhibition does not apply to modern businesses that run on credit with sufficient resources.","Musharakah with non-Muslims and interest-based banks is allowed for Shari’ah-acceptable operations, with assurances; in a syndicate, the Islamic bank's portion must rest on valid contracts such as Ijarah.","Interest is prohibited for all in an Islamic state; alcohol and pork are prohibited for Muslims only — a partnership in such trade is void if any partner is Muslim."],"examExplanation":"Partners: free, sane, not inhibited (minority, insanity, insolvency, etc.). Legal persons allowed. Musharakah with non-Muslims/conventional banks allowed for Halal operations with compliance assurances.","keyPoints":["Mutual agency requires full legal capacity.","Syndicate: the Islamic bank's portion must use valid contracts.","Partners must be free and of sound mind. Because a partnership involves mutual agency, anyone barred from making a contract of sale (e.g. through insolvency or court inhibition) cannot be a true partner (p. 312).","The Jafari scholars list five disqualifications from sale: minority, stupidity, insanity, fatal disease and insolvency (pp. 312–313).","Goods prohibited for Muslims only (e.g. liquor): a non-Muslim may trade in them, but a partnership in such trade is void if any partner is a Muslim (p. 313)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Minor as partner","kind":"textbook","text":"A minor cannot normally be a partner, but can join a partnership if his guardian allows it (Majallah, Art. 1335; p. 312)."},{"title":"Syndicate with conventional banks","kind":"textbook","text":"Where Islamic and conventional banks jointly finance a large project, the Islamic bank’s portfolio must consist of valid contracts such as Ijarah (p. 313)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A Muslim may not enter into Musharakah with non-Muslims or with interest-based banks.","right":"It is allowed for Shari’ah-acceptable operations, provided assurances are obtained that Shari’ah rules will be observed (p. 313)."},{"wrong":"Any businessman who runs his business on credit is “indebted” and cannot be a partner.","right":"Inhibition concerns near-insolvency, where liabilities exceed assets. It does not apply to modern businessmen who trade on credit but have the resources to meet their liabilities (p. 313)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why can an insolvent person under court inhibition not be a partner?","options":["He has no capital","Partnership involves mutual agency, which he cannot exercise over property","Partners must be wealthy","He cannot sign contracts in any case"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 312."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.3.1","sectionTitle":"Basic Rules of Musharakah / Conditions with Respect to Partners","title":"Conditions with Respect to Partners in Musharakah","covers":["t12.3.1"],"overview":"Covers who can validly be a Musharakah partner (competence, insolvency, minority), and the specific permission for Islamic banks to partner with non-Muslims and even conventional banks under safeguards.","simpleExplanation":"To be a valid Musharakah partner, you generally need to be a free, sane adult - minors and people declared legally insolvent by a court can't fully enter partnerships (though a minor can with a guardian's permission). Interestingly, Islamic banks CAN form partnerships with non-Muslims and even conventional (interest-based) banks, as long as proper safeguards ensure Islamic principles are actually followed in the joint venture - though the underlying business itself must be lawful (nothing like alcohol, gambling, or pork).","academicExplanation":"'Persons' in Musharakah definitions covers both individuals and legal/corporate bodies. For individuals, unanimous agreement requires them to be free and of sound mind. Insolvency and imprisonment disqualify one from a sale contract, and since partnership inherently involves mutual agency, anyone handicapped in exercising this right cannot be a true partner (a resulting contract is deemed ineffective) - so minors and the insane are incompetent, though a minor CAN partner if permitted by his guardian. Imam Shafi'e extends incompetence to anyone who temporarily loses decision-making power (e.g. intoxication). A person under court-ordered inhibition (due to insolvency or other reasons) also cannot partner, since his partner could be prevented from using the inhibited partner's property. Jafari scholars cite five sale-incompetence causes (applicable to Shirkah too): minority, stupidity, insanity, fatal disease, and insolvency - other jurists don't disagree. There is some dispute over indebtedness specifically: some jurists don't impose inhibition if debt recovery remains possible; Imam Abu Hanifa holds an indebted person cannot be inhibited (as this would prevent him improving his economic condition), though he can be imprisoned for nonpayment; later Hanafi law (per Abu Yusuf and Muhammad) allows debtor inhibition if creditors demand it. 'Indebtedness' here means total liabilities exceeding total assets with inability to pay (near-insolvency for a businessman) - inhibition does NOT apply to modern businessmen who run their entire business on credit while commanding sufficient resources to cover liabilities. Crucially, Musharakah CAN be concluded with non-Muslims AND even interest-based (conventional) banks, to carry out Shari'ah-acceptable operations - provided arrangements secure all necessary assurances/guarantees that Shari'ah rules will be observed during the partnership's operation. This excludes partnerships in businesses unlawful in Islam (swine flesh, liquor, pornography, gambling). A key distinction: goods/activities absolutely prohibited for EVERYONE (e.g. interest, in an Islamic state) vs those prohibited only for Muslims specifically (e.g. alcohol, pork) - a non-Muslim citizen of an Islamic state CAN trade in the latter category, but partnerships in such trade become VOID if any party involved is Muslim. Example: in a syndicate financing a large project comprising both Islamic and conventional banks, the Islamic bank's portfolio must comprise valid Shari'ah contracts (e.g. Ijarah) to ensure its own compliance, even while partnering alongside conventional banks in the broader syndicate.","examExplanation":"Partner competence: must be free, of sound mind; minors/insane are incompetent (minor can partner with guardian's permission); court-inhibited insolvent persons cannot partner. Indebtedness alone (without formal court inhibition) does NOT disqualify - modern credit-run businesses with sufficient resources are not treated as 'insolvent' for this purpose. Islamic banks CAN partner with non-Muslims AND conventional banks for Shari'ah-acceptable operations, with proper safeguards; the underlying business must be lawful (no swine/liquor/gambling/pornography). Key distinction: universally-prohibited items (e.g. interest, prohibited for all in an Islamic state) vs Muslim-only-prohibited items (e.g. alcohol/pork - non-Muslims can lawfully trade these, but a partnership in such trade becomes VOID if a Muslim is a party).","keyPoints":["A valid Musharakah partner must generally be free and of sound mind; minors and the insane are incompetent (though a minor CAN partner with his guardian's permission), and a court-inhibited insolvent person also cannot partner.","Mere indebtedness (without formal court-ordered inhibition) does not disqualify a partner - modern credit-run businesses with sufficient resources to cover liabilities are not treated as disqualifying 'insolvency'.","Musharakah can be concluded with non-Muslims and even interest-based conventional banks for Shari'ah-acceptable operations, provided proper assurances/guarantees ensure Shari'ah compliance in the partnership's operation.","A key distinction applies to prohibited goods/activities: those absolutely prohibited for everyone (e.g. interest, in an Islamic state) vs those prohibited only for Muslims (e.g. alcohol, pork) - non-Muslims can lawfully trade the latter, but partnerships in such trade become void if a Muslim party is involved."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Musharakah partners must be free and of sound mind; minors are incompetent unless permitted by their guardian, and court-inhibited insolvent persons cannot partner.","A partnership in trade of Muslim-only-prohibited goods (e.g. alcohol) becomes void if any party to the partnership is Muslim."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Islamic-Conventional Bank Syndicate","kind":"textbook","text":"If a syndicate of banks comprising both Islamic and conventional banks finances a huge project or corporate firm, the Islamic bank's portfolio must comprise valid Shari'ah-compliant contracts (e.g. Ijarah) to maintain its own compliance, even while partnering alongside conventional banks in the broader syndicate arrangement."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume Islamic banks cannot partner with non-Muslims or conventional banks at all. The chapter clarifies this IS permitted for Shari'ah-acceptable operations, subject to proper Shari'ah-compliance safeguards in the arrangement."],"distinctions":["Goods/activities absolutely prohibited for everyone (e.g. interest) vs those prohibited only for Muslims (e.g. alcohol, pork) - a partnership in the latter is void only if a Muslim party is involved."],"relatedConcepts":["Musharakah","Wakalah","Shari'ah Compliance"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","pages":[312,313]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Abu Hanifa","b":"Abu Yusuf and Muhammad (later Hanafi law)","text":"Abu Hanifa: an indebted person cannot be inhibited, since that would stop him improving his condition, though he may be imprisoned for non-payment. Abu Yusuf and Muhammad: the debtor may be inhibited if his creditors demand it (p. 313)."}]},{"id":"t12.3.2","section":"12.3.2","title":"Rules Relating to Musharakah Capital","pages":[313,314],"tier":"core","concepts":["musharakah"],"intuition":"Can a partner contribute a shop instead of cash?","simpleExplanation":"Majority: capital should be liquid (money) of known value; Hanafis allow it to be settled before business starts. It cannot be a debt or a nonexistent commodity. Contemporary jurists agree goods may be contributed at their market value. Capital is commingled into joint ownership, so appreciation belongs to all partners pro rata. Commingling may be actual or constructive (by valuation).","academicExplanation":["Maliki, Hanbali and Shafi‘i jurists require the value of capital to be known without ambiguity; Hanafis say it can be agreed before commencement. Al-Sarakhsi and Al-Kasani note that the form of capital follows the ‘Urf of the place; contemporary jurists are unanimous that goods be valued in money. Debt cannot become capital until received.","Usmani concludes that share capital may be cash or commodities, with the market value of commodities determining the partner's share.","Commingling replaces individual with collective ownership: a partner cannot claim that the shop he contributed has appreciated for him alone.","A person may join a running business with fixed assets by investing cash or kind; firms may merge. Valuation: cash/receivables at face value; currencies at the contract-date rate; fixed assets at agreed value; in running Musharakah, the average utilised amount is treated as capital (as in deposit pools)."],"examExplanation":"Capital: preferably money, known value; goods at market value; not a debt/nonexistent item; commingled (actual or constructive) → appreciation shared pro rata. Running business: receivables at face value, fixed assets at agreed value.","keyPoints":["Debt is not capital until received.","Appreciation of contributed assets belongs to all partners.","Al-Sarakhsi and al-Kasani: the form of capital follows local custom (‘Urf), cash or goods. Contemporary jurists agree that goods must be valued in money (pp. 313–314).","Usmani’s conclusion: capital may be cash or commodities, and the commodities’ market value fixes the partner’s share (p. 314)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Shop contributed as capital","kind":"textbook","text":"Once capital is commingled, a partner who contributed a shop cannot claim its later price rise for himself alone. Appreciation belongs to all partners in the ratio of their capital (p. 314)."},{"title":"Joining a running business","kind":"textbook","text":"When a partner joins a running business, cash and receivables are taken at face value and foreign currencies at the rate on the contract date. Fixed assets are taken at an agreed value (p. 314)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Commingling means capital must be in cash and paid in at the moment of contract.","right":"The merger may be actual or constructive, based on an agreed valuation such as market value. Goods are counted at their money value at the time of contract (p. 314)."},{"wrong":"A debt owed to a partner can be counted as his capital contribution.","right":"A debt cannot become part of partnership capital until it is received (p. 314)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.3.4","t12.4.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A partner contributes machinery to a Musharakah. How is his capital share determined?","options":["By its original purchase cost","By its market value at the time of the contract","It cannot be contributed","By the other partners' cash"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 314 (Usmani's conclusion)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.3.2","sectionTitle":"Rules Relating to Musharakah Capital","title":"Rules Relating to Musharakah Capital","covers":["t12.3.2"],"overview":"Explains what form Musharakah capital should take, why debt cannot count as capital, and how capital commingling works in principle even when it isn't physically pooled.","simpleExplanation":"Musharakah capital should ideally be money (or its clear value if it's goods), and everyone agrees an unpaid debt can't count as capital until it's actually collected. The idea of 'commingling' capital doesn't mean everyone's money has to be physically dumped into one pile - it can be done on paper (constructively), based on an agreed valuation, so each partner's ownership share in the joint venture is clear.","academicExplanation":"The majority of jurists (particularly Maliki, Hanbali, Shafi'e) hold that partner capital should be liquid assets (money/currency) with unambiguously known value; Hanafis, however, don't require the capital amount to be known at CONTRACT time - it can be agreed before business commencement. Capital must not be a debt or a nonexistent commodity. Al-Sarakhsi notes capital forms vary by local 'Urf (custom); Al-Kasani says the matter follows local investment practice (currency or goods). Contemporary jurists are unanimous that goods-based capital's VALUE must be assessed in monetary terms; debt cannot become partnership capital until actually RECEIVED. In limited companies, capital takes the form of equal 'shares' units, purchasable disproportionately by intended partners. Objects not sellable/usable under Islamic law cannot form partnership capital. Shirkah rules broadly require capital 'merger and commingling' - meaning individual ownership is replaced by collective joint-venture ownership, with any Musharakah asset value appreciation reflecting ALL partners' rights per their capital ratio (a partner cannot claim sole entitlement to appreciation of a specific asset, e.g. a shop, he personally contributed). Commingling does NOT require indistinguishable cash/identical goods or actual transfer at contract time - the merger can be ACTUAL or CONSTRUCTIVE (the latter based on agreed-standard valuation, e.g. market value); for goods, partner shares are calculated by their money value at contract time. Usmani's synthesis: Musharakah share capital can be contributed in cash OR commodities, with commodities' market value determining the partner's capital share. Other modern capital-related aspects: a person can become a partner of a running business (with fixed assets) by investing cash/kind capital; merger of various partnership firms is also possible. This implies all partners' capital must be quantified/specified - for a running business, cash/receivables are valued at face value, currency conversion uses the Musharakah contract execution date's rate, fixed assets use agreed valuation, and for running Musharakah (e.g. Islamic bank deposit management via Shirkah), the AVERAGE utilized amount is treated as Musharakah capital.","examExplanation":"Musharakah capital: majority view (Maliki, Hanbali, Shafi'e) requires liquid/known-value capital; Hanafi view allows the exact amount to be agreed BEFORE business commencement (not necessarily at contract signing). Capital cannot be a debt (until actually received) or a nonexistent commodity. Commingling = individual ownership replaced by COLLECTIVE ownership (so asset appreciation benefits all partners per capital ratio, not whoever contributed that specific asset) - can be ACTUAL (physical transfer) or CONSTRUCTIVE (agreed valuation, e.g. market value), with goods-based capital always valued in monetary terms. For running Musharakah (e.g. bank deposit pools), the AVERAGE utilized amount serves as Musharakah capital.","keyPoints":["The majority view (Maliki, Hanbali, Shafi'e) requires Musharakah capital to be liquid assets with unambiguously known value at contract time; the Hanafi view allows the exact capital amount to be agreed before business commencement, rather than strictly at contract signing.","Debt cannot become partnership capital until it is actually received; a nonexistent commodity also cannot serve as capital.","'Commingling' of capital does not require physical, indistinguishable pooling - the merger can be constructive, based on an agreed valuation standard (e.g. market value), with goods-based capital always assessed in monetary terms.","Because commingled capital means COLLECTIVE ownership, any appreciation in a Musharakah asset's value benefits ALL partners per their capital ratio - a partner cannot claim sole entitlement to the appreciation of a specific asset he personally contributed.","For a running Musharakah (e.g. an Islamic bank's deposit-based Shirkah pool), the AVERAGE utilized amount over the period is treated as the Musharakah capital for profit-sharing purposes."],"definitions":[{"term":"Commingling","definition":"The Shirkah requirement that partner capital be merged such that individual ownership is replaced by collective joint-venture ownership; the merger may be actual (physical) or constructive (based on an agreed valuation standard)."}],"conditions":["Musharakah capital must not be a debt (until actually received) or a nonexistent commodity.","Goods-based capital contributions must have their market value assessed in monetary terms to determine each partner's capital share."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think 'commingling' requires literally pooling identical cash or goods together at contract signing. The chapter clarifies this can be done constructively, via agreed valuation, without requiring physical transfer or indistinguishable pooling at that exact moment."],"distinctions":["Actual commingling (physical merger of capital) vs constructive commingling (based on agreed valuation, e.g. market value) - both are valid ways to satisfy the capital-merger requirement."],"relatedConcepts":["Musha'a","Diminishing Musharakah","Mudarabah Capital"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.2","pages":[313,314]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Maliki, Hanbali and Shafi‘i jurists","b":"Hanafi jurists","text":"Majority: the amount of capital must be known without ambiguity at the time of contract. Hanafis: it need not be known at contract, provided it is agreed before business begins (p. 313)."}]},{"id":"t12.3.3","section":"12.3.3","title":"Mutual Relationship Among Partners and Management Rules","pages":[314,316],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["musharakah"],"intuition":"Must every partner work — and can a working partner earn more?","simpleExplanation":"Partners are agents of one another and all have a right to manage (sales, credit, rentals, pledges, customary acts), but may not give grants or loans without all partners' consent. Management may be restricted to some partners or given to an outside manager on salary (an expense of the Shirkah), possibly with a performance bonus. A partner providing extra services may receive a bigger profit share or be employed separately. In ‘Inan a partner is Wakil but not Kafil.","academicExplanation":["On execution of Musharakah, agency is presumed; possession by one partner is constructive possession of all; a purchase made for oneself only is one's own.","Rights of all partners include cash or credit sales, rejecting defective goods, renting assets, cancelling contracts, seeking credit, collecting receivables, paying or depositing, pledging and customary acts; minor loans that do not affect operations are allowed; grants and loans need everyone's consent (AAOIFI).","An outside manager may receive fixed pay (an expense) and a bonus; if managing from the outset for a share of profit (as Mudarib), he gets only that share. A partner doing extra work (e.g. accounting) may get a larger profit share by consent, or be employed under a separate contract and dismissed without affecting the Shirkah.","Indemnification: in ‘Inan a partner is Wakil but not Kafil, so not liable to outsiders for another partner's loss; Al-Kasani says it is governed by ‘Urf.","Unless restricted, partners may invest capital in Mudarabah, appoint agents, deposit or lend property, mortgage it, travel at the Shirkah's expense, join another Musharakah, mix property with their own, accept mortgages, spend by consent and ‘Urf, and buy and sell for the business (Al-Sarakhsi's list of ten).","In companies and cooperatives, shareholders delegate powers to directors; some partners may be sleeping partners."],"examExplanation":"Partners = mutual agents; all may manage customary acts; no grants/loans without consent; management can be delegated (salary = expense; Mudarib-style manager gets only profit share); extra work → larger share by consent; ‘Inan partner = Wakil not Kafil.","keyPoints":["Sleeping partners are allowed.","Separate employment contract possible for a partner.","Partners may give minor, customary short-term loans but cannot give grants or loans without all partners’ consent (p. 315).","A partner employed under a separate contract can be dismissed without amending or ending the Shirkah (p. 315).","If a partner buys something for himself only, it is his alone, not the partnership’s (p. 314)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Wakil, not Kafil","definition":"In Shirkah al ‘Inan a partner acts as agent for the others but does not guarantee or indemnify their obligations to outsiders."}],"examples":[{"title":"Partner who keeps the accounts","kind":"textbook","text":"A partner who does the accounting, a service the others do not provide, may by mutual consent receive a larger profit share than his capital alone would earn (p. 315)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A Mudarib-type manager paid a share of profit may also draw a management salary.","right":"If management is done from the outset for a share in profit, the manager gets only that share. A fixed salary is possible only for a manager hired as an employee, whose pay is a Shirkah expense (p. 315)."},{"wrong":"In Shirkah al ‘Inan each partner must indemnify outsiders for the other partners’ losses.","right":"A partner in ‘Inan is Wakil but not Kafil (agent, not indemnifier). Al-Kasani adds that local usage may make indemnity a valid presumed condition (p. 315)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.3.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"An outside manager hired from the outset for a share of profit (as Mudarib) is entitled to:","options":["Salary plus profit share","Only the share in realised profit","A fixed fee only","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 315."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.3.3","sectionTitle":"Mutual Relationship Among Partners and Musharakah Management Rules","title":"Mutual Relationship Among Partners and Management Rules","covers":["t12.3.3"],"overview":"Explains the automatic agency relationship between Musharakah partners, what management actions they can take without special permission, and the rules for hiring an outside manager.","simpleExplanation":"When you form a Musharakah, you automatically become each other's agent - one partner's possession of the business property counts as everyone's constructive possession too. Partners can do routine business things (sell, buy, take credit, make small loans) without needing everyone's separate sign-off, but bigger things like giving away money as a gift or grant need everyone's consent. A partnership doesn't require every partner to actually work - you can hire an outside manager for a fixed fee, or even structure it as a Mudarabah where the manager only gets a profit share and no separate salary.","academicExplanation":"Shirkah business is managed by the will and equal right of all partners; upon Musharakah contract execution, agency conditions are automatically presumed to exist. One partner's actual possession of Musharakah property constitutes CONSTRUCTIVE possession for the other partners (though if a partner purchases something exclusively for himself, it belongs only to him, not the joint business). All partners have a right to participate in management for transactions including: cash/credit sales, rejecting defective goods, renting commercial assets, cancelling contracts, requesting credit facilities, collecting receivables, making payments/deposits, giving/receiving pledges, and anything customary in the joint business's interest. They can give short-term/minor loans not customarily affecting partnership operations, but CANNOT give grants or loans without ALL partners' consent. Working for the joint business is NOT mandatory for every partner - the partnership agreement can restrict management to one or specific partners (others then must not act on the partnership's behalf). Partners can also appoint an OUTSIDE manager with fixed remuneration (treated as a Shirkah expense) - this outside manager can also receive a profit-based bonus PLUS a fixed salary. However, if management is structured from the outset as a share-in-profit-only arrangement (i.e. the manager functions as a Mudarib), he is entitled ONLY to his profit share, with NO additional remuneration for management services. A partner contributing extra management/services (e.g. accounting) beyond ordinary partnership duties can, with other partners' mutual consent, receive a profit share GREATER than his pure capital-ratio entitlement. A partner can also be appointed to any Shirkah-required service via an INDEPENDENT employment contract, and can be dismissed from that service without needing to amend/terminate the underlying Shirkah contract itself. Indemnification treatment varies by Shirkah form: in Shirkah al 'Inan (most relevant here), a partner is Wakil (agent) but NOT Kafil (indemnifier/surety) of other partners - so he is not liable to indemnify an outsider for another partner's loss during agency; Al-Kasani notes indemnification is regulated by local 'Urf (custom) - if a society's common usage presumes/expects indemnification for a partnership type, that condition is considered valid. Absent any contrary condition, partners have additional rights including: investing Shirkah capital in Mudarabah; appointing any agent for Musharakah work; keeping Musharakah property as Amanah/deposit or as a loan with any person; mortgaging Shirkah property; traveling for business at Shirkah expense; becoming a partner in another Musharakah on behalf of his own Shirkah business; mixing Musharakah property with his own; accepting mortgage of outsider property on his Musharakah's behalf; spending Musharakah funds (subject to other partners' consent and 'Urf); and purchasing/selling goods necessary for business conduct. In modern Shirkah forms too, partners hold equal rights as above; partnership concerns distribute responsibilities by mutual agreement, limited companies/cooperatives delegate power to directors, and partners may agree that some serve as sleeping (non-working) partners.","examExplanation":"Musharakah partners are AUTOMATICALLY mutual agents upon contract execution; one partner's possession = constructive possession for all. Partners can independently perform routine business acts (sales, credit requests, small loans) WITHOUT needing others' separate consent, but grants/loans require ALL partners' consent. Working is NOT mandatory - management can be restricted to specific partners, or delegated to an outside manager (fixed salary + optional bonus) or structured as Mudarabah (profit-share only, no separate salary). Indemnification in Shirkah al 'Inan: partner is Wakil (agent) but NOT Kafil (surety) for others - not liable to indemnify outsiders for another partner's agency-related loss (though local 'Urf/custom can modify this).","keyPoints":["Upon Musharakah contract execution, agency conditions are automatically presumed - one partner's possession of partnership property constitutes constructive possession for the others.","Partners can perform routine business management acts (sales, rejecting defective goods, renting assets, requesting credit, collecting receivables, small loans) without needing separate consent from all partners - but grants or loans require ALL partners' consent.","Working for the joint business is not mandatory for every partner - management can be restricted to specific partners, or delegated to an outside manager (fixed salary, optionally plus a profit-based bonus) or structured as a Mudarabah arrangement (profit-share only, no separate salary).","In Shirkah al 'Inan, a partner is Wakil (agent) but NOT Kafil (indemnifier/surety) of other partners - he is not liable to indemnify an outsider for another partner's loss during agency, though local 'Urf (custom) can modify this default.","A partner contributing extra services beyond ordinary partnership duties (e.g. accounting) can, with mutual consent, receive a profit share greater than his pure capital-ratio entitlement."],"definitions":[{"term":"Wakil","definition":"An agent - the role a Shirkah al 'Inan partner holds with respect to other partners, entitling him to act on the partnership's behalf but not making him liable to indemnify outsiders for other partners' losses."},{"term":"Kafil","definition":"An indemnifier/surety - a role a Shirkah al 'Inan partner does NOT automatically hold with respect to other partners' losses (distinguishing agency from suretyship)."}],"conditions":["Partners cannot give grants or loans from Musharakah funds unless all partners consent.","If management is structured purely as a profit-share arrangement (the manager functioning as a Mudarib), he is entitled only to his profit share, with no additional remuneration for his management services."],"principles":["Indemnification obligations among Shirkah al 'Inan partners are regulated by local 'Urf (custom) - if common usage in a given trade presumes indemnification, that condition is considered valid."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume every partner must actively work in a Musharakah. The chapter clarifies this is not required - management can be restricted to specific partners, delegated to an outside manager, or even structured as a Mudarabah-style profit-share-only arrangement."],"distinctions":["An outside manager on a FIXED SALARY (plus possibly a bonus) vs a manager structured as a MUDARIB (profit-share only, no separate salary) - two distinct compensation structures for delegated Musharakah management."],"relatedConcepts":["Wakalah","Amanah","Mudarabah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.3","pages":[314,315,316]}}]},{"id":"t12.3.4","section":"12.3.4","title":"Treatment of Profit and Loss","pages":[316,320],"tier":"core","concepts":["musharakah","pls"],"intuition":"Why can profit be negotiated but loss never?","simpleExplanation":"The profit ratio must be agreed at the outset as a percentage of actual profit — never a lump sum or a percentage of capital. It may differ from the capital ratio (Abu Hanifa and Ahmad), but a declared sleeping partner cannot take more than his capital ratio (Hanafi; Hanbalis allow even that). Loss must always be shared exactly by capital ratio — the maxim attributed to Ali (ra): ‘Profit is based on agreement of the parties, but loss is always subject to the ratio of investment.’","academicExplanation":["The ratio must be agreed at execution (Ahmad, Abu Hanifa); otherwise the contract is invalid.","Malik, Shafi‘i and Zufar: profit exactly by investment. Ahmad and most Hanafis: profit may differ from investment by free consent. Abu Hanifa: may differ, but an express sleeping partner cannot exceed his capital ratio; it can be less for all jurists. Example: a 40% investor may take 50% of profit if not declared sleeping. Hanbalis allow even a sleeping partner more.","The general ruling follows Abu Hanifa and Ahmad because work, as well as capital, is a factor of profit.","Profit ratio relates to actual profit (e.g. 50:50, 60:40, 30:70 or A 30%, B 40%, C 30% of net profit); no lump sum and no rate tied to capital.","Drawings on account are adjusted at final settlement; if actual profit is less, the excess is deducted from capital. Partners may amend the profit ratio at any time, but loss remains by capital.","In Shirkatul Wujooh, profit is by agreement and loss by the liability each undertook in the credit purchases; no lump sum from profit.","Rationale: profit is legitimised by engaging in economic activity and equitable risk-sharing between capital and entrepreneur."],"examExplanation":"Profit: agreed % of actual profit at outset; may differ from capital ratio (not above capital ratio for a declared sleeping partner — Hanafi). Loss: strictly by capital (unanimous). No lump sum or % of capital. Drawings adjusted at final settlement.","keyPoints":["Golden rule: profit as agreed, loss by capital.","Drawings on account are adjusted against final profit or capital.","Box 12.1: provisional profit allowed; reserves allowed; cap on one partner's share allowed; receivables valued at cash value without time value.","Ali’s maxim: “Profit is based on agreement of the parties, but loss is always subject to the ratio of investment” (p. 317).","A partner who did not declare himself a sleeping partner may take a profit share above his capital ratio even if he did not in fact work (p. 320)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Sleeping partner","definition":"A partner who expressly declares he will not work; under the Hanafi view his profit share cannot exceed his capital ratio."}],"examples":[{"title":"40% investor taking 50% of profit","kind":"textbook","text":"A partner contributing 40% of capital may be allotted 50% of profit, provided he has not declared himself a sleeping partner (p. 316)."},{"title":"Loss split","kind":"practice","text":"A invests 600,000 and B 400,000; profit ratio 50:50. If the venture loses 100,000, A bears 60,000 and B 40,000 — the profit ratio is irrelevant to loss. (Practice example — generated for learning.)"},{"title":"Loss taken by one partner","kind":"textbook","text":"Under the AAOIFI Standard, one partner may take on the whole of a loss when it occurs, provided this was not a prior condition (p. 320)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Loss can be shared in the same ratio as profit if partners agree.","right":"Loss must follow the capital ratio; only profit follows agreement."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.4.4","t8.5.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A and B invest 70:30 and agree to share profit 50:50. The venture makes a loss of 10,000. B bears:","options":["5,000","3,000","7,000","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Loss follows capital: 30% × 10,000 = 3,000 (p. 317)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.3.4","sectionTitle":"Treatment of Profit and Loss","title":"Treatment of Profit and Loss in Musharakah","covers":["t12.3.4"],"overview":"Details the central Musharakah rule - profit ratio is flexible by agreement (reflecting work contribution), but loss must always track capital investment exactly - and the practical rules for provisional profit distribution.","simpleExplanation":"This is the single most important Musharakah rule: partners can agree to split PROFIT in almost any ratio they like (even different from their capital contributions, to reward whoever does more work) - but LOSS must always be split exactly according to how much capital each partner put in, no exceptions. You can't just fix a flat dollar amount or a guaranteed percentage-of-capital for anyone; profit has to be a genuine share of whatever profit actually gets made. There's some scholarly disagreement about a 'sleeping' (non-working) partner's profit share - most say it can't exceed their capital ratio if they explicitly declared themselves a sleeping partner, though it can always be less.","academicExplanation":"The profit/loss distribution ratio must be decided at agreement execution, else the contract is invalid (per Imam Ahmad and Abu Hanifa). There's some scholarly nuance on profit-vs-investment ratio divergence: Imam Malik, Shafi'e, and Zufar hold each partner must get profit EXACTLY proportional to his investment; Imam Ahmad and the majority of Hanafi jurists allow profit ratio to differ from investment ratio (by free consent); Imam Abu Hanifa combines both views - profit ratio may normally differ from investment ratio, BUT if a partner explicitly declares himself a SLEEPING partner (no work), his profit share cannot EXCEED his investment ratio (though it CAN be less, per all jurists). Hanbali jurists allow even a sleeping partner MORE than his investment ratio. In practice, the general ruling follows Abu Hanifa/Ahmad: profit ratio can differ from investment ratio based on WORK contribution (since labour, alongside capital, is a legitimate factor in profit accrual) - so a partner can stipulate extra profit as compensation for his work. However, ALL contemporary jurists agree LOSS must be shared EXACTLY per capital investment ratio - crystallized in the maxim (from Caliph Ali, RA): 'Profit is based on agreement of the parties, but loss is always subject to the ratio of investment.' Rationale: profit legitimacy flows from genuine economic activity contributing to socio-economic welfare, encompassing EQUITABLE RISK-SHARING between capital provider and entrepreneur. The profit ratio must relate to ACTUAL accrued profit, never to invested capital itself - contracts CANNOT stipulate a fixed percentage-of-capital or lump-sum-dollar profit for any partner; only percentages of actual net profit (e.g. 50:50, 30:70, or A:30%/B:40%/C:30%) are valid. If a partner contributes LESS capital but works MORE, he may get an equal OR greater profit share than his capital ratio would suggest; conversely, if capital shares are equal but work is unequal, profit shares may differ, provided the working partner gets the bigger share. On loss, there's complete jurist unanimity: each partner suffers loss EXACTLY per his investment ratio. Any partner can withdraw a provisional lump sum against expected profit, subject to final adjustment (any shortfall vs actual profit is deducted from his CAPITAL, not merely his expected profit). Partners can amend the profit-sharing ratio at any point (loss-sharing always follows capital ratio regardless). Once profit is realized, it must be shared per the agreed ratio. For Shirkatul Wujooh (creditworthiness partnership) specifically, profit-sharing and liability-sharing percentages must be determined at the outset, and loss-sharing can differ (up or down, by mutual consent) from the profit-sharing percentage - though a lump-sum profit stipulation for any partner remains impermissible. Box 12.1's consolidated rules add: profit allocation must be based on NET earnings percentages (after operating costs/expenses), never a sum of money or percentage of capital; ratios can be revised later (with surrender of profit share between partners); a partner can CAP his own profit share at a set amount, giving any excess to the others; final profit allocation cannot be based on merely EXPECTED profit (though PROVISIONAL distribution, subject to final settlement after liquidation, is allowed); if the Shirkah subject is a leased asset, rental distributed to partners is 'on account,' subject to final settlement; partners CAN agree to withhold a profit portion for reserves; different profit-sharing formulas are allowed for different periods/profit magnitudes, provided no partner is effectively precluded from participation; and final profit allocation requires actual/constructive liquidation (asset valuation), with receivables valued at cash value (net of doubtful-debt allowance) - time value of money/early-payment discounting is NOT permitted for receivables valuation.","examExplanation":"CORE RULE: PROFIT ratio is flexible (can differ from capital ratio, by mutual consent, reflecting work contribution) - Imam Ahmad/majority Hanafi view (general ruling followed); Malik/Shafi'e/Zufar require profit EXACTLY per capital ratio; Abu Hanifa's hybrid: profit can differ UNLESS a partner explicitly declares himself 'sleeping' (then capped at his capital ratio, though it can still be less); Hanbali allows even sleeping partners MORE than their capital ratio. LOSS: universal consensus - always EXACTLY per capital investment ratio, no exceptions (maxim: 'profit is based on agreement, loss is always subject to the ratio of investment'). Profit must be a % of ACTUAL net profit, never a lump sum or %-of-capital. Provisional profit draws are subject to final adjustment (shortfall deducted from CAPITAL if actual profit falls short).","keyPoints":["The central Musharakah rule: PROFIT ratio can differ from the capital investment ratio by mutual agreement (reflecting work contribution); LOSS must ALWAYS be shared exactly per capital investment ratio - this is the universal, unanimous jurist consensus.","Imam Abu Hanifa's hybrid rule (the practical general ruling followed): profit ratio may normally differ from investment ratio, but if a partner explicitly declares himself a SLEEPING (non-working) partner, his profit share cannot exceed his capital-investment ratio (though it can always be less).","The governing maxim, from Caliph Ali (RA): 'Profit is based on agreement of the parties, but loss is always subject to the ratio of investment.'","Profit ratios must be expressed as a percentage of actual accrued net profit - never as a lump sum amount or a fixed percentage tied to capital invested.","A partner contributing less capital but more work may receive an equal or greater profit share than his capital ratio alone would suggest; conversely, with equal capital but unequal work, the working partner must get the larger profit share.","Provisional profit withdrawals are subject to final settlement after liquidation; if actual profit falls short of what was drawn, the shortfall is deducted from the partner's CAPITAL, not merely treated as an overpayment of profit."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["The profit/loss distribution ratio must be agreed at the time of contract execution, or the Musharakah agreement is invalid.","Loss must always be shared exactly in proportion to each partner's capital investment - this cannot be varied by agreement.","A partner who explicitly declares himself a sleeping (non-working) partner cannot receive a profit share exceeding his capital investment ratio (per the Abu Hanifa/majority view), though it can be less."],"principles":["'Profit is based on agreement of the parties, but loss is always subject to the ratio of investment' - the governing maxim attributed to Caliph Ali (RA)."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Unequal Work, Higher Profit Share","kind":"textbook","text":"A partner with a 40% capital investment may be agreed to receive 50% of the profit, provided he has not declared himself a sleeping partner - reflecting his greater work contribution to the joint business, while his loss exposure remains strictly capped at his 40% capital ratio."}],"commonConfusions":["Students very commonly confuse the profit and loss rules, assuming both must track capital investment, or both are freely negotiable. The correct rule is asymmetric: profit ratio is flexible (by agreement, reflecting work); loss ratio is always fixed to capital investment, with no exceptions."],"distinctions":["Profit-sharing ratio (flexible, by mutual agreement, can reflect work contribution) vs loss-sharing ratio (rigid, always exactly proportional to capital investment) - the single most tested distinction in Musharakah."],"relatedConcepts":["Mudarabah","Diminishing Musharakah","Rabbul-mal"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,317,318,319,320]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Capital Contribution","description":"Partners contribute capital in any agreed proportion (contributions need not be equal); the capital is commingled - actually (physically pooled) or constructively (merged on an agreed valuation basis) - so individual ownership is replaced by collective joint-venture ownership."},{"step":"Joint Ownership and Mutual Agency","description":"Upon contract execution, partners automatically become each other's agents (Wakil, not Kafil/surety for one another); one partner's possession of Musharakah property constitutes constructive possession for all the others."},{"step":"Agreed Management Arrangement","description":"Partners agree how the business will be run: all partners may take part in routine management, management may be restricted to specific partners, or it may be delegated to an outside manager (fixed remuneration, optionally with a profit-based bonus) or structured as a Mudarabah-style profit-share-only role."},{"step":"Business Operates","description":"The partnership conducts its agreed trade or business activity using the commingled capital, under the agreed management arrangement, with partners handling routine matters such as sales, purchases, and credit requests as each other's agents."},{"step":"Profit Shared Per Agreed Ratio","description":"Realized net profit (never a lump sum or a fixed percentage of capital) is distributed per whatever ratio the partners agreed - which may differ from the capital ratio to reflect work contribution, subject to the rule that a partner who explicitly declared himself 'sleeping' cannot receive more than his capital ratio."},{"step":"Loss Shared Strictly Per Capital Ratio","description":"Any loss - meaning erosion of the Musharakah capital - is borne by every partner exactly in proportion to his capital investment, with no exceptions, per the maxim: 'profit is based on agreement of the parties, but loss is always subject to the ratio of investment.'"},{"step":"Termination or Maturity","description":"The Musharakah ends when its purpose is achieved, a partner withdraws after due notice, a partner dies, capital is wholly lost, a partner loses legal capacity, or (in modern practice) an agreed binding period expires - after which assets are valued and a final settlement made per the agreed profit and capital ratios."}],"calculations":[{"title":"Practice Example — generated for learning: Musharakah Profit vs Loss Split","formula":"Profit: split per the AGREED ratio (can differ from capital ratio). Loss: split strictly per the CAPITAL ratio, always — this can never be varied by agreement.","inputs":[{"label":"Partner A capital","value":"$40,000 (40% of $100,000 total)"},{"label":"Partner B capital","value":"$60,000 (60% of $100,000 total)"},{"label":"Agreed profit ratio (A works the business; B is a sleeping partner)","value":"50% A / 50% B"}],"steps":["Capital ratio: A = 40%, B = 60% — this governs LOSS sharing only, never profit sharing.","Profit ratio is separately agreed at 50:50, compensating A for running the business.","Scenario 1 — venture earns $20,000 profit: A's share = 50% × $20,000 = $10,000; B's share = 50% × $20,000 = $10,000.","Scenario 2 — venture instead loses $20,000: loss must follow the capital ratio, not the profit ratio. A bears 40% × $20,000 = $8,000; B bears 60% × $20,000 = $12,000."],"result":"Profit is split evenly ($10,000 / $10,000) but a loss on the same venture is split 40/60 ($8,000 / $12,000) — the two ratios are independent by design.","interpretation":"This is the single most tested Musharakah rule (12.3.4): a partner's profit share may exceed their capital ratio to reward work or skill, but no partner's loss share can ever exceed their capital ratio — loss-sharing cannot be varied by agreement under any school of thought.","generated":true,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,317,318,319,320]}}]}],"principles":["Profit is based on agreement of the parties, but loss is always subject to the ratio of investment."],"table":{"caption":"Box 12.1 — selected profit rules (pp. 319–320)","head":["Rule","Content"],"rows":[["When fixed","Ratio or basis agreed at the start"],["Form","Percentage of net earnings, never a sum or % of capital"],["Sleeping partner","Profit share capped at his capital ratio"],["Cap","A partner may cap his profit at an amount; the excess goes to others"],["Provisional profit","Allowed, subject to final settlement on actual or constructive liquidation"],["Reserves","Part of profit may be retained for reserves"],["Tiered formulas","Allowed if no partner is likely to be shut out of profit"],["Receivables","Valued at cash value, net of doubtful debts, with no time-value discount"]]},"calc":{"type":"musharakah-pl","note":"Practice calculator: enter each partner's capital, agreed profit ratio and the venture's result; profit follows the agreed ratio while any loss follows capital. Generated for learning."}},{"id":"t12.3.5","section":"12.3.5","title":"Guarantees in Shirkah Contracts","pages":[318,318],"tier":"core","concepts":["musharakah","amanah"],"intuition":"Can a bank ask its Musharakah partner for collateral?","simpleExplanation":"Partners hold assets as a trust, so no partner is liable except for breach of contract, misconduct or proven negligence. Therefore a partner cannot guarantee another's capital or profit — but may be asked for security to cover misconduct and negligence. A third party may guarantee capital against loss, under four conditions, as a promise without consideration.","academicExplanation":["Negligence occurs when a partner (i) breaches the contract's terms, (ii) acts against the norms of the business, or (iii) shows established ill-intention.","A bank may obtain adequate security from its partner to cover misconduct, breach and negligence, as risk management for depositors' funds.","Third-party guarantee conditions: (1) the guarantor does not own more than 50% of the guaranteed venture; (2) the venture does not own more than half of the guarantor; (3) the Shirkah is not conditional on the guarantee; (4) no consideration is charged. The undertaking is a ‘promise to guarantee’: partners cannot void the Shirkah or refuse obligations because it was not fulfilled (AAOIFI)."],"examExplanation":"Partners = trustees; liable only for breach, misconduct, negligence; no guarantee of capital/profit by co-partner; security for negligence allowed; independent third-party guarantee allowed on four conditions (≤50% links both ways, not a condition, no fee).","keyPoints":["Three forms of negligence.","Third-party guarantee is a promise, not a condition of the Shirkah.","Partners hold partnership assets as a trust: no one is liable except for breach, misconduct or proven negligence (p. 318)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Bank securing itself against a client partner","kind":"textbook","text":"In a Musharakah with a business client, the bank may take adequate security against the partner’s misconduct, breach of contract or negligence. It may not take security for its capital or profit as such (p. 318)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"If a third party’s guarantee is not honoured, the protected partner can declare the Shirkah void.","right":"The third party gives only a promise to guarantee. The beneficiary cannot tie the Shirkah to it or refuse his own obligations because of it (p. 318)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.4","t7.15"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which condition applies to a third-party guarantee of Musharakah capital?","options":["The guarantor must own over 50% of the venture","The guarantee must be paid for","The Shirkah contract must not be conditional on it","Only a partner can give it"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 318 (AAOIFI)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.3.5","sectionTitle":"Guarantees in Shirkah Contracts","title":"Guarantees, Security, and Third-Party Guarantees in Shirkah","covers":["t12.3.5"],"overview":"Explains why no partner's capital or profit can be guaranteed by co-partners (since assets are held in trust), when security can be demanded, and the strict conditions for a valid third-party guarantee.","simpleExplanation":"Partners hold Shirkah assets as a trust, so nobody can guarantee another partner's capital or profit - that would defeat the whole point of genuine risk-sharing. The only time a partner is liable is if they actually breach the contract, act against normal business practice, or show clear bad intent. Partners CAN ask each other for security to cover THAT specific risk (misconduct/negligence), and a THIRD PARTY (unrelated to the venture) can even guarantee against loss - but this guarantee can't be a condition of the partnership itself, and the guaranteed partners can't use the third party's failure to guarantee as an excuse to walk away from their own obligations.","academicExplanation":"All Shirkah partners hold partnership assets as a TRUST - no one is liable except for proven breach of contract, misconduct, or negligence. Negligence specifically covers three cases: (i) not abiding by contract terms/conditions; (ii) working against the concerned business's norms; (iii) established ill-intention. Consequently, neither the PROFIT nor even the CAPITAL of any partner can be guaranteed by co-partners - but one partner CAN demand security, surety, or a pledge from another to cover cases of misconduct/negligence specifically. In a bank-client Musharakah, the bank (as part of prudent risk management for depositor funds) can obtain adequate security from a partner against his misconduct, breach of contract, or negligence. Third-Party Guarantee: a genuine THIRD PARTY can guarantee to make up capital loss for all or some partners, subject to strict conditions: (1) the third party must NOT be legally/financially related to the Musharakah by owning more than 50% of the guaranteed joint venture's capital; (2) the guaranteed joint venture must not own more than half the guarantee-provider's capital; (3) the Shirkah contract itself must NOT be conditional on such a guarantee; (4) the guarantee must NOT be provided for any consideration (i.e. fulfilment of the promise by the third party is not itself a condition of the underlying Shirkah contract's validity). Critically, the third party's undertaking is legally characterized as a 'PROMISE to guarantee' - it does not create a right for the beneficiary partners to tie the Shirkah contract itself to fulfillment of that guarantee. The partners in whose favor the guarantee runs can neither claim the Shirkah should become void, nor refuse to meet their own Shirkah obligations, on the grounds that they entered the partnership relying on the third party's undertaking.","examExplanation":"Shirkah assets held as TRUST: no partner liable except for proven breach/misconduct/negligence (3 negligence triggers: not abiding by contract terms, working against business norms, established ill-intention). Neither PROFIT nor CAPITAL of a partner can be guaranteed by CO-PARTNERS - but security CAN be demanded to cover misconduct/negligence specifically. THIRD-PARTY guarantee is possible, subject to 4 conditions: (1) guarantor not >50%-owning the guaranteed venture; (2) guaranteed venture not >50%-owning the guarantor; (3) Shirkah contract NOT conditional on the guarantee; (4) guarantee not for consideration. The third-party guarantee is legally just a 'promise to guarantee' - beneficiary partners cannot void the Shirkah or refuse their own obligations based on it.","keyPoints":["Shirkah partners hold partnership assets as a trust; no partner is liable except for proven breach of contract, misconduct, or negligence (three negligence triggers: not abiding by contract terms, working against business norms, or established ill-intention).","Neither a partner's PROFIT nor his CAPITAL can be guaranteed by co-partners - this would undermine genuine risk-sharing; however, security CAN specifically be demanded to cover cases of proven misconduct or negligence.","A genuine third party CAN guarantee to make up capital loss for partners, subject to four strict conditions: the guarantor must not own more than 50% of the guaranteed venture; the venture must not own more than 50% of the guarantor; the Shirkah contract must not be conditional on the guarantee; and the guarantee must not be provided for any consideration.","The third party's undertaking is legally just a 'promise to guarantee,' not a term of the Shirkah contract itself - beneficiary partners cannot void the Shirkah or refuse their own obligations by citing the guarantee."],"definitions":[{"term":"Promise to Guarantee","definition":"The legal characterization of a third party's undertaking to cover Musharakah capital loss - it does not create a right for beneficiary partners to tie the Shirkah contract's validity to the guarantee's fulfillment."}],"conditions":["A third-party guarantor must not own more than 50% of the capital of the guaranteed joint venture, and the guaranteed venture must not own more than 50% of the guarantor's capital.","The Shirkah contract must not be made conditional on a third-party guarantee, and the guarantee must not be provided for any consideration."],"principles":["Neither a Musharakah partner's profit nor his capital can be guaranteed by his co-partners, since this would undermine the genuine risk-sharing that legitimizes profit in Shirkah."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think a third-party guarantee makes the Shirkah contract's validity contingent on that guarantee being honored. The chapter is explicit that this is not the case - the guarantee is a separate 'promise' the beneficiary partners cannot use to void or excuse themselves from their own Shirkah obligations."],"distinctions":["A co-partner guaranteeing another partner's capital/profit (NOT permitted - undermines risk-sharing) vs a security demand covering proven misconduct/negligence specifically (permitted) vs a qualifying third-party guarantee (permitted, subject to strict conditions)."],"relatedConcepts":["Kafalah","Amanah","Diminishing Musharakah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","pages":[318]}}],"processSteps":["Negligence is established in three cases (p. 318): (i) breaching the contract’s terms","(ii) acting against the norms of the business concerned","(iii) established ill-intention of the partner"]},{"id":"t12.3.6","section":"12.3.6","title":"Maturity/Termination of Musharakah","pages":[318,320],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["musharakah"],"intuition":"How does a partner exit a partnership that is meant to last?","simpleExplanation":"Musharakah is basically non-binding — a partner may withdraw with notice — but partners may agree a period or bind themselves to continuity. It ends when its purpose is achieved, a partner withdraws, dies (heirs may replace him by consent), capital is lost, or a partner loses legal capacity. In companies, shareholders exit by selling shares. A partner may promise to buy the assets at market value or a price agreed at the time, but not at a pre-agreed price or face value.","academicExplanation":["Termination events: purpose achieved (profit by ratio, loss by capital); withdrawal after notice — remaining partners continue, assets distributed pro rata then profit by ratio, preferably valued in money; death (heirs may continue by consent); loss of all capital; a partner's legal disability.","Classical jurists imposed no restriction on withdrawal because caravan ventures were short-lived; later jurists said a multi-partner contract survives one partner's withdrawal.","Under modern ‘Urf a shareholder cannot withdraw capital from a company but can sell shares; long-gestation projects cannot be terminated midway.","Partners may give a binding promise of continuity for a fixed period and terminate earlier only by mutual consent. A promise to buy partnership assets at market value, or at a price agreed on the date of buying, is valid; a promise at a pre-agreed price or face value is not, because it guarantees capital (AAOIFI)."],"examExplanation":"Musharakah non-binding but continuity may be promised. Ends: purpose achieved, withdrawal with notice, death, capital lost, disability. Buy-out promise at market/agreed-at-time price allowed; pre-agreed/face value not allowed (capital guarantee).","keyPoints":["Shareholders exit by sale of shares.","Pre-agreed buy-back price = guarantee of capital.","Partners may make a binding promise to keep the partnership going for a set period, and may still end it earlier by mutual consent (p. 319)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Caravan-trade background","kind":"textbook","text":"Classical jurists put no limits on withdrawal because partnerships were short-lived, self-liquidating ventures such as caravan trade. Long-gestation modern projects cannot be wound up midway (p. 319)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A shareholder in a limited company can withdraw his capital like a classical partner.","right":"By modern ‘Urf he cannot withdraw capital, but he can sell his shares to anyone. The jurists allow this change of ownership (p. 319)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.8","t12.9.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A partner promises to buy the other's share at face value at maturity in a contractual Musharakah. This is:","options":["Permissible","Not allowed — it guarantees the other partner's capital","Required by AAOIFI","Allowed only in trade"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 319."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.3.6","sectionTitle":"Maturity/Termination of Musharakah","title":"Maturity and Termination of Musharakah","covers":["t12.3.6"],"overview":"Explains the non-binding nature of traditional Musharakah, the recognized termination triggers, and how contemporary practice (binding-period commitments, modern corporate structures) accommodates long-gestation businesses.","simpleExplanation":"Traditionally, Musharakah isn't a locked-in commitment - any partner can pull out whenever they want. It automatically ends when the specific purpose is achieved, when a partner properly withdraws (with notice), when a partner dies, when all the capital is lost, or when a partner loses legal capacity. But this free-withdrawal idea doesn't work well for modern businesses that need years to become profitable - so scholars allow partners to agree upfront to a fixed commitment period, and a partner CAN even give a binding promise to buy out the partnership's assets later at market value (though not at some pre-fixed guaranteed price, since that would basically be guaranteeing the other partner's capital).","academicExplanation":"Musharakah is basically a NON-BINDING contract - any partner can withdraw his share at will, though partners CAN agree on a specific Shirkah timeframe. Traditional termination triggers: (1) achieving the Shirkah's purpose (for a specific-purpose Musharakah) - profit distributed per the agreed ratio, loss per investment ratio; (2) a partner's withdrawal after due notice - this does NOT necessitate terminating the partnership among REMAINING partners; assets are distributed pro rata by mutual consent (preferably valued in monetary terms), then profit per the agreed ratio; (3) death of any partner (though his heirs can replace him with other parties' consent); (4) total exhaustion/loss of Musharakah capital; (5) a partner being prevented/prohibited from exercising his legal powers over his property. Historically, early termination needs rarely arose because classical joint enterprises (like caravan trade) had short, liquidating life spans - so classical jurists imposed no restrictions on partner withdrawal. Later jurists held that for partnerships among MORE than two persons, the contract remains intact even after one partner's withdrawal. Modern practice ('Urf) differs significantly: a limited company shareholder cannot withdraw his capital directly - he can only SELL his shares to a willing buyer. In today's complex commercial environment with public control/legal structures requiring extended timeframes, and businesses needing long gestation periods and huge capital, midway project termination is essentially out of the question - jurists have accommodated this by allowing OWNERSHIP CHANGES via share sale/purchase instead of capital withdrawal. Partners CAN enter a BINDING promise for partnership continuity over a stipulated period (though they can still terminate early by MUTUAL consent). One partner can give a BINDING promise to buy the partnership's assets (within the operation period or at liquidation) at their MARKET VALUE or per an agreement made AT THE TIME of buying - but a promise to buy at a PRE-AGREED FIXED price, or at a company's share FACE VALUE, is NOT allowed in contractual partnerships, since this would effectively guarantee another partner's capital, which is prohibited in Shirkatul'aqd.","examExplanation":"Musharakah = NON-BINDING by default (any partner can withdraw at will), UNLESS a fixed timeframe is agreed. Termination triggers: purpose achieved, partner withdrawal (with notice - doesn't end the partnership among remaining partners), death, total capital loss, legal incapacity. Modern accommodation: partners CAN agree to a BINDING continuity period; a binding promise to buy assets at MARKET VALUE (or a value agreed AT THE TIME of purchase) IS allowed; a promise to buy at a PRE-FIXED price or share FACE VALUE is NOT allowed (= effectively guaranteeing the other partner's capital, prohibited in Shirkatul'aqd).","keyPoints":["Musharakah is a non-binding contract by default - any partner can withdraw at will, unless the partners have agreed on a specific timeframe for the Shirkah.","Recognized termination triggers: achievement of the Shirkah's specific purpose, a partner's withdrawal after due notice (does not end the partnership among remaining partners), death of a partner, total exhaustion/loss of capital, or a partner's legal incapacity.","Modern practice accommodates long-gestation businesses by allowing partners to agree on a BINDING continuity period, and by treating ownership changes through share sale/purchase as the mechanism for exit, rather than direct capital withdrawal (as with limited company shares).","A binding promise to buy out a partner's assets at MARKET VALUE (or a value agreed at the actual time of purchase) is permissible; a promise to buy at a PRE-FIXED price or a company's share FACE VALUE is not allowed, since this would effectively guarantee the other partner's capital - prohibited in Shirkatul'aqd."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["A binding promise to buy a partner's Musharakah share must be at market value or a value agreed at the actual time of purchase - not at a pre-fixed price or face value, which would constitute a prohibited capital guarantee."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume any binding buy-back promise in Musharakah is prohibited, confusing it with Diminishing Musharakah's price-fixing rule. In fact, a binding promise to buy at MARKET VALUE (or a value fixed at the actual purchase time) is permitted in ordinary contractual Musharakah - only a PRE-FIXED price or face-value promise is disallowed, since that would guarantee capital."],"distinctions":["A binding promise to buy assets at market value / a value fixed at purchase time (permitted) vs a promise to buy at a PRE-AGREED fixed price or face value (not permitted, constitutes a prohibited capital guarantee)."],"relatedConcepts":["Diminishing Musharakah","Khiyar","Musha'a"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.6","pages":[318,319,320]}}],"processSteps":["Five ways a traditional Shirkah ends (pp. 318–319): (1) the specific purpose is achieved","(2) a partner withdraws after due notice; the others may continue","(3) a partner dies; his heirs may replace him with the others’ consent","(4) all the capital is exhausted or lost","(5) a partner is barred from exercising legal powers over his property"]},{"id":"t12.4","section":"12.4","title":"The Concept and Rules of Mudarabah","pages":[320,323],"tier":"core","concepts":["mudarabah"],"intuition":"Why did early Muslim society need a contract that separates capital from skill?","simpleExplanation":"Mudarabah is a special Shirkah in which an investor provides capital to a manager (Mudarib) who trades with it; profit is shared in a pre-agreed proportion and loss (shortfall of capital) is borne by the investor alone, the Mudarib losing his effort. Profit cannot be a fixed amount or a percentage of capital; ambiguity about capital or ratio voids the contract, and in a void Mudarabah the Mudarib gets only a fair wage (Ujratul-mithl).","academicExplanation":["Also called Qirad and Muqaradah (Hijazi usage, adopted by Malikis and Shafi‘is); Mudarabah is the Hanafi term. Al-Sarakhsi derives it from ‘journeying’ (yadribuna fil-ard, travel for trade); Muqaradah from al-Qard, ‘cutting off’ disposition of money to the agent.","Legitimacy: Al-Marghinani notes no disagreement; the Prophet (pbuh) approved Abbas's terms; Abu Musa's funds traded by Umar's sons were treated as ex post facto Mudarabah with half the profit taken; Umar invested orphans' property on Mudarabah.","Rationale (Al-Sarakhsi): the capital owner may not find profitable trade and the skilled trader may lack capital; profit requires both.","The Mudarib acts as trustee, agent, partner, indemnifier (if he breaches) and wage-earner (if void). Multilateral Mudarabah (many financiers or many workers) and sub-Mudarabah (only with the financier's permission or discretion) are allowed.","Classical jurists (especially Shafi‘i) confined Mudarabah to trade; most contemporary scholars allow wider use by Islamic banks.","Mudarabah is a contract of fidelity: the Mudarib is not liable for normal business loss, but is liable for breach of trust, misconduct and negligence; a guarantee may be taken but enforced only for negligence or breach of stipulated conditions."],"examExplanation":"Mudarabah: capital from Rabbul-mal, work from Mudarib; profit by agreed ratio; loss on capital owner only (Mudarib loses effort). No fixed amount/% of capital. Void → Ujratul-mithl. Mudarib = trustee, agent, partner, liable for negligence/breach.","keyPoints":["Qirad/Muqaradah = Mudarabah.","Sub-Mudarabah only with permission.","Guarantee from Mudarib enforceable only for negligence or breach.","A guarantee to return funds may be taken from the Mudarib, but it can be enforced only for negligence or breach of the contract’s terms (p. 322)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Mudarabah","definition":"Partnership in which one party provides capital and the other manages; profit shared as agreed, loss borne by the capital provider."},{"term":"Rabbul-mal","definition":"The capital owner/financier in Mudarabah."},{"term":"Mudarib","definition":"The working partner/manager in Mudarabah, a trustee entitled to an agreed share of profit."},{"term":"Ujratul-mithl","definition":"Fair (market) wage paid to a Mudarib when the Mudarabah contract becomes invalid."},{"term":"Qirad / Muqaradah","definition":"The Hijazi terms for Mudarabah, used by the Maliki and Shafi‘i schools."}],"examples":[{"title":"Abu Musa and the sons of Umar","kind":"textbook","text":"Abu Musa, governor of Kufa, gave public money meant for the Bayt al-Mal to Abdullah bin Umar and his brother, who traded with it. Because the money was not a loan, the Caliph’s assembly treated it as an ex post facto Mudarabah and took half the profit (pp. 321–322)."},{"title":"Orphans’ property","kind":"textbook","text":"Umar used to invest orphans’ property on the basis of Mudarabah (p. 322)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t12.4.1","t12.5","t8.5.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"If a Mudarabah contract becomes invalid, the Mudarib is entitled to:","options":["His agreed profit share","A fair wage (Ujratul-mithl) only","All the profit","Nothing at all"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 321."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.4-12.4.1","sectionTitle":"The Concept and Rules of Mudarabah / The Nature of Mudarabah Capital","title":"The Concept and Rules of Mudarabah, and the Nature of Mudarabah Capital","covers":["t12.4","t12.4.1"],"overview":"Introduces Mudarabah's structure (capital provider + working manager), its deep historical/Prophetic roots, and the strict rules on what can (and cannot) form Mudarabah capital.","simpleExplanation":"Mudarabah is a special partnership: one side (Rabbul-mal) puts up all the money, the other side (Mudarib) does all the work and trading, and profit is split per an agreed ratio. If there's a loss, only the money-provider bears it financially - the working partner just loses their unpaid time and effort. This isn't a modern invention - it predates Islam, the Prophet (pbuh) himself worked as a Mudarib for his wife Khadijah before their marriage, and it's fully backed by consensus. The capital itself should ideally be actual money (not goods, which risk disputes), and crucially, you can NEVER convert an existing debt into Mudarabah capital - that's a classic trick to disguise an interest loan as a partnership.","academicExplanation":"Mudarabah is a special Shirkah type where an investor (or investor group) provides capital to an agent/manager who trades with it; profit is shared per a pre-agreed proportion, while LOSS is borne EXCLUSIVELY by the investor (meaning shortfall in the financier's capital/investment) - the agent's (Mudarib's) only 'loss' is his expended, unremunerated time/effort. There's no restriction on the number of capital-providers OR working partners. As with Musharakah, profit cannot be a fixed amount or percentage of capital employed; any ambiguity about capital or profit ratio invalidates the contract. If a Mudarabah becomes invalid for any reason, the Mudarib is instead entitled to Ujratul-mithl (fair market wage) for his work - no profit share. Terminology: Mudarabah is used interchangeably with Qirad and Muqaradah in Fiqh literature - the latter two presumably originated in Hijaz, Mudarabah in Iraq; Maliki/Shafi'e schools adopted 'Qirad'/'Muqaradah,' Hanafis 'Mudarabah.' Al-Sarakhsi explains 'Mudarabah' derives from 'making a journey' (the Mudarib earns profit through his effort/work, being the investor's partner in profit and in capital-use/disposition during the 'journey'); the Madinan term 'Muqaradah' derives from Al-Qard ('cutting'), reflecting how the investor cuts off his own disposition of the money and transfers it to the agent. Al-Sarakhsi preferred 'Mudarabah' for its Qur'anic resonance (referencing those who 'travel in the land [yadribuna fil-ard] in search of Allah's bounty'). Legality: Al-Marghinani confirms no dispute among Muslims about Qirad's legality - a pre-Islamic institution Islam confirmed, where the capital-user gets an agreed proportion (e.g. one-third, one-fourth, or half). Numerous Prophetic sayings/Companion reports establish unanimous jurist agreement on Mudarabah's legitimacy - the Prophet's uncle Abbas's Mudarabah contract terms were Prophet-approved; Abu Musa (governor of Kufa) remitted public money via Abdullah bin Umar and his brother, who traded with it - treated ex post facto as Mudarabah by the Caliph's assembly (half the profit taken, since the public money in their hands was not a loan); Caliph Umar also invested orphans' property on a Mudarabah basis. Al-Sarakhsi explains the underlying economic RATIONALE: capital owners may lack a path to profitable trade, while those with trading skill may lack capital - profit requires BOTH capital and trading activity, so Mudarabah lets both parties' goals be attained. The Mudarib functions in multiple capacities: trustee, agent, partner, indemnifier/liable party, and even wage-earner if the contract becomes void - as Rabbul-mal's agent, he undertakes the business and shares profit. Multilateral/sub-Mudarabahs are possible: multiple financiers can contract with a single Mudarib, a financier can contract with multiple workers (severally/jointly), and multiple workers can associate for one or more subscribers. Sub-Mudarabah (Mudarib passing capital to a THIRD party on Mudarabah terms) is only valid with the original financier's explicit or discretionary permission - absent permission, the original contract becomes voidable. Mudarabah, like other contracts, requires lawful trade items - a Mudarib cannot trade wine/swine with Mudarabah capital. Classical jurists generally restricted Mudarabah to trade (buying/selling) activity; the overwhelming majority of contemporary jurists/scholars allow WIDER scope for Islamic banks as an interest alternative. Mudarabah is a contract of FIDELITY - the Mudarib is trustworthy regarding entrusted capital, NOT liable for normal-course-of-business losses, but IS liable for property loss from breach of trust, misconduct, or negligence. A guarantee to return funds CAN be taken from him but is enforceable ONLY in two situations: his negligence in fund use, or his breach of stipulated Mudarabah conditions. On capital NATURE: Mudarabah capital should preferably be LEGAL TENDER MONEY (commodity-based capital risks uncertainty/disputes); illiquid asset values must be clearly determined in legal tender at contract time with no ambiguity. It is NOT permitted to use a debt owed by the Mudarib (or another party to the capital provider) as Mudarabah capital - the capital must be free from ALL liabilities. This prohibition specifically guards against disguising a usurious LOAN as a Mudarabah, where the financier would effectively secure both debt recovery AND an illegal return under cover of a Mudarabah profit share. A financier cannot give a Mudarib TWO different capital amounts stipulating profit from one goes to himself and the other to the Mudarib, nor specify different PERIODS with profit from one period going to one party and another period to the other, nor stipulate profit from one TRANSACTION going to the financier and another transaction's profit to the Mudarib.","examExplanation":"Mudarabah = Rabbul-mal (capital) + Mudarib (labour/management); profit shared per pre-agreed ratio; LOSS borne exclusively by the FINANCIER (Mudarib only loses unpaid time/effort). Historical roots: pre-Islamic institution confirmed by Islam; Prophet's uncle Abbas's Mudarabah terms were Prophet-approved; Caliph Umar invested orphans' property via Mudarabah. Mudarib's multiple roles: trustee, agent, partner, indemnifier (if negligent/breaching conditions), wage-earner (if contract void). Capital rules: should be LEGAL TENDER MONEY (not commodities, to avoid disputes); CANNOT be an existing debt owed by the Mudarib or third party (guards against disguising a usurious loan as Mudarabah); cannot split capital/profit by amount, period, or transaction to create artificial profit allocation between the two parties.","keyPoints":["In Mudarabah, the investor (Rabbul-mal) provides capital and the agent (Mudarib) manages/trades; profit is shared per a pre-agreed ratio, but LOSS is borne exclusively by the financier - the Mudarib's only 'loss' is his uncompensated time and effort.","Mudarabah's legitimacy is unanimous, grounded in Prophetic precedent (the Prophet's uncle Abbas's Mudarabah terms were Prophet-approved) and Companion practice (Caliph Umar investing orphans' property on a Mudarabah basis).","The Mudarib is a trustee - not liable for normal-course-of-business losses, but IS liable for loss from breach of trust, misconduct, or negligence; a guarantee from him is enforceable only for negligence or breach of stipulated conditions.","Mudarabah capital should preferably be legal tender money (not commodities, which risk uncertainty/disputes); if illiquid, its value must be clearly determined in legal tender terms at contract time.","It is NOT permitted to use a debt owed by the Mudarib (or a third party) as Mudarabah capital - this specifically guards against disguising a usurious loan as a Mudarabah, where the financier would secure both debt recovery and an illegal return under cover of a profit share.","A financier cannot artificially split capital/profit by giving two different capital amounts, specifying different time periods, or specifying different transactions, each assigned exclusively to one party - such structuring is not permitted."],"definitions":[{"term":"Rabbul-mal","definition":"The capital provider/investor in a Mudarabah or Shirkah contract, who bears the exclusive financial loss in Mudarabah (limited to erosion of his invested capital)."},{"term":"Mudarib","definition":"The working partner/manager in a Mudarabah contract, who contributes labour and expertise (not capital) and shares in profit per the pre-agreed ratio, bearing no financial loss beyond his uncompensated effort (absent negligence or misconduct)."},{"term":"Ujratul-mithl","definition":"Fair market wage - what a Mudarib is entitled to (instead of a profit share) if the Mudarabah contract becomes invalid."}],"conditions":["It is not permitted to use a debt owed by the Mudarib, or by another party to the capital provider, as capital in a Mudarabah contract.","A financier cannot stipulate that profit from one capital tranche, time period, or specific transaction goes exclusively to him while profit from another goes exclusively to the Mudarib."],"principles":["Profit requires both capital and trading skill/activity; Mudarabah lets a capital owner lacking trading ability and a skilled trader lacking capital each attain their goal, per Al-Sarakhsi's rationale."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Ex Post Facto Mudarabah under Caliph Umar","kind":"textbook","text":"Abu Musa, governor of Kufa, wanted to remit public money to the Bayt al Mal. He gave the amount to Abdullah bin Umar and his brother, who traded with it. The Caliph's assembly treated this as an ex post facto Mudarabah and took half the profits earned by the two brothers, because the public money in their hands was not a loan."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think converting an existing debt into Mudarabah capital is a convenient shortcut. The chapter explicitly prohibits this, precisely because it is a classic device for disguising a usurious loan (guaranteed recovery plus illegal return) as a legitimate Mudarabah profit-sharing arrangement."],"distinctions":["Mudarabah capital (should be legal tender money, cannot be an existing debt) vs Musharakah capital (majority view also prefers liquid assets, but goods with assessed market value are more readily accepted)."],"relatedConcepts":["Musharakah","Qard","Riba"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4-12.4.1","pages":[320,321,322,323]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Rabbul-mal Provides Capital","description":"The capital provider (Rabbul-mal) contributes the Mudarabah capital, preferably as legal tender money (an existing debt cannot serve as capital, since this would risk disguising a usurious loan as a Mudarabah)."},{"step":"Mudarib Undertakes the Business","description":"The working partner (Mudarib) takes charge of trading with the capital, acting as the Rabbul-mal's trustee and agent - he contributes no capital of his own, only labour, skill, and management."},{"step":"Business Operated by the Mudarib Alone","description":"The Mudarib runs the agreed business - restricted to a specified line of trade, place, or time if the financier so stipulated, or left unrestricted otherwise - while the Rabbul-mal has no right to participate in management, retaining only the right to oversee honest and efficient conduct."},{"step":"Profit Shared Per Agreed Ratio","description":"Any profit is divided between the two parties strictly per the pre-agreed ratio (never a lump sum or fixed amount), with final distribution determined only at actual or constructive liquidation, though provisional on-account draws are permitted subject to later adjustment."},{"step":"Loss Borne Solely by the Capital Provider","description":"Loss - meaning erosion of the Mudarabah capital - falls exclusively on the Rabbul-mal; the Mudarib bears no financial loss beyond his uncompensated time and effort, unless his negligence, misconduct, or breach of the agreed conditions caused the loss, in which case he becomes liable for it."},{"step":"Termination","description":"The Mudarabah ends by mutual consent or on completion of its restricted purpose; once the Mudarib has actually commenced business, or where a fixed duration was agreed, it becomes binding until liquidation or expiry - at which point illiquid assets are valued or sold so the final profit or loss can be determined and settled."}]}]},{"id":"t12.4.1","section":"12.4.1","title":"The Nature of Mudarabah Capital","pages":[323,324],"tier":"core","concepts":["mudarabah"],"intuition":"Why can't a lender convert a loan into Mudarabah capital?","simpleExplanation":"Capital should preferably be money; non-cash assets must be valued in money at the start. A debt owed by the Mudarib (or another) cannot be capital, otherwise a usurious loan could be disguised as Mudarabah. Profit cannot be split by separate amounts, periods or transactions. A Mudarib may mix his own capital with permission, combining Mudarabah and Musharakah.","academicExplanation":["Debt conversion into Mudarabah is prohibited so that the financier cannot recover his debt plus an illegal return under cover of profit share (AAOIFI).","Not allowed: two amounts with profit of one to the financier and of the other to the Mudarib; profit of one period to one party; profit of one transaction to one party.","Mixing: if the Mudarib invests his own funds with permission, he is a partner to that extent (Musharakah rules) as well as a worker. Example: A gives $100,000, B (Mudarib) adds $50,000; B takes one-third of profit for his capital and the remaining two-thirds is shared (e.g. equally) — other ratios may be agreed.","Bank example: depositors $2,000, bank $1,000, ratio 50:50, profit $300 → bank takes $100 on its capital; remaining $200 split 50:50 → bank $100 as Mudarib, depositors $100."],"examExplanation":"Capital: money (or assets valued in money); not a debt. No split by amounts/periods/transactions. Mudarib's own capital (with permission) → Musharakah–Mudarabah; worked example: depositors 2,000, bank 1,000, profit 300 → bank 100 + 100, depositors 100.","keyPoints":["Debt-to-Mudarabah conversion is prohibited.","Combined Mudarabah–Musharakah is common in bank pools."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Bank investing in its own Mudarabah pool","kind":"textbook","text":"Depositors $2,000, bank $1,000, 50:50 ratio, profit $300: bank receives $100 for its capital plus $100 as Mudarib; depositors receive $100 (pp. 323–324)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t12.7.1","t8.5.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why is converting a debt into Mudarabah capital prohibited?","options":["Debts are too small","It could disguise a usurious loan earning a return","Debts cannot be valued","The Mudarib would lose"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 323."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"calc":{"type":"mudarabah-mixed","note":"Reproduces the textbook's depositor/bank example: enter depositor funds, bank funds, profit and the Mudarib ratio; shows profit on bank capital first and the Mudarabah split of the remainder."}},{"id":"t12.4.2","section":"12.4.2","title":"Types of Mudarabah and Conditions Regarding Business","pages":[324,324],"tier":"core","concepts":["mudarabah"],"intuition":"How much freedom should the financier give the manager?","simpleExplanation":"Restricted Mudarabah: the financier specifies the business, items, conditions or time. Unrestricted: the Mudarib may invest in any business he sees fit. In both, the Mudarib must follow business custom. The financier may set time limits, specify or exclude goods, persons and places, and bar further Mudarabahs, provided conditions are not counterproductive. A Mudarib who violates a restriction becomes a usurper, liable for capital.","academicExplanation":["Conditions may concern the nature, place or period of work; Hanafi and Hanbali jurists allow binding the worker to a person or commodity, making it a special Mudarabah.","The financier need not contract directly: a banker may act as intermediary on investment agency (Wakalatul Istismar).","Permitted conditions: time limit; articles to trade or avoid; persons to avoid; places; fulfilment of fiduciary duties (not profitability); according to some, compel sale when profitable; no Mudarabah with others.","The Mudarib may not sell below or buy above market price, donate funds or waive receivables without explicit permission (AAOIFI)."],"examExplanation":"Restricted vs unrestricted Mudarabah. Financier may impose non-counterproductive conditions (time, goods, persons, place). Violation → Mudarib becomes usurper, liable for capital. Wakalatul Istismar possible.","keyPoints":["Financier may require fiduciary conduct, not profitability.","No sale below / purchase above market price."],"definitions":[{"term":"Restricted Mudarabah","definition":"Mudarabah where the financier specifies the business, items, conditions or time."},{"term":"Unrestricted Mudarabah","definition":"Mudarabah where the Mudarib may invest in any business he deems fit, within business custom."}],"examples":[{"title":"Restricting the Mudarib","kind":"textbook","text":"A Rabbul-mal may set a time limit, name the goods to trade (say, only cloth), bar dealings with a particular person and specify the city of trade. If the Mudarib breaks such a condition he becomes a usurper, responsible to the owner for the capital (p. 324)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t13.2.2","t8.8.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A Mudarib trades in a commodity the financier expressly excluded and loses money. He:","options":["Shares the loss with the financier","Becomes a usurper liable for the capital","Bears no liability","Must pay interest"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 324."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t11"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t11","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.4.2-12.4.3","sectionTitle":"Types of Mudarabah and Conditions Regarding Business / Work for the Mudarabah Business","title":"Types of Mudarabah, Conditions on the Mudarib, and the Financier's Right to Work","covers":["t12.4.2","t12.4.3"],"overview":"Distinguishes restricted from unrestricted Mudarabah, catalogs the conditions a financier can validly impose, and covers the contested question of whether the financier can also work in the business.","simpleExplanation":"Mudarabah can be 'restricted' (the money-provider says exactly what business to do) or 'unrestricted' (the manager gets to choose any business they see fit). Financiers can set reasonable conditions - like a time limit, which goods to trade, which places to avoid - as long as those conditions don't actually sabotage the business. If the manager breaks a condition, they become responsible for any resulting loss, almost like they've misused the money. Traditionally, most scholars said the financier isn't allowed to actually work alongside the manager (since Mudarabah exists specifically because the financier lacks trading skill) - but the book argues this reasoning breaks down if the financier DOES have skill and just needs help, so in practice this can be left to what the parties agree.","academicExplanation":"Mudarabah business types: RESTRICTED (financier specifies a particular business, items, conditions, and time - the Mudarib must operate strictly within those bounds) or UNRESTRICTED (Rabbul-mal leaves the business choice open, and the Mudarib can invest in any business he deems fit) - in both cases, the Mudarib's actions must follow the business customs relevant to the Mudarabah's subject matter. A Mudarabah contract can thus be conditional or unconditional, with conditions pertaining to the nature of work, place of work, and/or work period; Hanafi and Hanbali jurists permit binding the worker to trade with a particular person or in a particular commodity, though this converts it into a 'special Mudarabah.' It is not legally necessary for the financier to contract directly with the Mudarib - a bank can act as an investor's agent, serving as a middleman via investment agency (Wakalatul Istismar). The financier has the right to impose conditions on the Mudarib provided they are not prejudicial to the business's interests or counterproductive to the Mudarabah's purpose - examples include: fixing a time limit; specifying items to trade in or avoid; barring dealing with a particular person/company; restricting or specifying travel/trading location; requiring fulfilment of fiduciary responsibilities (but not profitability guarantees); (per some jurists) compelling the worker to sell goods if the bargain is profitable, even if he wants to hold; and barring the worker from contracting a Mudarabah with any other party. The Mudarib must follow the financier's conditions - violating a restriction or contravening a beneficial condition turns him into a USURPER, responsible for the capital to the owner. He cannot sell Mudarabah goods below general market price, buy goods above the common market price, or donate Mudarabah funds/waive business receivables without the financier's explicit permission. On the financier's right to WORK: the majority of traditional jurists disallow the financier from working in the joint business or stipulating a right to be involved in buying/selling/supplying/ordering - though he retains the right to OVERSEE and ensure the Mudarib is honestly/efficiently fulfilling his fiduciary duties. Only Hanbali jurists (and, to some extent, Hanafi jurists) allow the owner to work alongside the Mudarib; the majority's disapproval reasoning is that it would jeopardize the worker's discretion. The author critiques this classical position: it makes sense if a Mudarabah is presumed to exist BECAUSE the financier lacks business skill, but if the financier ALSO has skill and simply cannot do everything single-handedly, the rationale for prohibiting his participation breaks down; it now seems more reasonable to let the owner ensure honesty/efficiency by taking a personal interest in business affairs - and even some Hanafi jurists have allowed the financier's sale of Mudarabah goods if profitable. In present circumstances, this can be left to the parties' agreement, mindful of its impact on joint-business profitability. After capital transfer, the Mudarib needs INDEPENDENCE for normal business conduct, though the financier can still impose place/object/method restrictions, and may want quick/direct capital access (e.g. stipulating trading within a certain marketing zone).","examExplanation":"Mudarabah types: RESTRICTED (specific business/items/time set by Rabbul-mal) vs UNRESTRICTED (Mudarib chooses any business). Financier's PERMITTED conditions: time limit, specific tradeable items, barred counterparties, location restrictions, fiduciary-duty requirements (NOT profitability guarantees), forced-sale-if-profitable (some jurists), and exclusivity (no parallel Mudarabahs). Mudarib violating a condition = becomes a USURPER, personally liable for the capital. Financier's right to WORK: majority view DISALLOWS it (jeopardizes Mudarib's discretion) - only Hanbali (and partly Hanafi) allow it; author critiques the majority reasoning as outdated where the financier also has genuine skill, suggesting it be left to party agreement in modern practice.","keyPoints":["Restricted Mudarabah: the financier specifies the business, items, conditions, and time for the Mudarib's operations. Unrestricted Mudarabah: the Mudarib is free to invest in any business he deems fit, subject to relevant business customs.","A financier can validly impose conditions on the Mudarib (time limits, tradeable items, barred counterparties, location restrictions, fiduciary-duty requirements) as long as these are not prejudicial to the business's interests or counterproductive to the Mudarabah's purpose.","A Mudarib who violates a restriction or contravenes a beneficial condition becomes a 'usurper,' personally responsible for the capital to the owner - he also cannot sell below or buy above market price, nor donate funds/waive receivables without the financier's explicit permission.","The majority of traditional jurists disallow the financier from actively working in the business (jeopardizing the Mudarib's discretion) - only Hanbali jurists (and partly Hanafi jurists) permit it, though the financier always retains the right to oversee/ensure the Mudarib's honest, efficient fiduciary conduct.","The author critiques the majority's classical disapproval as premised on the financier lacking business skill - where the financier genuinely has skill and simply needs help, the rationale weakens, and the author suggests leaving this to the parties' own agreement in modern practice."],"definitions":[{"term":"Restricted Mudarabah","definition":"A Mudarabah where the financier specifies a particular business, items, conditions, and time period within which the Mudarib must operate."},{"term":"Unrestricted Mudarabah","definition":"A Mudarabah where the financier leaves the choice of business open, authorizing the Mudarib to invest the funds in any business he deems fit, subject to relevant business customs."},{"term":"Wakalatul Istismar","definition":"Investment agency - an arrangement where a bank acts as an investor's agent/middleman rather than directly contracting as Mudarib, letting the financier and ultimate working party be linked indirectly."}],"conditions":["A financier's conditions on the Mudarib must not be prejudicial to the business's interests nor counterproductive to the Mudarabah's purpose.","The Mudarib cannot sell Mudarabah goods below the general market price, buy goods above the common market price, or donate funds/waive receivables without the financier's explicit permission."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think the financier's right to impose conditions extends to guaranteeing profitability. The chapter is explicit that the financier can require the Mudarib to fulfil his FIDUCIARY responsibilities, but not a profitability outcome, since loss remains the financier's own risk to bear."],"distinctions":["A Mudarib violating a restriction (becomes a 'usurper,' personally liable for the capital) vs a Mudarib incurring a genuine business loss through no fault of his own (financier bears the loss, Mudarib is not liable)."],"relatedConcepts":["Wakalah","Ujratul-mithl","Musharakah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.2-12.4.3","pages":[324,325]}}]},{"id":"t12.4.3","section":"12.4.3","title":"Work for the Mudarabah Business","pages":[325,325],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["mudarabah"],"intuition":"Should a financier be allowed to help run the business?","simpleExplanation":"Most classical jurists (and AAOIFI) do not allow the financier to work in the business; he may only oversee the Mudarib's fiduciary conduct. Hanbalis, and to some extent Hanafis, permit his work. The author argues that where the financier has skill and simply cannot do all the work, the prohibition is not understandable, and parties may today agree any investor role bearing in mind its effect on profitability.","academicExplanation":["Reason for the majority's disapproval: it jeopardises the worker's freedom of discretion.","The author: the classical position fits if the financier lacks skill; if not, prohibition is hard to justify, and personal interest helps ensure the worker's honesty; some Hanafis even allow the financier to sell Mudarabah goods profitably.","After transfer of capital the Mudarib needs independence; the financier may still restrict place, object and method and stipulate trading within a marketing zone."],"examExplanation":"Majority/AAOIFI: financier cannot work, only supervise. Hanbali (and some Hanafi): may work. Author: parties may agree an investor role in present circumstances.","keyPoints":["Author's view is presented as his own reasoning.","The financier may restrict the Mudarib as to place, object and method of trade, e.g. a marketing zone (p. 325)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Supervision, not interference","kind":"textbook","text":"The financier may inspect accounts and visit the business to make sure the Mudarib is honest and efficient. On the majority view he may not stipulate a right to do the buying and selling himself (p. 325)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"All jurists forbid the Rabbul-mal from working in the Mudarabah business.","right":"That is the majority view (and the AAOIFI’s). The Hanbalis, and to some extent the Hanafis, allow the owner to work with the Mudarib (p. 325)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the majority of classical jurists, the Rabbul-mal:","options":["Must work alongside the Mudarib","May not work but may oversee the Mudarib's fiduciary duties","Must manage the business","Has no rights at all"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 325."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"debate":[{"issue":"May the Rabbul-mal work in the Mudarabah business?","criticism":"Majority classical view (and AAOIFI): no — it undermines the Mudarib's discretion.","response":"Hanbali and some Hanafi jurists allow it; the author argues that where the financier has skill, the prohibition is not understandable and personal involvement can promote honesty and efficiency.","alternative":"Leave it to the parties' agreement, while preserving the Mudarib's independence in normal conduct of business.","takeaway":"Know both positions and attribute the permissive position to Hanbalis and the author's reasoning, not to consensus."},{"issue":"Should the financier be allowed to work in the Mudarabah?","criticism":"The majority forbid it because it limits the Mudarib’s freedom to act on his own judgement.","response":"The author notes the rule assumes the financier lacks business skill. Where he is skilled and simply cannot do all the work alone, the rationale is less clear. Owner involvement can also help secure honesty and efficiency.","takeaway":"In present circumstances, the parties may agree on the investor’s role, bearing in mind its effect on profitability (p. 325)."}]},{"id":"t12.4.4","section":"12.4.4","title":"Treatment of Profit/Loss in Mudarabah","pages":[325,327],"tier":"core","concepts":["mudarabah","pls"],"intuition":"When does the Mudarib actually own his share of profit?","simpleExplanation":"The ratio (e.g. 50:50, 40:60) is agreed at the contract; lump sums are not allowed; different ratios may apply to different activities or places. Loss — erosion of capital — is borne by the financier; no profit is recognised until capital is intact. Profit is determined on actual or constructive liquidation; reserves may absorb losses; provisional drawings are adjusted. The Mudarib cannot claim salary or fee beyond his share.","academicExplanation":["Example: ‘If you deal in wheat you get 50%, in cloth 40%; in your town 40%, in another town 50%.’","Reserves may be created by consent; losses may be compensated from future profit or reserves; a party may donate part of profit; the financier may award a good-management bonus; net loss at liquidation is deducted from capital.","Accrual means overall adjustment over a period (closing of accounts), not transaction-wise calculation or winding up; provisional drawings are allowed.","The Mudarib's entitlement arises once profit is clear but becomes absolute only on distribution; receivables at net realisable value, without time value or discount (AAOIFI).","Ratio changes apply for the period fixed; if unspecified, custom applies; absent custom, the contract is void and the Mudarib gets the market wage.","A ceiling arrangement is allowed: above a set profit, one party takes a greater share. Final distribution follows payment of expenses.","No periodic salary: Imam Ahmad allowed daily food expenses; Hanafis limit travel expenses; a separate independent fee agreement for work outside Mudarabah custom is allowed.","If the Mudarib also contributed capital, he bears pro rata loss. Distributed profit (after liquidation) cannot be clawed back for later loss; within a period, profits first offset losses."],"examExplanation":"Profit ratio agreed at contract, no lump sum; loss = capital erosion, borne by financier; profit only after capital intact; distribution on actual/constructive liquidation; reserves allowed; no salary for Mudarib (except limited expenses); separate fee agreement allowed.","keyPoints":["Capital must be kept whole before profit exists.","Profit ceiling arrangements allowed.","If no ratio is set and there is no custom, the Mudarabah is void ab initio and the Mudarib gets a market wage (p. 326).","Imam Ahmad allows the Mudarib daily food expenses from the Mudarabah account; the Hanafis restrict the claim to expenses on business journeys (p. 326).","Profit already distributed on actual or constructive liquidation cannot be taken back to cover a later loss (p. 326)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Different ratios for different lines","kind":"textbook","text":"“If you deal in wheat you get 50 %, in cloth 40 %”, or 40 % in your own town and 50 % elsewhere. Different ratios for different situations are allowed (p. 325)."},{"title":"Profit and loss across transactions","kind":"practice","text":"Deal A makes a profit of 30,000 and deal B a loss of 10,000. The profit first makes up the loss, and the net 20,000 is shared by the agreed ratio (pp. 326–327)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A Mudarib earns a salary plus a share of profit.","right":"He earns only his agreed share; any extra-customary job needs a separate, independent agreement."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.3.4","t8.5.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A Mudarabah earns 30,000 on one deal and loses 20,000 on another in the same period. What is distributable profit?","options":["30,000","10,000","50,000","None"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Profits first offset losses; the remainder is shared (pp. 326–327)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t12"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t12","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.4.4-12.4.5","sectionTitle":"Treatment of Profit/Loss / Termination of a Mudarabah Contract","title":"Treatment of Profit/Loss and Termination in Mudarabah","covers":["t12.4.4","t12.4.5"],"overview":"Details how Mudarabah profit is shared and finalized only at liquidation, how loss (capital erosion) is treated, and the two situations where an otherwise-freely-terminable Mudarabah becomes binding.","simpleExplanation":"Both sides agree upfront on a profit split (like 50:50 or 40:60) - never a lump-sum amount. Loss simply means the capital eroded; you can't claim profit unless the original capital is still fully intact. Final profit is only really settled when the business winds down (or is valued as if it did), though partners can draw provisional amounts along the way, subject to adjustment. A Mudarabah can normally be ended by either party at any time - EXCEPT once the Mudarib has actually started working (then it's binding until wind-down) or if the parties agreed to a fixed duration upfront (can't be cut short except by mutual agreement).","academicExplanation":"Both parties can freely agree on the profit-sharing proportion (e.g. 50:40:60, etc.), decided at contract conclusion - a LUMP SUM profit/return for either party is not allowed. Different profit ratios CAN be agreed for different conditions/situations (e.g. 'if you deal in wheat, 50%; if cloth, 40%'; 'if you trade in your town, 40%; another town, 50%'). LOSS is borne by the financier and specifically means EROSION OF CAPITAL - no profit can be recognized/claimed unless the Mudarabah's capital remains INTACT. Profit distribution depends on the FINAL result at (physical or constructive) liquidation; reserves can be created by mutual consent, and any loss can be offset by future operation profits or prior reserves. At profit allocation, one partner can donate part of his profit to the other; the financier can also award a Mudarib a good-management bonus. If losses exceed profits at liquidation, the net loss balance is deducted from CAPITAL. Profits are shared 'when they accrue' - meaning overall periodic adjustment (treated as accounts closing, not necessarily business wind-up), not a transaction-by-transaction calculation. The Mudarib's profit entitlement becomes clear once Mudarabah operations demonstrably realize a profit, though this entitlement is NOT absolute until actual distribution (subject to interim-profit retention for capital protection). Receivables must be valued at cash-equivalent/net-realizable value (net of doubtful-debt provision) - neither time value of money NOR early-payment discounting is permitted in this valuation. Parties can change the profit ratio at any time, effective for the period so fixed; if no ratio is stipulated, customary practice governs; absent custom, the contract is void ab initio, and the Mudarib gets the common market wage for his service. While a lump sum cannot be stipulated for either party, parties CAN agree that above a certain profit ceiling, one party takes a greater share (and below/at the ceiling, the standard ratio applies). Profits cannot be finally distributed until all expenses are paid per custom/original agreement; final accounting covers NET profits; the Mudarib's profit share becomes SECURE only after Mudarabah liquidation and the capital owner's recovery of capital plus his profit portion. The Mudarib cannot claim any periodical salary/fee/remuneration beyond his agreed profit share - though Imam Ahmad allows drawing daily FOOD expenses from the Mudarabah account, and Hanafi jurists restrict the Mudarib's right to claim business-journey expenses. The financier and manager CAN enter a SEPARATE agreement (independent of the Mudarabah agreement) assigning any non-customary job for a fee - meaning the Mudarabah contract itself is unaffected if the Mudarib is terminated from that separate service. As a principle, only the financier bears loss - UNLESS the Mudarib has also contributed capital (with mutual consent), in which case he bears PRO RATA loss on that portion. Profit already distributed upon constructive/actual liquidation cannot later be withdrawn to cover a subsequent loss or any other purpose. If loss occurs in some transactions and profit in others, the profit first OFFSETS the loss, with any remainder distributed per the agreed ratio. On TERMINATION: the general principle is that Mudarabah is NOT a binding contract - either party can terminate unilaterally, EXCEPT in two situations: (i) once the Mudarib has already commenced business (the contract becomes binding until actual/constructive liquidation); (ii) when the parties agreed on a specific contract duration (cannot terminate before expiry except by mutual agreement). Upon termination, the Mudarib is given time to sell illiquid assets so actual profit can be determined. The AUTHOR notes that unlimited termination power creates modern difficulties, since most contemporary enterprises need time to bear fruit and demand continuous, complex effort - so an upfront agreement that NEITHER party will terminate during a specified period (except in specified circumstances) does not violate Shari'ah, supported by the Hadith: 'All the conditions agreed upon by the Muslims are upheld, except a condition which allows what is prohibited or prohibits what is lawful in Shari'ah.' If all Mudarabah assets are cash at termination with some profit earned, distribution follows the agreed ratio directly; if NOT in cash form, the Mudarib gets an opportunity to sell/liquidate first, so actual profit can be determined. A restricted Mudarabah automatically winds up once its object is achieved. A general (unrestricted) Mudarabah winds up at will whenever both parties mutually agree - if one party wants to continue while the other doesn't, reconciliation should be sought via a court or arbitration.","examExplanation":"Profit: freely agreed ratio (NEVER a lump sum); can vary by condition/scenario (different business type, location). LOSS = capital erosion specifically; no profit recognized unless capital remains INTACT. Final distribution only at (actual/constructive) liquidation; provisional draws subject to adjustment. Mudarib gets NO separate salary/fee beyond his profit share (exceptions: Imam Ahmad allows daily food expenses; Hanafi restricts journey-expense claims); a SEPARATE non-Mudarabah service agreement is possible for other jobs. Termination: Mudarabah is NON-BINDING by default (either party can terminate anytime) EXCEPT: (1) once the Mudarib has actually started business (binding until liquidation); (2) if parties agreed a fixed duration upfront (binding until expiry, absent mutual consent to end early). Author endorses upfront no-early-termination agreements as Shari'ah-compliant, citing the Hadith on upholding agreed conditions (except those permitting the prohibited or prohibiting the lawful).","keyPoints":["Mudarabah profit must be a freely agreed RATIO (never a lump sum), which can validly vary by condition or scenario (e.g. different business type or trading location).","'Loss' in Mudarabah specifically means EROSION OF CAPITAL - no profit can be recognized or claimed unless the Mudarabah's original capital remains fully intact.","Final profit distribution occurs only at actual or constructive liquidation; provisional/on-account profit draws during the Mudarabah's life are subject to final adjustment.","The Mudarib cannot claim any separate salary, fee, or remuneration beyond his agreed profit share (with narrow classical exceptions like daily food expenses under Imam Ahmad's view) - though a SEPARATE, independent agreement can compensate him for non-customary services.","Mudarabah is non-binding by default (either party can terminate unilaterally at will), EXCEPT once the Mudarib has actually commenced business (binding until liquidation) or where the parties agreed a specific contract duration upfront (binding until expiry, absent mutual consent to end early).","The author endorses upfront agreements binding both parties to a fixed no-early-termination period as Shari'ah-compliant, given modern businesses' need for sustained time and effort, citing the Hadith on upholding agreed conditions unless they permit the prohibited or prohibit the lawful."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["No profit can be recognized or claimed in a Mudarabah unless the Mudarabah's original capital remains fully intact.","Mudarabah termination cannot occur unilaterally once the Mudarib has commenced business (binding until liquidation) or where the parties agreed to a specific contract duration (binding until expiry, absent mutual consent)."],"principles":["The Hadith 'All the conditions agreed upon by the Muslims are upheld, except a condition which allows what is prohibited or prohibits what is lawful in Shari'ah' supports the validity of upfront agreements fixing a Mudarabah's non-terminable duration."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Conditional Profit Ratios","kind":"textbook","text":"A financier can validly agree to different profit ratios depending on business conditions: 'If you deal in wheat, you will get 50%, but if you deal in cloth, you will be given 40% of the profit. Or if you do business in your town, you will get 40%, and if in another town, your share in the profit will be 50%.'"}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think Mudarabah profit can be drawn and kept freely during the contract's life. In fact, any provisional/on-account draws are subject to final adjustment at liquidation, and distributed profit cannot later be clawed back to cover a subsequent loss."],"distinctions":["A Mudarabah before the Mudarib has started work (freely terminable by either party) vs after work has commenced or a fixed duration was agreed (binding until liquidation or expiry)."],"relatedConcepts":["Rabbul-mal","Musharakah","Diminishing Musharakah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4-12.4.5","pages":[325,326,327]}}]},{"id":"t12.4.5","section":"12.4.5","title":"Termination of a Mudarabah Contract","pages":[327,327],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["mudarabah"],"intuition":"Can a financier pull out halfway through a voyage?","simpleExplanation":"Mudarabah is non-binding and either party may terminate, except (i) once the Mudarib has commenced business — binding until actual or constructive liquidation — and (ii) where a fixed duration was agreed. The Mudarib is given time to liquidate non-cash assets. The author considers an agreed non-termination period compatible with Shari’ah, citing the Hadith that Muslims are bound by their conditions.","academicExplanation":["On termination, cash profit is shared by the ratio; non-cash assets are sold first so actual profit can be determined (AAOIFI).","Unlimited termination rights create difficulty for modern enterprises needing time; a specified non-termination period does not seem to violate any principle.","A restricted Mudarabah winds up when its object is achieved; disputes over continuation go to a court or arbitration."],"examExplanation":"Mudarabah terminable unilaterally except after business commences (until liquidation) or within an agreed term; Mudarib given time to liquidate; agreed lock-in acceptable.","keyPoints":["Hadith: ‘Muslims are bound by their conditions’ except those making Haram lawful or Halal unlawful.","A fixed non-termination period is supported by the Hadith: “All the conditions agreed upon by the Muslims are upheld, except a condition which allows what is prohibited or prohibits what is lawful” (p. 327).","A restricted Mudarabah ends when its object is achieved; disputes over continuing a general one go to a court or arbitration (p. 327)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Non-cash assets at termination","kind":"textbook","text":"If the Mudarabah’s assets are not in cash when it ends, the Mudarib must be given time to sell them so the actual profit can be determined (p. 327)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Either party may end a Mudarabah at any time.","right":"Normally yes, but not once the Mudarib has started business (then it binds until liquidation) or within an agreed fixed term, except by mutual consent (p. 327)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.3.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"When does a Mudarabah become binding until liquidation?","options":["At signing","Once the Mudarib has commenced the business","Never","Only after profit"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 327."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t12.5","section":"12.5","title":"Mudarabah Distinguished from Musharakah","pages":[327,328],"tier":"core","concepts":["mudarabah","musharakah"],"intuition":"If you had to explain the difference in a single table, what would its rows be?","simpleExplanation":"Seven differences: investment source; management rights; loss-bearing; liability; profit distribution timing; ownership of assets; and valuation on dissolution.","academicExplanation":["(1) Musharakah investment from all partners; Mudarabah from the financier(s), not the Mudarib.","(2) All Musharakah partners may manage; the Rabbul-mal may not (unless agreed) but may ensure fiduciary duties.","(3) Musharakah loss by capital ratio; Mudarabah loss on the Rabbul-mal only, unless the Mudarib is negligent or dishonest.","(4) Musharakah partners' liability is normally unlimited (unless a no-debt condition was breached by one partner); the Rabbul-mal's liability is limited to his investment unless he permitted borrowing.","(5) Musharakah profit may be distributed periodically by valuing assets; Mudarabah final distribution after liquidation, with on-account payments; contemporary jurists accept constructive liquidation.","(6) Musharakah assets are jointly owned, so partners gain from appreciation even without sale; Mudarabah assets belong to the Rabbul-mal, and the Mudarib earns only on profitable sale (minority exceptions).","(7) On dissolution, Mudarabah assets must be valued in money before distribution; not necessary in Shirkah."],"examExplanation":"Seven differences: capital source; management; loss; liability (unlimited vs limited); distribution timing; ownership/appreciation; valuation on dissolution.","keyPoints":["Most-asked comparison in the chapter."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Unsold goods","kind":"textbook","text":"A Mudarib buys goods for 100, which rise to 130 but are unsold at year-end. In Mudarabah the goods belong solely to the Rabbul-mal and the Mudarib gains only by selling at a profit. In Musharakah both partners would share in the appreciation as joint owners (p. 328)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A Mudarib shares in the appreciation of unsold goods, just as a partner does.","right":"Goods bought by the Mudarib belong solely to the Rabbul-mal. The Mudarib earns only by selling them at a profit, though a minority view has exceptions (p. 328)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.3","t12.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In which contract is the financier's liability normally limited to his investment?","options":["Musharakah","Mudarabah","Both","Neither"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 328."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t13"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t13","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.5","sectionTitle":"Mudarabah Distinguished from Musharakah","title":"Mudarabah Distinguished from Musharakah","covers":["t12.5"],"overview":"Consolidates the seven key differences between Mudarabah and Musharakah, covering investment source, management rights, loss-bearing, liability, profit distribution timing, asset ownership, and dissolution valuation.","simpleExplanation":"Here's a clean side-by-side comparison. Musharakah: everyone puts in money and can help manage; Mudarabah: only one side puts in money, the other just manages. Musharakah: loss is shared by everyone per their capital; Mudarabah: only the money-provider bears the loss (unless the manager was negligent). Musharakah: partners can generally owe unlimited debts on the business; Mudarabah: the money-provider's liability is capped at what they invested, unless they explicitly allowed more. Musharakah: profit can be split up periodically as the business runs; Mudarabah: it's really only final once the business winds down (though you can draw provisionally). Musharakah: everyone jointly owns everything and benefits from any value appreciation; Mudarabah: the money-provider alone owns the purchased assets, and the manager only earns via profitable sales.","academicExplanation":"Seven key distinctions: (1) Investment source: Musharakah - all partners invest; Mudarabah - investment comes from a person/group, NOT from the Mudarib. (2) Management rights: Musharakah - all partners can participate in management/work; Mudarabah - the Rabbul-mal has NO right to participate in management (though he CAN work with mutual consent, and always retains the right to ensure the Mudarib performs his fiduciary duties honestly). (3) Loss-bearing: Musharakah - all partners share loss per their investment ratio; Mudarabah - loss is suffered by the Rabbul-mal ALONE, UNLESS the Mudarib acted with negligence or dishonesty (in which case he becomes liable for the loss he caused). (4) Liability scope: Musharakah partners' liability is normally UNLIMITED (though if all partners agree no partner shall incur business debt, exceeding-assets liability falls solely on whoever violates this in incurring a debt); Mudarabah's Rabbul-mal liability is LIMITED to his investment, unless he permitted the Mudarib to incur debts on his behalf. (5) Profit distribution timing: Musharakah profit can be distributed on an annual/quarterly/monthly basis via asset valuation; Mudarabah's FINAL distribution can only occur after liquidation of the business (though on-account payments are possible, subject to ultimate adjustment) - contemporary jurists have accepted CONSTRUCTIVE liquidation (determining nonliquid assets' market value) to solve problems in perpetual Mudarabah. (6) Asset ownership: in Musharakah, all assets become JOINTLY owned by all partners per their investment proportion - each can benefit from asset value appreciation even without a sale; in Mudarabah, all goods/assets purchased by the Mudarib are SOLELY owned by the Rabbul-mal, and the Mudarib earns his profit share only by selling assets profitably (though minority-view exceptions exist). (7) Dissolution valuation: if a Mudarabah business is dissolved, its assets/profit can only be distributed after money-terms valuation; for Shirkah (Musharakah), this valuation step is not necessary.","examExplanation":"7 Mudarabah-vs-Musharakah distinctions: (1) investment source - all partners (Musharakah) vs only Rabbul-mal, not the Mudarib (Mudarabah); (2) management - all partners can (Musharakah) vs Rabbul-mal cannot, absent mutual consent (Mudarabah); (3) loss - shared per capital ratio (Musharakah) vs Rabbul-mal alone, unless Mudarib negligent/dishonest (Mudarabah); (4) liability - normally unlimited (Musharakah) vs capped at investment, unless debt-incurring permission given (Mudarabah); (5) profit distribution timing - periodic via valuation (Musharakah) vs only at liquidation (constructive liquidation now accepted) (Mudarabah); (6) asset ownership - jointly owned, appreciation benefits all (Musharakah) vs solely Rabbul-mal-owned, Mudarib only profits via sale (Mudarabah); (7) dissolution valuation - not necessary (Musharakah) vs required, money-terms (Mudarabah).","keyPoints":["Investment: in Musharakah, all partners invest capital; in Mudarabah, investment comes solely from the Rabbul-mal (financier), never from the Mudarib.","Management: in Musharakah, all partners have a right to participate in management; in Mudarabah, the Rabbul-mal has no right to participate (though he can work with mutual consent, and always retains the right to oversee the Mudarib's fiduciary conduct).","Loss: in Musharakah, all partners share loss per their investment ratio; in Mudarabah, loss falls exclusively on the Rabbul-mal, unless the Mudarib's negligence or dishonesty caused it.","Liability: Musharakah partners' liability is normally unlimited; the Mudarabah Rabbul-mal's liability is limited to his investment, unless he permitted the Mudarib to incur debts on his behalf.","Asset ownership: in Musharakah, assets are jointly owned by all partners (each benefiting from value appreciation even without a sale); in Mudarabah, purchased assets are solely owned by the Rabbul-mal, and the Mudarib profits only through profitable sale."],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students often conflate the two modes since both involve profit-sharing. The seven distinctions - especially investment source, loss-bearing, and asset ownership - are the clearest tests for identifying which mode a given structure actually is."],"distinctions":["Musharakah (all partners invest and can manage, joint asset ownership, proportional loss-sharing) vs Mudarabah (only the financier invests, financier cannot manage, financier sole asset owner, financier bears all loss absent Mudarib fault)."],"relatedConcepts":["Rabbul-mal","Mudarib","Shirkatul'aqd"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.5","pages":[327,328]}}],"table":{"caption":"Mudarabah vs Musharakah (pp. 327–328)","head":["Point","Musharakah","Mudarabah"],"rows":[["Investment","All partners","Rabbul-mal only"],["Management","All may work","Rabbul-mal has no right (may work by consent)"],["Loss","By capital ratio","Rabbul-mal only, unless the Mudarib is negligent or dishonest"],["Liability","Normally unlimited","Limited to investment unless debts authorised"],["Profit distribution","Periodic, by valuation","After liquidation; on-account payments allowed"],["Asset ownership","Joint; all gain from appreciation","Rabbul-mal owns; Mudarib earns only by profitable sale"],["On dissolution","Money valuation not required","Assets valued in money first"]]}},{"id":"t12.6","section":"12.6","title":"Modern Corporations: Joint Stock Companies","pages":[328,330],"tier":"core","concepts":["shares","musharakah"],"intuition":"Is buying a share in a listed company a form of Shirkah?","simpleExplanation":"Modern corporations are based on Shirkah al ‘Inan or a Musharakah–Mudarabah combination. Shareholders own the company's assets pro rata with limited liability (AAOIFI); the company is a juristic person bound by its Articles, so shareholders exit by selling shares. New shares at fair value are allowed; preference shares with priority or fixed dividends are not. Shares of Halal companies may be traded; interest income, if identified, is purified. Shares can be pledged but not short-sold.","academicExplanation":["AAOIFI: a stock company's capital is divided into equal tradable shares with liability limited to pro rata capital; ‘Inan rules apply except for limited liability. Shareholders own assets but have no discretion over them.","A juristic personality separates company liability from shareholders'; limited liability requires public disclosure of its financial position; continuity is binding under the Articles of Association.","Subscriptions may be in instalments; new shares at the fair value of old shares (premium, discount or par).","Trading: allowed in Halal companies; price may differ from par if more than 50% of assets are fixed (liquid assets under 50%); if the main business is Halal but some transactions involve interest, purchase allowed with charity of identified interest income.","Short-selling is not allowed; a broker's promise to lend a share is not ownership. Shares can be pledged.","Shares redeemed gradually through distributed profits are non-compliant (Fiqh Academy Resolution 63): the payments are profit, so holders remain owners.","Preference shares earmarking fixed dividends are non-compliant (no gain without risk); qualification shares for directors are different.","Underwriting may be done without consideration (buying unsubscribed shares at par); fees may be charged for other services such as feasibility studies and marketing (AAOIFI).","Liquidity rule: shares whose capital is still liquid trade only at face value (else money-for-money = Riba); once assets are nonliquid, any price. For mixed assets: Shafi‘i classical — separate sale; Hanafi — price must exceed liquid assets; most contemporary scholars require nonliquid assets above 50% (some 33%)."],"examExplanation":"Company = ‘Inan/Musharakah–Mudarabah with limited liability; exit by sale; preference shares and profit-redeemed shares non-compliant; trade Halal shares with purification; liquid-capital shares at par only; mixed assets: >50% (or 33%) nonliquid; no short-selling; underwriting without fee (other services can be charged).","keyPoints":["Preference shares: non-compliant.","Underwriting commitment itself is not a paid service.","Links to Chapter 8 screening rules.","Qualification shares for directors are acceptable: they set a minimum commitment without preferential pecuniary rights (p. 330)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"When can a new company’s shares trade above par?","kind":"textbook","text":"A company raises 100 million and still holds it all in the bank. Its shares may change hands only at face value, since they represent money. Once it has bought land and machinery forming the majority of its assets, they may trade at any agreed price (p. 330)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"An underwriter may charge a fee for undertaking to buy unsubscribed shares.","right":"Underwriting itself must be without consideration. The underwriter may charge only for other services, such as feasibility studies or marketing (p. 330)."},{"wrong":"Shares of a company whose capital is still cash may trade at any market price.","right":"While the capital is liquid, shares represent money and may be sold only at face value, since otherwise money is exchanged for more money (p. 330)."}],"relatedTopics":["t8.8.2","t15.3.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A company's assets are still entirely cash just after its IPO. Its shares can be traded:","options":["At any price","Only at face value","Not at all","At a discount only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Trading liquid money at a different price would be Riba (p. 330)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t14"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t14","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.6","sectionTitle":"Modern Corporations: Joint Stock Companies","title":"Modern Corporations: Joint Stock Companies as Shirkah al 'Inan","covers":["t12.6"],"overview":"Explains how modern limited-liability joint stock companies map onto Shirkah al 'Inan principles (with limited liability as the key exception), and the rules governing share trading, preference shares, and mixed-asset company stocks.","simpleExplanation":"Modern companies with shares are basically a form of general partnership (Shirkah al 'Inan), with one key twist: shareholders' liability is limited to what they invested, unlike a traditional partner who could be on the hook for more. You can only buy/sell shares of companies doing lawful (Halal) business. 'Preference shares' that guarantee some shareholders a fixed dividend or priority payout are NOT Shari'ah-compliant, because they break the basic rule that profit-sharing must come with real risk-sharing. Whether you can freely trade a company's shares on the stock market depends on what the company actually owns: if it's mostly just cash/receivables, trading the shares is basically trading money for money (not allowed above face value); if it owns real assets (over 50%, per most contemporary scholars), you can trade the shares at any market price.","academicExplanation":"Modern corporate bodies can be considered based on Shirkah al 'Inan, or a combination of Musharakah and Mudarabah, allowed by the generality of contemporary jurists. Various modern corporate forms exist (joint stock companies with limited liability, joint liability companies, companies limited by shares, partnerships in commendum), governed by similar general principles - the chapter focuses on modern limited-liability stock companies. The main corporate ingredient is share/certificate issuance to investors, representing proportionate ownership. AAOIFI defines a stock company as an entity whose capital is distributed into equal, tradable share units, with shareholders' liability LIMITED to their pro rata capital. Shirkah al 'Inan rules apply EXCEPT on the limited-liability issue - shareholders own company assets to the extent of their shares, can sell/transfer shares, but have no discretion over company assets directly. A legally incorporated company is a JURISTIC PERSONALITY, unable to avoid obligations to those dealing with it - this separates company liability from shareholder liability (capped at paid-up capital, made public for customer awareness); it involves a binding continuity contract per its Articles of Association, so no one can terminate the company based on his shares alone (though a shareholder CAN sell/relinquish his shares). Share capital can be contributed as a lump sum or instalments (unpaid instalments = an undertaking to later increase the company share). New shares can be issued to increase capital, provided they're issued at the OLD shares' fair value (premium, discount, or nominal value all permitted). Preference shares giving priority profit allocation at liquidation do NOT conform to Shari'ah. IFIs can purchase/sell any company's shares PROVIDED the company undertakes Halal business; share prices can be above or below nominal value, provided over 50% of the company's assets are fixed (i.e. liquid assets - cash plus receivables - are under 50%). If a company's MAIN business is not against Shari'ah but SOME transactions involve un-Islamic elements (e.g. dealing with interest-based institutions), its shares can still be purchased, provided any identified interest-derived income portion is given to charity. One cannot sell shares one does not own; a broker's promise to LEND a share to a short-seller at delivery date does not confer ownership/possession. Shares CAN be pledged (anything saleable can be pledged). Shares that are gradually redeemed BEFORE company termination, solely through profit distribution, do NOT conform to Shari'ah - because the funds certificate holders receive in this manner constitute PROFIT on their shares, and claiming redemption 'in consideration for' distributed profit is invalid; certificate holders REMAIN owners and are entitled to liquidation proceeds. Preference shares earmarking a fixed dividend percentage similarly fail Shari'ah principles - the basic profit-distribution principle is 'no gain without undertaking loss risk'; ALL subscribing partners must be treated alike (per shares held); if any partner is privileged to share profit WITHOUT sharing loss, the partnership contract is VOID due to the interest element involved. Qualification shares (determining minimum director attention/devotion, without preferential PECUNIARY treatment) are treated differently and remain acceptable. Underwriting: a shareholder or third party can underwrite a share issue WITHOUT consideration, undertaking (via agreement at incorporation/issue time) to buy all/part of unsubscribed shares at nominal value; the underwriter CAN charge for OTHER services (feasibility studies, marketing) beyond bare underwriting. On share tradability: shares/certificates float to raise LIQUID assets; while capital remains in LIQUID form (cash, receivables, advances due), shares represent only money and can be sold ONLY AT FACE VALUE (else it's money exchanged for more money = Riba). Once raised money is used to purchase NONLIQUID assets (land, buildings, machinery, raw material, furniture), the shares/Musharakah certificates represent proportionate ownership in THOSE assets, and CAN be sold in the secondary market at ANY agreed price (more or less than face value), since the sale's subject matter is a tangible-asset share, not pure money. For companies with MIXED liquid/nonliquid assets (the typical case: raw material, fixed assets, finished-goods inventory, sales proceeds, receivables), classical Shafi'e jurists hold combined assets cannot be sold unless tangible assets are separated and sold independently; Hanafi jurists allow selling combined liquid+tangible assets for an amount GREATER than the liquid-asset portion, without prescribing a specific tangible-asset proportion threshold. However, most contemporary scholars (including Shafi'e-school scholars) allow trading shares/certificates only if NONLIQUID assets exceed 50% of the business (some reduce this floor to 33%).","examExplanation":"Modern joint stock companies = Shirkah al 'Inan + limited liability (the one exception to standard Shirkah rules). Preference shares (guaranteed dividend/liquidation priority) = NOT Shari'ah-compliant (violates 'no gain without loss risk'; makes the partnership VOID if effectively interest-like). Qualification shares (minimum director attention, no pecuniary preference) = ACCEPTABLE. Share redemption solely via profit distribution before company termination = NOT compliant (redemption 'in consideration for profit' is invalid; holders remain owners). Share tradability rule: capital still LIQUID (cash/receivables) -> tradable only at FACE VALUE (else = Riba, money for more money); capital in NONLIQUID assets -> tradable at ANY agreed market price. Mixed liquid/nonliquid company: most contemporary scholars (including Shafi'e-school) require nonliquid assets > 50% (some use 33% floor) for share trading to be permissible.","keyPoints":["Modern joint stock companies are treated as Shirkah al 'Inan, with limited liability of shareholders being the one key exception to standard Shirkah rules.","Preference shares (guaranteeing fixed dividends or liquidation priority) do not conform to Shari'ah - the core principle is 'no gain without undertaking loss risk'; if a partner shares profit without sharing loss, the partnership contract is void due to the effective interest element.","Shares that are gradually redeemed before company termination solely through profit distribution are not Shari'ah-compliant - the redeemed amount constitutes profit, not a valid consideration for redemption, so certificate holders remain owners until actual company liquidation.","Share tradability hinges on the company's asset composition: while capital remains purely liquid (cash/receivables), shares can only be sold at FACE VALUE (else it is money-for-more-money, Riba); once invested in nonliquid/tangible assets, shares can be sold at any agreed market price.","For companies with mixed liquid and nonliquid assets (the typical real-world case), most contemporary scholars require nonliquid assets to exceed 50% of the business (some use a 33% floor) for share trading to be Shari'ah-permissible."],"definitions":[{"term":"Preference Shares","definition":"Shares carrying special financial characteristics that give holders priority for profit allocation or liquidation proceeds - not Shari'ah-compliant, since they break the link between profit entitlement and loss risk."},{"term":"Qualification Shares","definition":"Shares determining the minimum extent of a director's attention/devotion to a company, without any preferential pecuniary (financial) treatment - distinct from preference shares and Shari'ah-acceptable."}],"conditions":["IFIs can purchase or sell shares of a company only if its core business is Halal; if some incidental transactions involve un-Islamic elements, any identified interest-derived income must be given to charity.","While a company's capital remains purely liquid (cash, receivables, advances due), its shares can be sold only at face value.","For companies with mixed liquid and nonliquid assets, most contemporary scholars require nonliquid assets to exceed 50% (some use a 33% floor) for share trading to be permissible."],"principles":["The basic principle of profit distribution: no gain can be had without undertaking the risk of loss - the basis for rejecting preference shares and any arrangement privileging one partner to share profit without sharing loss."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think all share redemption arrangements before company termination are impermissible. The distinction is between redemption funded purely by PROFIT distribution (not compliant, since it disguises profit as a redemption consideration) vs a genuine sale of shares to another buyer (compliant, an ordinary tradable-asset transaction)."],"distinctions":["Shares of a company with purely liquid capital (tradable only at face value) vs shares of a company holding nonliquid/tangible assets exceeding the relevant threshold (tradable at any agreed market price)."],"relatedConcepts":["Shirkah al 'Inan","Musha'a","Sukuk"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[328,329,330]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Classical Shafi‘i view","b":"Hanafi view","text":"Shafi‘i: mixed assets cannot be sold together unless the tangible assets are separated and sold independently. Hanafi: the combination can be sold for more than its liquid part, with no fixed proportion. Most contemporary scholars require non-liquid assets above 50 %; some say 33 % (p. 330)."}]},{"id":"t12.7","section":"12.7","title":"Modern Application of the Concept of Shirkah","pages":[330,331],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["shirkah"],"intuition":"Where does Shirkah appear in a modern financial system?","simpleExplanation":"Musharakah and Mudarabah can be used on both the liabilities and assets sides: corporate firms, mutual funds, Mudarabah companies, fund-managed portfolios, participation certificates, Musharakah agreements, project finance, running Musharakah, investment certificates and Diminishing Musharakah. A combination of Shirkatulamwal and Shirkatula‘mal suits modern service firms in medicine, law, IT and architecture.","academicExplanation":["The principles allow a system that conforms to Shari’ah and meets business needs on both sides of the balance sheet (Sections 12.7.1–12.7.3).","Section 12.7 turns from classical rules to modern practice. Since what is allowed for an individual is allowed for a group, Shirkah can underpin a whole system on both sides of a bank’s balance sheet. On the liabilities side this means pooled Mudarabah deposits. On the assets side the forms include corporate firms, mutual funds, Mudarabah companies, portfolios run by fund managers, participation certificates, project finance, running Musharakah and Diminishing Musharakah for fixed assets. Service firms in medicine, law, IT and architecture can combine partnership in capital (Shirkatulamwal) with partnership in work (Shirkatula‘mal) (pp. 330–331)."],"examExplanation":"Shirkah applications: deposits (Mudarabah pools), assets (trade, project, running Musharakah, DM), securitisation (TFCs, Sukuk), service firms (Amwal + A‘mal).","keyPoints":["Service firms: combination of capital and labour partnership.","Shirkatulamwal combined with Shirkatula‘mal suits modern professional service firms (p. 331)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Professional partnerships","kind":"textbook","text":"A group of architects contributes capital for an office (Shirkatulamwal) and also shares fee income from joint work (Shirkatula‘mal). The book suggests this combination for modern service firms in medicine, law, IT and architecture (p. 331)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t12.7.1","t12.7.2","t12.7.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which combination suits modern professional service firms?","options":["Wujooh and Mufawadah","Shirkatulamwal and Shirkatula‘mal","Mudarabah only","Shirkatulmilk only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 331."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t15"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t15","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.7-12.7.3","sectionTitle":"Modern Application of the Concept of Shirkah / Deposits Side / Assets Side / Securitization on a Shirkah Basis","title":"Modern Application of Shirkah: Deposits, Assets Side, and Securitization","covers":["t12.7","t12.7.1","t12.7.2","t12.7.3"],"overview":"Surveys how Shirkah principles apply across the full breadth of modern Islamic banking - deposit pool mobilization, asset-side trade/project financing, and Shirkah-based securitization via TFCs/PTCs and Sukuk.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic banks use Shirkah on both sides of their balance sheet. On the deposits side, everyone's money gets pooled together, the bank invests it (acting mostly like a Mudarib, though technically like a Musharakah partner if the bank contributes its own money from the start), and profits get split by pre-agreed ratios with different 'weights' for different deposit types. On the assets side, Musharakah and Mudarabah can finance trade (especially import/export, where fraud risk is lower), running businesses, and big projects - and can even be securitized into tradable certificates (like TFCs) that spread risk among many investors. Despite genuine potential, banks have shied away from Musharakah in practice because it's riskier to monitor and administer than simpler modes like Murabaha and leasing.","academicExplanation":"Since whatever is permissible for an individual is permissible for a group, Shirkah (traditionally an individual owner-Mudarib contract) has become a group activity in modern banking. Deposits Side (12.7.1): the core depositor-Islamic bank relationship is financier-Mudarib. All deposits are pooled; the bank invests the pool; direct expenses are charged to the pool, while general management/Head Office expenses are borne by the bank (Mudarib) itself; net proceeds are distributed per stipulated ratios, with different weightages assignable by tenure/size (subject to sufficient disclosure to all depositors). Profit distribution involves CONSTRUCTIVE LIQUIDATION after each tenure/accounting period, after which the joint relationship restarts. If the bank puts its own equity into the pool AT POOL CREATION, the relationship is MUSHARAKAH; if SUBSEQUENTLY, it is MUDARABAH (with the bank as just another investor). The OVERALL relationship remains Mudarib/Rabbul-mal, since (unlike true Musharakah partners) depositors have no right to participate in the business's overall appreciation and are essentially sleeping partners with no management/decision rights. Longer deposit tenors get greater weightage; preferential weightages CAN be assigned to specific categories (e.g. pensioners, welfare associations), but assigning different weightages merely for BIGGER deposit sizes as a matter of course would violate justice principles. Per Mudarabah rules, ALL financial losses are borne by the pool (Rabbul-mal) - but in that case, the bank also earns NO service-based amount; pool-attributable losses are distributed among pool members per their investment ratio (Musharakah-style). The bank must cover its OWN expenses from its own profit share within the Mudarabah arrangement; weightages can only be amended at the start of an accounting period; the bank can reduce/withdraw its OWN profit-sharing ratio in the pool's favor (never increase it unilaterally); the bank may also invest in the pool as a depositor itself. Accounts should be linked to pools containing over 50% Ijarah/fixed assets to allow premature withdrawal (Hanafi view relaxes this to 10% nonliquid assets). Profit allocation fairness requires disclosure of accounting policies, weightage bases, expense charges, and the profit-allocation formula between owners' equity and unrestricted investment account holders. Assets Side (12.7.2): Shirkah (Mudarabah, Musharakah, or Shirkah-based certificates/Sukuk) is considered the best interest alternative for bank financing. Mudarabah suits import trade financing (single-transaction/consignment basis, firm order + L/C without margin, full bank investment), running businesses, project financing, and securitization purposes. Musharakah suits trade finance WITHOUT excessive complexity, since fraud/negligence/other-problem risks are relatively LOWER in international trade than other Musharakah-based projects - a bank can enter a Musharakah with an importing client (possibly appointing him agent for acquisition/disposal), sharing net profit per an agreed ratio; a similar structure applies to inland L/C bills. For export finance under L/C, goods are acquired/shipped-ready on a Musharakah basis, with the client preparing documents per L/C terms and indemnifying the bank for any failure to honor commitments; export proceeds are distributed per the agreed ratio. Banks can build a right into Musharakah agreements (with Shari'ah-expert approval) to convert outstanding investment into ordinary company shares on an agreed formula, require bank clearance before client dividend declarations, and establish review/resolution mechanisms for contract-breach disputes - requiring robust bank monitoring machinery for project feasibility appraisal and ongoing implementation oversight. For project financing: Mudarabah suits full-financier-funded projects; Musharakah suits both-sides-invest scenarios; a Musharakah-Mudarabah combination suits cases where management is one party's sole responsibility. Shirkah also enables financing via Musharakah/Mudarabah certificates like Shirkah-based Sukuk, term finance certificates (TFCs), or participation term certificates (PTCs) - negotiable instruments issued for funds/accommodation received, whether in cash or kind, or against a promise/guarantee/undertaking/indemnity for the issuer's benefit; MORAL HAZARD is generally LOWER for TFCs/PTCs than for direct Musharakah investments. Banks flag several Shirkah-financing risks: managing partners may manipulate financial reporting or withhold actual profit disclosure (return risk); guarantees/collateral can only safeguard against negligence/misconduct (not general business risk); partners may misallocate indirect/personal expenses; guarantors' legal capacity/financial stability must be independent of the Musharakah contract; and Diminishing Musharakah buying-partner banks face unit-price fluctuation exposure if not fixed upfront. The AUTHOR attributes Musharakah's limited widespread use mainly to lack of documentation, defective taxation systems, and insufficient banker effort/initiative - arguing Islamic banks have favored the 'easier, less risky' alternatives of Murabaha/leasing rather than genuinely researching and realizing Musharakah's potential, particularly for trade and project financing. Securitization on a Shirkah Basis (12.7.3): a 'security' is a paper asset whose cash flow is backed by a pool of liquid/tangible assets; securitization makes nonliquid joint-business assets negotiable via share/certificate sales in the financial market. Musharakah readily suits securitization, especially for BIG projects needing huge sums - subscribers get certificates representing proportionate ownership, which (once the project holds substantial nonliquid assets) become NEGOTIABLE INSTRUMENTS tradable in the secondary market. Musharakah and Mudarabah Sukuk can be issued as redeemable certificates for corporate-sector purposes or to individuals for rehabilitation/employment, automobile purchase, or establishing clinics/hospitals/factories/trading centers/endowments.","examExplanation":"Deposits side: pooled deposits, bank = Mudarib (or Musharakah partner if bank contributes equity AT pool creation, vs Mudarabah if subsequently); weightage by tenor/size; overall relationship remains Mudarib/Rabbul-mal since depositors are effectively sleeping partners (no management rights, no right to overall business appreciation). Losses borne by the pool per Mudarabah rules (bank then earns NO service fee); pool-attributable losses distributed per investment ratio (Musharakah-style). Assets side: Mudarabah suits import trade/running business/project financing/securitization; Musharakah suits trade finance (lower fraud/negligence risk in int'l trade), project financing, and can convert to company shares via agreed formula. Shirkah-based Sukuk/TFCs/PTCs enable securitization with LOWER moral hazard than direct Musharakah investment. Author's diagnosis: Musharakah underused due to documentation gaps, tax system defects, and banker inertia favoring 'easier' Murabaha/leasing - not any inherent Shari'ah limitation.","keyPoints":["On the deposits side, the relationship is Musharakah if the bank contributes its own equity to the pool at pool creation, but Mudarabah if contributed subsequently - though the OVERALL relationship remains Mudarib/Rabbul-mal, since depositors function as sleeping partners with no management rights or right to overall business appreciation.","Per Mudarabah rules, financial losses are borne by the pool (Rabbul-mal) - and when this happens, the bank correspondingly earns NO service-based amount for that period; pool-attributable losses are then distributed among pool members per their investment ratio.","On the assets side, Musharakah particularly suits trade finance (fraud/negligence risk is relatively lower in international trade than other Musharakah-based projects), while Mudarabah suits import financing, running businesses, and project/securitization financing.","Shirkah-based Sukuk, TFCs, and PTCs (negotiable certificates) generally carry LOWER moral hazard than direct Musharakah investments, making them an attractive securitization tool.","The author attributes Musharakah's limited real-world use mainly to lack of documentation, defective taxation systems, and insufficient banker effort - not to any inherent Shari'ah limitation - arguing banks have favored the easier, lower-risk alternatives of Murabaha and leasing over genuinely developing Musharakah's potential."],"definitions":[{"term":"Term Finance Certificate (TFC)","definition":"A negotiable instrument issued on the principle of Shirkah, representing a claim on the issuer's operating profit/loss, tradable in the securities market."},{"term":"Participation Term Certificate (PTC)","definition":"A negotiable instrument, similar to a TFC, issued on Shirkah principles as a fund-raising mechanism with generally lower moral hazard than direct Musharakah investment."}],"conditions":["Accounts within a deposit pool should be linked to pools containing over 50% Ijarah/fixed assets to allow premature withdrawal (the Hanafi view relaxes this to a 10% nonliquid-asset threshold).","Weightages assigned to deposit pool categories can only be amended at the start of an accounting period."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Sitara Chemical Industries TFCs (Box 12.3, summarized)","kind":"textbook","text":"Sitara Chemical Industries (Pakistan) issued Rs. 360 million of 5-year Shirkah-based term finance certificates in June 2002, funding part of an expansion project. Profit/loss was linked to the company's operating profit via a two-tier structure (Level I and Level II profit rates), paid semi-annually, with a Takaful reserve absorbing losses attributable to TFC holders before any shortfall was adjusted against principal redemption."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume Islamic bank depositors are genuine Musharakah partners with full management rights. The chapter clarifies they are effectively sleeping partners in a Mudarabah-style relationship overall, even when the bank's own equity contribution technically creates a Musharakah relationship within the pool."],"distinctions":["Bank equity contributed to the pool AT pool creation (creates a Musharakah relationship) vs contributed SUBSEQUENTLY (creates a Mudarabah relationship, with the bank as just another investor)."],"relatedConcepts":["Mudarabah","Musharakah","Sukuk"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7-12.7.3","pages":[330,331,332,333,334]},"calculations":[{"title":"Practice Example — generated for learning: Box 12.3's Two-Tier TFC Profit Mechanism Applied to a Year's Operating Profit","formula":"Level I profit = 12% p.a. x outstanding principal, applied to the first Rs. 100 million of operating profit (pro-rated if operating profit is under Rs. 100 million). Level II profit = 2% p.a. x outstanding principal, applied to each subsequent Rs. 100 million slab of operating profit; one-quarter of Level II profit is transferred to the Takaful reserve and the remaining three-quarters go to TFC holders.","inputs":[{"label":"Outstanding TFC principal (full issue size)","value":"Rs. 360,000,000"},{"label":"Level I rate (on first Rs. 100 million operating profit)","value":"12% p.a. of outstanding principal"},{"label":"Level II rate (on each subsequent Rs. 100 million operating profit)","value":"2% p.a. of outstanding principal"},{"label":"Share of Level II profit kept in the Takaful reserve","value":"1/4 (remaining 3/4 goes to TFC holders)"},{"label":"TFC face value per certificate","value":"Rs. 5,000 (so Rs. 360,000,000 / 5,000 = 72,000 certificates)"},{"label":"Assumed operating profit for the year (illustrative, not stated in the book)","value":"Rs. 300,000,000"}],"steps":["Level I: the first Rs. 100 million of operating profit fully clears the Level I tier, entitling TFC holders to 12% p.a. of the outstanding principal: 12% x Rs. 360,000,000 = Rs. 43,200,000 for the year.","Operating profit above Rs. 100 million = Rs. 300,000,000 - Rs. 100,000,000 = Rs. 200,000,000, which exactly fills two full Rs. 100 million Level II slabs (Rs.100-200m and Rs.200-300m).","Each full Level II slab earns 2% p.a. of principal: 2% x Rs. 360,000,000 = Rs. 7,200,000 per slab; two slabs = Rs. 14,400,000 total Level II profit for the year.","One-quarter of the Rs. 14,400,000 Level II profit is transferred to the Takaful reserve: Rs. 14,400,000 / 4 = Rs. 3,600,000; the remaining three-quarters go to TFC holders: Rs. 14,400,000 x 3/4 = Rs. 10,800,000.","Total annual profit entitlement to TFC holders = Level I (Rs. 43,200,000) + Level II holders' share (Rs. 10,800,000) = Rs. 54,000,000.","As a percentage of the Rs. 360,000,000 principal: Rs. 54,000,000 / Rs. 360,000,000 = 15% p.a. - and per certificate, Rs. 54,000,000 / 72,000 certificates = Rs. 750 per certificate per year, i.e. 15% of the Rs. 5,000 face value.","Since profit is paid six-monthly, each semi-annual on-account payment would be roughly half of the annual figures: about Rs. 27,000,000 to all TFC holders (Rs. 375 per certificate), subject to final adjustment once audited annual accounts are available."],"result":"On an assumed Rs. 300 million operating profit, TFC holders earn Rs. 54,000,000 for the year (Rs. 750 per Rs. 5,000 certificate, a 15% return), while Rs. 3,600,000 is set aside in the Takaful reserve to absorb any future loss attributable to TFC holders.","interpretation":"This reproduces Box 12.3's real profit-sharing mechanism and outstanding principal exactly, using an assumed (not book-stated) operating profit to make the two-tier calculation concrete. Note that a 15% p.a. return is precisely the lower bound of the 15-24% per annum range the book reports Sitara's TFCs actually achieved historically - illustrating why the certificates 'remained highly profitable' and holders preferred to hold rather than sell them. The Takaful reserve mechanic means TFC holders' downside in a loss year is cushioned before any shortfall is adjusted against principal redemption.","generated":true,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.3 (Box 12.3)","pages":[335,336,337]}}]}]},{"id":"t12.7.1","section":"12.7.1","title":"Use of Shirkah on the Deposits Side","pages":[331,332],"tier":"core","concepts":["mudarabah","deposit-pool"],"intuition":"Why are bank depositors treated as Rabbul-mal rather than partners?","simpleExplanation":"Depositors are financiers and the bank is Mudarib. Deposits are pooled; direct expenses are charged to the pool and head-office expenses borne by the bank; net profit is shared by stipulated ratios and disclosed weightages (longer tenor → higher weightage). The bank's own equity makes it a Musharakah partner if added at the start, or an investor like other depositors if later. Losses fall on the pool by investment ratio and the bank then earns nothing for its services.","academicExplanation":["Whatever is permissible for an individual is permissible for a group, so Shirkah has become a group activity.","Constructive liquidation occurs at the end of each accounting period and the relationship starts afresh.","The overall relationship remains Mudarib–Rabbul-mal: depositors do not share appreciation of the whole business and, like sleeping partners, have no management rights; clearer still in special/portfolio investment accounts.","Preferential weightages for pensioners and welfare bodies are acceptable, but routinely favouring larger deposits is not good practice in view of justice.","The bank covers its expenses from its own share; weightages can only change at the start of an accounting period; the bank may waive part of its share in favour of the pool.","Accounts should be linked to pools with more than 50% Ijarah or fixed assets to permit premature withdrawal; under the Hanafi view, over 10% nonliquid suffices.","Disclosure of accounting policies, weightages, expenses charged and the basis of allocation between owners' equity and unrestricted investment account holders is essential for fairness and trust."],"examExplanation":"Depositors = Rabbul-mal, bank = Mudarib; pool; direct expenses to pool, HQ expenses to bank; weightages disclosed (tenor-based); bank equity at start = Musharakah; loss borne by pool by investment ratio; disclosure essential.","keyPoints":["Weightages change only at start of a period.","Loss → bank earns nothing as Mudarib.","Bank equity in the pool: Musharakah if added at the pool’s creation, Mudarabah (the bank as an ordinary investor) if added later (p. 331).","Weightages can be changed only at the start of an accounting period (a month or a quarter) (p. 331).","Early withdrawal is allowed if the pool holds more than 50 % Ijarah or other fixed assets, or more than 10 % on the Hanafi view (p. 332)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Preferential weightage","kind":"textbook","text":"Banks may give higher weightages to longer-tenor deposits, and preferential weightages to pensioners or welfare associations. Giving bigger deposits more weight as a matter of course is not good practice on grounds of justice (p. 331)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A bank may charge its Mudarib fee even when the pool suffers a loss.","right":"If the pool makes a loss, the bank earns nothing for its services. The loss is shared among pool members by their investment ratio (p. 331)."},{"wrong":"Head-office and general management expenses are charged to the depositors’ pool.","right":"Only direct expenses go to the pool. General and head-office expenses are the bank’s, paid from its share of profit (p. 331)."}],"relatedTopics":["t8.5.2","t14.3.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Who bears head-office expenses in an Islamic bank's Mudarabah deposit pool?","options":["The pool","The bank as Mudarib","Depositors pro rata","The central bank"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 331."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t12.7.2","section":"12.7.2","title":"Use of Shirkah on the Assets Side","pages":[332,334],"tier":"core","concepts":["musharakah","mudarabah","running-musharakah"],"intuition":"Why do banks prefer Murabaha even for trade that could be financed by Musharakah?","simpleExplanation":"Shirkah is considered the best alternative to interest. Mudarabah suits import consignments financed wholly by the bank; Musharakah suits import/export trade, project finance and running businesses. In running Musharakah, the client's operating sale proceeds pass through a Musharakah account and the bank's share of operating profit is paid periodically. The author holds that banks avoid Musharakah mainly because Murabaha and leasing are easier and less risky.","academicExplanation":["Import Musharakah: the client may be appointed agent to acquire and dispose of goods; L/C in bank's or client's name; net profit shared by ratio. Export: goods prepared under joint ownership; the client indemnifies the bank if he fails to honour L/C terms; proceeds shared.","Export steps: the exporter gets an order at a known price, estimates profit, obtains Shirkah finance; profit shared at a pre-agreed percentage; the bank is protected against negligence but bears other losses.","Musharakah agreements may allow the bank to convert outstanding investment into ordinary shares, require clearance before dividends, and provide adjudication or review committees; banks need stronger appraisal and monitoring.","Project finance: Mudarabah if the financier funds all; Musharakah if both invest; combination if one party manages.","Running Musharakah flow: (1) running Musharakah account opened; (2) sale proceeds credited; (3) investing and long-term financing cash flows excluded; (4) clients categorised as seasonal, cyclical or continuous; (5) profit paid monthly/quarterly; (6) sharing based on computed operating profit for the limit period.","Certificates (TFCs, PTCs) reduce moral hazard compared with direct Musharakah.","Risks cited by banks: manipulated reporting, non-disclosure of profit, collateral only for negligence, allocation of personal expenses, guarantor independence, and unit-price fluctuation in DM.","Obstacles: lack of documentation, defective taxation, and lack of banker effort; in the author's view Musharakah could have been safely applied at least to trade and project finance."],"examExplanation":"Assets side: Mudarabah (import consignments, projects, securitisation), Musharakah (trade, project, running business, certificates). Running Musharakah six steps. Risks: misreporting, moral hazard. Obstacles: documentation, tax, banker effort (author's view).","keyPoints":["Running Musharakah excludes investing and financing cash flows.","Collateral only against negligence/misconduct.","Author's criticism of bankers' preference for Murabaha."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Running Musharakah","kind":"textbook","text":"The bank opens a Running Musharakah Account for the client. Proceeds from selling finished goods are credited to it, but not proceeds from selling fixed assets or long-term loans. Each month or quarter the bank receives its share of the computed operating profit, its capital measured by the average amount utilised (pp. 314, 333)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t14.4.1","t14.4.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which cash flows cannot be credited to a Running Musharakah account?","options":["Sale proceeds of finished goods","Proceeds from disposal of fixed assets and long-term finance","Operating receipts","Customer payments"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 333."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"processSteps":["Open a running Musharakah account for the client","Credit the client's sale proceeds of finished goods","Exclude investing and long-term financing cash flows","Classify the client: seasonal, cyclical or continuous","Pay the bank's profit share monthly/quarterly","Base sharing on computed operating profit for the limit period"]},{"id":"t12.7.3","section":"12.7.3","title":"Securitisation on a Shirkah Basis (Boxes 12.2–12.3)","pages":[334,337],"tier":"core","concepts":["securitization","musharakah","sukuk"],"intuition":"How can a Musharakah be split into tradable certificates?","simpleExplanation":"Securitisation makes nonliquid joint-business assets negotiable by selling certificates. Each subscriber gets a certificate of proportionate ownership; once the project has acquired substantial nonliquid assets, the certificates can trade. Box 12.2 shows a Shirkatul Wujooh import finance for equipment; Box 12.3 describes Sitara Chemical's Musharakah-based TFCs with tiered profit and a Takaful reserve.","academicExplanation":["A security is a paper asset whose cash flow is backed by a pool of liquid and tangible assets.","Musharakah and Mudarabah Sukuk may be redeemable certificates for corporates or individuals (vehicles, clinics, hospitals, factories, trading centres, endowments) — Chapter 15.","Box 12.2 (import case): ABC & Co, supplying equipment to a utility, uses Shirkatul Wujooh with an Islamic bank — no investment by partners; goods bought on credit and sold at spot. Steps: Usance L/C Rs.10 million in favour of XYZ Machines, Italy; 180-day credit; air shipment; ABC inspects and bank accepts documents; 30 days customs; 50 days installation; utility tests; satisfaction certificate and bill; payment within 150 days of shipment; profit shared; L/C settled at maturity.","Box 12.3 (Sitara TFCs, June 2002): 5-year TFCs worth Rs.360 million, tradable, for an expansion project; profit/loss linked to operating profit; investors bear operating loss pro rata plus regulatory and liquidity risk. Level I: on the first Rs.100 million operating profit, 12% p.a. of outstanding principal, reduced proportionately if lower (Actual OP / 100m × 12%). Level II: 2% p.a. on each further Rs.100 million, one quarter to a Takaful reserve. Losses offset first against the Takaful reserve, then against principal at redemption. Face value Rs.5,000; redemption Rs.1,650, Rs.1,650 and Rs.1,700 at the end of years 3, 4 and 5. The TFCs paid 15–24% p.a. and holders kept them."],"examExplanation":"Shirkah securitisation: certificates of pro rata ownership; tradable once nonliquid assets acquired. Box 12.2: Wujooh import (Rs.10m usance L/C, 180-day credit, payment in 150 days). Box 12.3: Sitara TFCs Rs.360m; Level I 12% on first Rs.100m OP; Level II 2% per extra Rs.100m, ¼ to Takaful reserve.","keyPoints":["Wujooh requires no capital from partners.","Sitara losses: Takaful reserve first, then principal."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Sitara Level I","kind":"textbook","text":"If operating profit is Rs.80 million, Level I entitlement is 80/100 × 12% = 9.6% p.a. of outstanding principal (formula from Box 12.3)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t15.3.5","t12.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Box 12.3, what share of Level II profit went to the Takaful reserve?","options":["One half","One quarter","All of it","None"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 336."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"calc":{"type":"sitara-tfc","note":"Reproduces Box 12.3's tiered formula: enter annual operating profit and outstanding principal; shows Level I rate (OP/100m × 12%, capped at 12%), Level II rate (2% per extra Rs.100m) and the quarter of Level II transferred to the Takaful reserve."}},{"id":"t12.8","section":"12.8","title":"Diminishing Musharakah (Concept)","pages":[337,339],"tier":"core","concepts":["diminishing-musharakah","musha"],"intuition":"How can a financier exit a partnership gradually without guaranteeing his own capital?","simpleExplanation":"In Diminishing Musharakah (DM) one partner progressively sells his ownership units to the other. It rests on Musha‘a — undivided joint ownership — which may be leased to a co-owner. It combines two or three separate contracts: partnership, lease of the financier's share (for usable assets) and sale of units. These must not be conditional on each other. The key difference: in DM through Shirkatul‘aqd (trade), units cannot be bought at a pre-agreed price; in DM through Shirkatulmilk (e.g. housing), a pre-agreed price schedule is permissible.","academicExplanation":["Jurists are unanimous on permissibility; a promise to sell units periodically is enforceable, especially in commerce.","DM is a contemporary contract devised by jurists; Musha‘a means each co-owner owns every part pro rata; leasing Musha‘a to a co-owner is allowed.","Leasing an undivided share to a third party: Malik, Shafi‘i and Abu Yusuf allow; Abu Hanifa and Zufar disallow — so IFIs lease to co-partners only, which is agreed.","Three separate, independently enforceable agreements (partnership, lease, sale) are compliant; stipulating them collectively is not (Chapter 5); even Hanbalis reject a contract dependent on another.","Shirkatul‘aqd DM: profit ratio agreed (may differ from equity), changeable as equity changes; loss by equity at the time; no lump sum. Units bought at market value or price agreed at time of sale; pre-agreed/original/fair value not allowed (capital guarantee).","Shirkatulmilk DM: no profit ratio needed; each bears risk and reward pro rata; the financier leases its part to the co-owner, rent falls as units are bought; ownership costs pro rata; a pre-agreed unit price is permissible — IFIs' housing DM uses this.","For trade DM the financier must sell at valuation at each sale (or let the client sell elsewhere with a fixed offer), which does not reduce equity well — so DM is feasible mainly for fixed/leasable assets."],"examExplanation":"DM = partnership + (lease) + periodic sale of units; separate contracts; Musha‘a leased to co-owner. Shirkatul‘aqd DM: price at market/at-time value only. Shirkatulmilk DM: pre-agreed price schedule allowed. Feasible mainly for leasable fixed assets.","keyPoints":["Crucial difference: pre-agreed price allowed only in Shirkatulmilk DM.","Lease/sale to co-partners avoids the third-party dispute.","In contractual DM, profit ratios may differ from equity and may change as equity changes. Loss follows equity at the time it occurs, and no lump-sum profit is allowed (p. 338).","In ownership DM no profit ratio is needed: each owner takes risk and reward by his share, and the lessee partner pays rent only on the financier’s part (p. 338)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Diminishing Musharakah","definition":"Partnership in which one partner's share is gradually purchased by the other through periodic unit sales, often combined with lease of the financier's share."},{"term":"Musha‘a","definition":"Undivided joint ownership in which each co-owner owns every part of the property pro rata."}],"examples":[{"title":"Leasing an undivided share","kind":"textbook","text":"A bank owns 80 % of a house in common with the client. It can lease its undivided share (Musha‘a) to the client, a co-owner, which all jurists accept. Leasing it to a stranger is disputed: Malik, Shafi‘i and Abu Yusuf allow it, while Abu Hanifa and Zufar do not (pp. 337–338)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The partnership, lease and sale in DM may be stipulated as one package.","right":"They must be separate, independently enforceable contracts, none conditional on another. Even the Hanbalis reject a contract whose enforcement depends on another (p. 338)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.9","t12.2.1","t5.5.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In which form of Diminishing Musharakah may the client promise to buy the bank's units at a pre-agreed price?","options":["Shirkatul‘aqd (trade)","Shirkatulmilk (joint ownership, e.g. housing)","Neither","Both"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 338."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t16"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t16","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.8","sectionTitle":"Diminishing Musharakah","title":"Diminishing Musharakah: Concept and the Three Independent Subcontracts","covers":["t12.8"],"overview":"Introduces Diminishing Musharakah's core concept - a partner progressively buying out another's ownership share via a promise-to-sell mechanism - and the critical rule that its constituent subcontracts (partnership, lease, sale) must remain legally independent.","simpleExplanation":"Diminishing Musharakah lets a bank and a customer jointly own something (like a house), with the customer gradually buying out the bank's share over time until they own it outright. This relies on 'Musha'a' - the idea that co-owners each own a proportional slice of EVERY part of the shared property, not specific separate pieces. Depending on whether the asset can be leased (like a house) or not (like a trading business), the structure uses either three separate contracts (partnership + lease + sale) or two (partnership + sale). The crucial rule: these contracts must be entered separately and kept completely independent of each other - you can't bundle them into one single deal, and one partner generally can't promise upfront to buy the other's share at some FIXED pre-agreed price (since that would secretly guarantee the other partner's capital).","academicExplanation":"Diminishing Musharakah (DM) suits financing fixed assets and ongoing projects, particularly for financial intermediaries: a party, after participating in ownership of a business/project, can gradually LIQUIDATE his investment from the asset/ongoing business. Jurists are UNANIMOUS on this arrangement's permissibility. DM contracts contain a sale provision under which one partner promises to sell his ownership portion to the other PERIODICALLY - since promises are enforceable (a court can compel a promisor to fulfil his promise, especially in commercial contexts), this structure is workable. DM as a financing technique is a NEW contract type, suggested by contemporary jurists in response to perceived limitations of traditional Musharakah/Mudarabah in the broader economic context. It relies on the concept of Musha'a - UNDIVIDED ownership, where all co-owners own EVERY part of the joint property pro rata (no partner can claim a specific separable portion for himself, leaving other portions for other partners); it is permissible to LEASE Musha'a to another joint owner. A DM arrangement comprises either TWO or THREE subcontracts, depending on whether the asset can be leased: for LEASABLE assets (rendering some service) - THREE subcontracts: (1) partnership by ownership between two or more persons, (2) one partner LEASING its share in the asset to the other partner(s), (3) one partner SELLING its share to the other partner(s) over time; for TRADE assets (not leasable) - TWO subcontracts: partnership and sale, which effectively becomes a general partnership governed by all Shirkah al 'Inan rules. CRITICALLY: one partner generally CANNOT sell his portion to the other at a PRE-AGREED price in this type of DM arrangement. All two or three subcontracts are considered PERMISSIBLE by jurists, particularly when the sale/lease contracts are stipulated AMONG the partners (i.e. assets sold/leased to co-partners) - the combination of Shirkah and lease contracts creates NO Shari'ah problem, but the sale of a part by one partner to the other must be SEPARATE AND INDEPENDENT from the underlying Shirkah or leasing arrangement. There is scholarly disagreement on leasing an UNDIVIDED SHARE to a THIRD PARTY (not a co-partner): Imams Malik, Shafi'e, and Abu Yusuf allow it; Imams Abu Hanifa and Zufur disallow it. Current IFI practice sidesteps this dispute entirely by making the lease/sale ONLY to co-partners, on which there is no scholarly disagreement. Contemporary jurist opinion concludes: any arrangement where three separate agreements (partnership, leasing, sale) are made such that they are NOT conditional upon one another and are SEPARATELY enforceable is Shari'ah-compliant; but if the three contracts are STIPULATED COLLECTIVELY (bundled as one interdependent package), this is NOT approved by jurists (per Chapter 5's discussion) - even Hanbali jurists, who permit some conditions within a sale contract, do not approve a contract whose enforcement DEPENDS on another contract. The parties' relationship shifts across stages: partners and lessor/lessee in the first instance, seller and buyer at the LATER stage - the sale must be kept independent from the partnership-plus-leasing part. DM can be conducted through EITHER partnership in ownership (Shirkatulmilk) OR contractual partnership (Shirkatul'aqd), but crucial differences govern Shari'ah compliance between the two: (1) In DM via Shirkatul'aqd (contractual partnership): the profit distribution ratio (possibly disproportionate to equity ratio) must be clearly determined, changeable by mutual agreement if equity ratios change, with LOSS necessarily allocated per the equity ratio AT THE TIME the loss occurs - no lump sum for any partner. In DM via Shirkatulmilk (ownership partnership): since the major objective isn't profit-earning through business, the profit-distribution ratio need not even be stipulated - each partner simply owns risk/reward proportionate to his share; a leasing partner can charge rental on the leased portion, with the lessee-owner-partner benefiting from using the asset without paying rent on his OWN owned portion; as the lessee partner buys more of the financier's share, rental correspondingly decreases; both parties bear ownership-related expenses/liabilities and sale-related losses pro rata. (2) In DM via Shirkatul'aqd (contractual partnership): the lessee partner can only PROMISE to buy the financier partner's share periodically at MARKET VALUE or a price AGREED AT THE TIME of sale - stipulating a PRE-AGREED FIXED price or the original/fair value at contract formation is NOT permitted, as this would constitute a prohibited GUARANTEE of one partner's share capital by the other. In DM via Shirkatulmilk (ownership partnership): ONE PARTNER CAN purchase the co-partner's ownership units AT A PRE-AGREED PRICE - this crucial difference explains why IFIs providing housing finance based on DM in Shirkatulmilk generally DO take a client promise to purchase bank units at pre-agreed prices, and this IS permissible under Shari'ah rules (unlike the Shirkatul'aqd/trade case).","examExplanation":"DM = progressive buyout structure; relies on Musha'a (undivided pro rata co-ownership). 2 or 3 subcontracts: LEASABLE assets (e.g. house) -> 3 subcontracts (partnership + lease + sale); TRADE/non-leasable assets -> 2 subcontracts (partnership + sale). CRITICAL RULE: the three (or two) subcontracts must be SEPARATE, INDEPENDENT, and NOT conditional on each other - bundling them collectively is NOT approved. Lease/sale to co-partners = universally accepted; lease of undivided share to a THIRD PARTY (non-co-partner) = disputed (Malik/Shafi'e/Abu Yusuf allow, Abu Hanifa/Zufur disallow) - IFI practice sidesteps this by only leasing/selling to co-partners. KEY DIFFERENCE by partnership type: DM via Shirkatul'aqd (trade/profit-motivated) -> sale price CANNOT be pre-fixed (only market value/price-at-sale-time; pre-fixed = prohibited capital guarantee); DM via Shirkatulmilk (ownership, e.g. housing) -> price CAN be pre-fixed and IS how IFIs structure housing finance DM (permissible).","keyPoints":["Diminishing Musharakah (DM) relies on Musha'a - undivided ownership, where each co-owner holds a pro rata share of EVERY part of the joint property, with unanimous jurist permissibility for this arrangement.","A DM arrangement has THREE subcontracts for leasable assets (partnership, lease, sale) or TWO for non-leasable trade assets (partnership, sale) - and these subcontracts must be entered SEPARATELY, remain legally INDEPENDENT, and not be conditional on one another.","Bundling the partnership, lease, and sale into one collectively-stipulated, interdependent package is NOT approved by jurists - even Hanbali jurists, who permit some sale-contract conditions, reject a contract whose enforcement depends on another contract.","Current IFI practice structures lease and sale ONLY between co-partners (universally accepted), sidestepping the disputed question of leasing an undivided share to a genuine third party (allowed by Malik/Shafi'e/Abu Yusuf, disallowed by Abu Hanifa/Zufur).","The CRITICAL Shari'ah-compliance difference between DM types: in DM via Shirkatul'aqd (contractual, profit-motivated), the sale price CANNOT be pre-fixed (only market value or a price agreed at the actual sale time is allowed - a pre-fixed price would constitute a prohibited capital guarantee); in DM via Shirkatulmilk (ownership-based, e.g. housing), ONE PARTNER CAN purchase the other's units at a PRE-AGREED price - which is exactly how IFIs structure most housing finance DM."],"definitions":[{"term":"Diminishing Musharakah (DM)","definition":"A financing arrangement where a partner, after participating in ownership of a business/project, progressively liquidates (sells) his investment/ownership share to the other partner, gradually 'diminishing' his stake until fully exited."},{"term":"Musha'a","definition":"Undivided ownership - the concept underlying Diminishing Musharakah, where all co-owners own every part of the joint property on a pro rata basis, with no partner able to claim a specific separable portion for himself."}],"conditions":["The subcontracts within a Diminishing Musharakah arrangement (partnership, lease, sale) must be entered separately and remain legally independent of, and not conditional upon, one another.","In DM via Shirkatul'aqd (contractual partnership), the sale price for buying out a partner's share cannot be pre-fixed - it must be at market value or a price agreed at the actual time of sale.","In DM via Shirkatulmilk (ownership partnership), one partner CAN purchase the co-partner's ownership units at a pre-agreed price."],"principles":["Sale of a part by a partner to another partner in a Diminishing Musharakah arrangement must be kept separate and independent from the underlying partnership or leasing arrangement."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students very commonly apply the 'no pre-fixed price' rule universally to all Diminishing Musharakah. This is only true for DM via Shirkatul'aqd (contractual, trade-focused); in DM via Shirkatulmilk (ownership-based, e.g. most housing finance), a pre-agreed price IS permissible and is standard IFI practice."],"distinctions":["DM via Shirkatul'aqd (contractual partnership, profit-motivated, sale price CANNOT be pre-fixed) vs DM via Shirkatulmilk (ownership partnership, e.g. housing, sale price CAN be pre-fixed) - the most consequential distinction in Diminishing Musharakah."],"relatedConcepts":["Ijarah","Bai' al 'Inah","Shirkatulmilk"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.8","pages":[337,338,339]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"DM in Shirkatul‘aqd","b":"DM in Shirkatulmilk","text":"Contractual (trade) DM needs a profit ratio and forbids a pre-agreed unit price; ownership DM needs no profit ratio, uses rent on the financier's share, and allows a pre-agreed price schedule."},{"a":"Malik, Shafi‘i and Abu Yusuf","b":"Abu Hanifa and Zufar","text":"The first group allow an undivided share (Musha‘a) to be leased to a third party; the second do not. Leasing it to a co-owner is agreed by all, and that is how Islamic banks use DM (pp. 337–338)."}]},{"id":"t12.9","section":"12.9","title":"Diminishing Musharakah as an Islamic Mode of Finance","pages":[339,339],"tier":"core","concepts":["diminishing-musharakah"],"intuition":"How does DM finance a house step by step?","simpleExplanation":"DM can finance houses, autos, plant, machinery and buildings. For housing, joint ownership is created, the financier leases its undivided share to the client, and the client pays rent and periodically buys units, so rent falls. Three independent contracts in sequence: (1) joint ownership with the client's promise to purchase; (2) lease of the bank's units; (3) periodic purchase of units. Commercial assets may involve no lease.","academicExplanation":["The modus operandi approved by scholars requires each contract to be independent of the other two; the promise may be given before or after the lease agreement."],"examExplanation":"DM uses: house, auto, plant/machinery, building. Sequence: joint ownership + promise → lease of bank's share → periodic purchase; rent decreases.","keyPoints":["Rent is charged only on the bank's outstanding units.","DM suits house, auto, plant and machinery and factory or building finance. Commercial assets may be financed without leasing (p. 339).","The client’s promise to buy may be made before or after the lease agreement is finalised (p. 339)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Sequence of the three contracts","kind":"textbook","text":"First a joint-ownership agreement, with the client’s promise to buy units; then a lease of the bank’s share; then periodic purchase of units, each with its own offer and acceptance, the rent falling as units are bought (p. 339)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t12.9.2","t11.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"As the client buys the bank's units in housing DM, the rent:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Stays constant","Is paid to the client"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 339."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch12-t17"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch12-t17","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"12.9-12.9.2","sectionTitle":"Diminishing Musharakah as an Islamic Mode of Finance / Diminishing Musharakah in Trade / Procedure and Documentation in Diminishing Musharakah","title":"Diminishing Musharakah as a Financing Mode: Housing, Trade, and Documentation Procedure","covers":["t12.9","t12.9.1","t12.9.2"],"overview":"Walks through the full Diminishing Musharakah procedure used for housing finance, contrasts it with the trade-financing variant (where only the bank's promise to sell is binding), and details the documentation sequence and sale-and-lease-back rules for construction/renovation.","simpleExplanation":"For fixed-asset financing (like buying a house), the bank and client jointly own the asset; the client rents the bank's share and gradually buys it out unit by unit, with rent shrinking as the client's ownership grows. For a TRADE business (not a physical asset with rent), it works differently: only the BANK's promise to eventually sell its stake is binding - the client-entrepreneur is never forced to actually buy it, and any sale happens at a fair market valuation, not a fixed price. In practice, this all happens through a careful sequence of separate legal documents: first a partnership agreement, then a rental agreement, then a separate purchase undertaking - each one signed independently.","academicExplanation":"DM can easily finance fixed assets for Islamic banks: house financing, auto financing, plant/machinery financing, factory/building financing, and other fixed-asset financing. For HOUSING FINANCE specifically: joint ownership is created; the financier partner leases his undivided share to the using partner; the client pays rent on the financier's part and PERIODICALLY purchases units of the financier's ownership. The Shari'ah-approved modus operandi requires THREE SEPARATE contracts, each independent of the other two, in this SEQUENCE: (1) a contract between partners creating joint ownership - the client partner makes a promise (before or after the lease agreement is finalized) to purchase the financier partner's share; (2) the financing partner gives units of his share to the client ON LEASE; (3) the client partner progressively purchases the financing partner's ownership units per his promise, with RENT correspondingly decreasing. Financing forms VARY by asset type: SOME assets can be leased (house financing, plant/machinery purchase); assets of a COMMERCIAL nature typically don't involve leasing. Diminishing Musharakah in Trade (12.9.1): if leasing is NOT involved and it's a simple business partnership (e.g. 40:60 split), the partners can agree that one partner's share units will be PERIODICALLY SOLD to the other, who keeps buying until the first party exits entirely. Since this contract exists for PROFIT-EARNING and involves NO leasing (unlike house/vehicle DM), the share-unit PRICE CANNOT be fixed in the promise to sell - one partner may instead agree to sell based on BUSINESS VALUATION at each unit-purchase time, per recognized expert principles (with the expert's identity agreed when the promise is signed); at actual purchase time, the sale must be executed via proper offer and acceptance. Although the entrepreneur partner has an inherent motivation to acquire full ownership, Shari'ah experts are NOT inclined to make the PURCHASE binding on him. Per OIC Fiqh Academy Resolution No. 2 (Jeddah) and IRTI research, the sale provision can be made binding ONLY on the FINANCIER partner, with the sale effected at PREVAILING MARKET PRICES at the time of actual sale. After creating joint ownership, the bank may sign a ONE-SIDED promise to sell different ownership-share units periodically, undertaking that as the client purchases a unit, remaining-unit rental will decrease accordingly - the bank makes a BINDING promise to offer a specific ownership portion for sale on a specified future date, at a price DETERMINED AT ACTUAL SALE TIME; the entrepreneur partner may VOLUNTARILY buy the financier's share at prevailing stock market prices or a freely-consented price. Procedure and Documentation (12.9.2) for housing finance via Shirkatulmilk-based DM: (1) Joint ownership creation via a Musharakah agreement - customer and IFI become co-owners; if the customer ALREADY holds legal title, the agreement instead has the IFI acquiring a certain Musha'a share, involving a SALE-AND-LEASE-BACK arrangement. (2) Rent agreement - both parties agree the IFI will lease its undivided share to the client for stipulated rental, governed by Ijarah rules; this is signed AFTER the Musharakah agreement, detailing rent, calculation formula, and lease-period schedule. (3) Undertaking to purchase units of the bank's share - a UNILATERAL promise binding ONLY on the promisor (either the client or the bank can make it); for Shirkatulmilk arrangements, it may include a PRICE SCHEDULE for periodic unit purchase, provisions for accelerated purchase, and security/guarantee details (normally equitable mortgage of the financed property, plus possibly additional security given the client's financial position). The customer thus pays rent for using the bank's property share while progressively purchasing part of that share until full ownership transfers - usable for buying, building, or renovating a house, or replacing an existing interest-based housing loan (a 'balance transfer facility'), and other fixed assets can similarly be DM-financed. For construction on the customer's OWN land or renovation (Box 12.4): this involves a purchase/SALE-AND-LEASE-BACK structure - the bank purchases a PART of the customer's land/property (forming Shirkatulmilk joint ownership) using proceeds the customer then uses for construction/renovation; once complete/habitable, the bank leases its share to the client; crucially, the bank's units are NOT sold back to the client until a ONE-YEAR gap after the LAST disbursement instalment, specifically to avoid Bai' al 'Inah (prohibited buy-back) - during this initial no-sale period, the client pays ONLY rent (which does not decrease), and only after the one-year gap does the unit-selling (and corresponding rent decrease) begin. If the client regularly pays rental and periodically purchases the bank's share, ownership fully transfers over time; if he delays, rental simply doesn't decrease (the bank's income loss is far LESS severe than in Murabaha or even simple leasing, since the customer keeps paying rent on the bank-owned units regardless). The client CAN purchase multiple units at once if cash flow allows - in which case banks typically conduct a VALUATION and share any capital-gain appreciation (up to a pre-stipulated rate, e.g. 3-4%) with the client, giving the client the remainder - this built-in valuation-sharing mechanism provides risk mitigation for the bank. Rental rates can be fixed or floating, subject to a proper floor-and-cap to avoid Gharar (which could otherwise render the transaction non-Shari'ah-compliant).","examExplanation":"Housing DM procedure (3 sequential, independent contracts): (1) joint ownership + client's promise to purchase; (2) financier leases share to client (Ijarah rules); (3) client progressively buys financier's units per the promise, rent decreases correspondingly. Trade DM: NO leasing involved; price CANNOT be pre-fixed (business VALUATION at each purchase instead); ONLY the FINANCIER's promise to sell is BINDING (per OIC Fiqh Academy Resolution No. 2/IRTI) - the entrepreneur is NEVER obligated to buy, sale at PREVAILING MARKET price. Construction-on-own-land/renovation (Box 12.4): sale-and-lease-back structure; bank buys PART of customer's land/property; ONE-YEAR gap required after LAST disbursement before bank starts selling units back (avoids Bai' al 'Inah) - during that year, client pays ONLY rent (no decrease). Multi-unit purchases: bank shares capital-gain appreciation (up to a pre-stipulated cap %) with the client as a built-in risk-mitigation feature.","keyPoints":["Housing finance DM uses three sequential, independent contracts: (1) joint ownership + client's promise to purchase, (2) the financier leasing its share to the client under Ijarah rules, (3) the client progressively purchasing the financier's units, with rent decreasing correspondingly.","Trade-financing DM differs sharply from housing DM: since it involves no leasing and exists purely for profit-earning, the share-unit price CANNOT be pre-fixed (business valuation is used at each purchase instead), and per OIC Fiqh Academy Resolution No. 2, ONLY the financier's promise to sell is binding - the entrepreneur partner is never obligated to buy, and any sale occurs at prevailing market prices.","For construction on a customer's own land or house renovation, the structure involves sale-and-lease-back, with a mandatory ONE-YEAR gap after the last disbursement instalment before the bank begins selling its units back to the client, specifically to avoid Bai' al 'Inah.","If a client delays rental payment or unit purchase in housing DM, the bank's income loss is far less severe than under Murabaha or simple leasing, since the customer continues paying rent on the bank-owned units regardless.","If a client wants to purchase multiple units at once, banks typically conduct a valuation and share any capital-gain appreciation with the client (up to a pre-stipulated rate, e.g. 3-4%), giving the client the remainder - a built-in risk-mitigation mechanism."],"definitions":[{"term":"Balance Transfer Facility (BTF)","definition":"A Diminishing Musharakah arrangement used to replace an existing interest-based housing loan with a Shari'ah-compliant structure."}],"conditions":["In construction-on-own-land or renovation DM arrangements, the bank's ownership units cannot begin being sold back to the client until at least one year after the last disbursement instalment, to avoid prohibited Bai' al 'Inah.","In trade-financing DM, the sale provision can be made binding only on the financier partner, and the sale must be effected at prices prevailing in the market at the time of actual sale."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Box 12.4 - Construction on Customer-Owned Land","kind":"textbook","text":"A plot worth 1,000,000 Dirhams belongs to the customer, who needs 800,000 Dirhams from the bank. The bank purchases 8 of 10 land units (100,000 Dirhams each) to form Shirkatulmilk joint ownership. Using the 800,000 Dirhams (in four instalments), the customer constructs the house. Once complete and habitable, the bank leases its share to the client; for the first year, the client pays only rent (no unit purchases, no rent decrease). One year after the last instalment's disbursement, the bank begins selling its ownership units to the client per the pre-agreed schedule, with rent decreasing accordingly until full title transfers."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume the entrepreneur/client is always bound to eventually buy out the financier's share in Diminishing Musharakah. This is only true where the client himself has made a binding promise (as in most Shirkatulmilk housing DM); in trade-financing DM, only the FINANCIER's promise to sell is binding - the client entrepreneur retains a free choice."],"distinctions":["Housing/fixed-asset DM (client's promise to purchase can be binding, price can be pre-agreed, lease is involved) vs trade DM (only the financier's promise to sell is binding, price cannot be pre-fixed, no leasing is involved)."],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al 'Inah","Ijarah","Shirkatulmilk"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9-12.9.2","pages":[339,340,341,342,343]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Joint Ownership Created","description":"The bank and client form a Shirkatulmilk (ownership) partnership, each contributing a share of the asset's cost (e.g. the bank funds 80% of a house purchase, the client 20%); if the customer already holds legal title, the bank instead acquires a Musha'a share via a sale-and-lease-back arrangement."},{"step":"Client's Promise to Purchase","description":"The client (or, for trade DM, the bank) makes a unilateral promise, binding only on the promisor, to periodically purchase the co-owner's ownership units - for housing/fixed-asset DM (Shirkatulmilk), this promise can specify a pre-agreed price schedule; for trade DM (Shirkatul'aqd), only the financier's promise to sell is binding, and price cannot be pre-fixed."},{"step":"Lease of the Bank's Share","description":"The financing partner (bank) leases its undivided ownership share to the client under Ijarah rules, via a separate rent agreement signed after the Musharakah agreement, detailing rent, its calculation formula, and the lease schedule."},{"step":"Periodic Unit Purchase","description":"The client periodically purchases units of the bank's ownership share per the agreed schedule or promise; as the bank's ownership share shrinks, the rental charged on its remaining share decreases correspondingly."},{"step":"Full Ownership Transfer","description":"Once the client has purchased all of the bank's ownership units, full title to the asset transfers to the client, ending both the Musharakah (joint ownership) and the Ijarah (lease) relationship."}],"calculations":[{"title":"Box 12.5: Diminishing Musharakah Housing Finance — Monthly Unit-Purchase and Rent Schedule","formula":"Fixed monthly unit purchase = Bank's investment / Number of months. Monthly rent = (Outstanding bank investment x Annual rental rate) / 12. Total monthly payment = Unit purchase + Rent.","inputs":[{"label":"Cost of house","value":"Drs/Ryls 1,000,000"},{"label":"Bank financing","value":"80% = Ryls 800,000 (paid in one tranche)"},{"label":"Client's own share","value":"20% = Ryls 200,000"},{"label":"Tenure","value":"10 years = 120 months"},{"label":"Rental rate on bank's outstanding investment","value":"7% p.a."},{"label":"Number of bank's ownership units (= number of months)","value":"120"},{"label":"Price per unit","value":"Ryls 800,000 / 120 = Ryls 6,666.67 (constant every month)"}],"steps":["Fixed monthly unit-purchase amount = Ryls 800,000 / 120 months = Ryls 6,666.67 - this stays the same every month for the whole 10-year tenure.","Month 1 rent = outstanding bank investment (Ryls 800,000) x 7% / 12 = Ryls 4,666.67. Month 1 total payment = Ryls 6,666.67 (unit) + Ryls 4,666.67 (rent) = Ryls 11,333.33.","After the month-1 unit purchase, the bank's outstanding investment falls to Ryls 800,000 - 6,666.67 = Ryls 793,333.33, and its remaining units fall from 120 to 119.","Month 2 rent recalculates on the new, smaller outstanding investment: Ryls 793,333.33 x 7% / 12 = Ryls 4,627.78. Month 2 total = Ryls 6,666.67 + 4,627.78 = Ryls 11,294.44 - already lower than month 1's total.","This pattern repeats every month: the Ryls 6,666.67 unit-purchase amount never changes, but rent shrinks each month as the bank's outstanding investment (and remaining units) keep falling.","By month 116 (four months from the end), only 4 units (Ryls 26,666.7 outstanding investment) remain, so rent has fallen to Ryls 194.44 and the month's total payment is Ryls 6,861.11.","In month 120, the client buys the last unit: Ryls 6,666.67 (unit) + Ryls 38.89 (rent) = Ryls 6,705.56. Outstanding bank investment reaches zero, remaining bank units reach zero, and full title to the house transfers to the client."],"result":"The client's monthly payment falls steadily from Ryls 11,333.33 in month 1 to Ryls 6,705.56 in month 120, purely because the rent component shrinks from Ryls 4,666.67 to Ryls 38.89 as the bank's ownership units (and outstanding investment) run down from 120 to 0; the Ryls 6,666.67 unit-purchase component never changes.","interpretation":"This is the arithmetic behind the process step 'as the client purchases a unit, remaining-unit rental decreases correspondingly': rent is charged only on the bank's remaining outstanding investment (its unsold units), so buying back one unit each month shrinks both the bank's ownership share and its rentable base together. Because the unit-purchase amount is fixed while rent shrinks, the client's total monthly outlay declines over the facility's life - the opposite of a conventional amortizing loan, where the total instalment is typically held constant and only the interest/principal split moves within it.","generated":false,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2 (Box 12.5)","pages":[341,342,343]}}]}],"processSteps":["Create joint ownership (client promises to purchase bank's share)","Bank leases its units to the client","Client purchases units periodically; rent falls accordingly"]},{"id":"t12.9.1","section":"12.9.1","title":"Diminishing Musharakah in Trade","pages":[339,340],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["diminishing-musharakah"],"intuition":"Why can't the entrepreneur be bound to buy the bank out?","simpleExplanation":"Without a lease, two partners (e.g. 40:60) agree that one partner's units are sold periodically to the other. As this is for profit, the unit price cannot be fixed in the promise; it must be based on valuation (by experts agreed at the promise) at each purchase, with offer and acceptance at each sale. Per OIC Fiqh Academy Resolution No. 2 and IRTI research, the promise can be binding only on the financier, at market prices at the time of sale.","academicExplanation":["After joint ownership, the bank may give a one-sided binding promise to sell units periodically at prices determined at the time, and to reduce the rent of remaining units accordingly; the entrepreneur buys voluntarily at market price or a freely agreed price."],"examExplanation":"Trade DM: no fixed unit price; valuation at each purchase; sale by offer/acceptance; binding promise only on the financier (OIC Fiqh Academy Res. 2, IRTI).","keyPoints":["Client's purchase is voluntary in trade DM.","Each unit sale in trade DM is executed at the time by offer and acceptance (p. 340).","Because a fixed-price buy-back is not allowed, DM in trade is feasible mainly for assets that can be leased or given for use to the partner (p. 339)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"40:60 business partnership","kind":"textbook","text":"Two partners start a business 40:60 and agree that one will buy the other’s units over time until the other exits. Because it is for profit and involves no lease, the unit price cannot be fixed in the promise. It follows a valuation of the business by agreed experts when each unit is bought (pp. 339–340)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"In trade DM the entrepreneur can be bound to buy the financier’s units at a fixed price.","right":"OIC Fiqh Academy Resolution No. 2 and IRTI research make the sale binding only on the financier, at market prices when each sale actually takes place (p. 340)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.8","t12.3.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In trade DM (per OIC Fiqh Academy Res. 2), the binding promise is made by:","options":["The client to buy","The financier to sell, at market price at the time","Both parties at a fixed price","The central bank"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 340."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t12.9.2","section":"12.9.2","title":"Procedure, Documentation and Housing Cases (Boxes 12.4–12.7)","pages":[340,346],"tier":"core","concepts":["diminishing-musharakah","accounting"],"intuition":"What documents does an Islamic bank sign for a DM home loan, and how is each monthly payment built?","simpleExplanation":"Documentation: (1) Musharakah agreement creating joint ownership (a sale and lease-back if the client already holds title); (2) rent agreement under Ijarah rules; (3) a unilateral undertaking to purchase units — in Shirkatulmilk with a price schedule — plus security (usually equitable mortgage). Box 12.5: house 1.0 million, bank 80% (800,000), 120 monthly units of 6,666.67, 7% rent on outstanding investment → first month 6,666.67 + 4,666.67 = 11,333.33, last month 6,666.67 + 38.89.","academicExplanation":["Uses: buying, building or renovating a house and balance transfer from an interest-based mortgage; other fixed assets too.","Box 12.4 construction on client's land: land worth 1 million; bank buys 8 of 10 units (800,000) under Shirkatulmilk, paid in four instalments for construction; rent on the bank's share when the house is habitable; unit sales begin one year after the last instalment to avoid Bai‘ al ‘Inah. Renovation: the client sells, say, four units; rent starts from the first month after the first tranche (the client already lives there); unit sales start one year after the last tranche.","Box 12.5: monthly payment = constant unit price + rent on outstanding units; rent per unit (annual) 466.67; payments fall from 11,333.33 to 6,705.56 over 120 months.","If the client delays, rent does not fall, so the bank's income loss is smaller than in Murabaha or simple leasing. Extra unit purchases trigger a valuation, with the bank sharing appreciation up to about 3–4% on its part — a built-in risk mitigation. Rent may be fixed or floating with floor and cap to avoid Gharar.","Box 12.6 (Mudarabah accounting, financing side): recognise capital when paid or placed at the Mudarib's disposal; in-kind at fair value with the difference as profit/loss; losses before work starts reduce capital; full loss without negligence terminates it; profits recognised on liquidation or to the extent distributed; unpaid amounts are receivables.","Box 12.7 (Musharakah accounting): constant Musharakah at historical cost; DM at historical cost less share transferred, with the difference between cost and fair value on transfer as profit/loss; loss due to partner's negligence is a receivable."],"examExplanation":"DM documents: Musharakah agreement → rent agreement → unilateral purchase undertaking (price schedule in Shirkatulmilk) + security. Box 12.5 numbers: 800,000; 120 units × 6,666.67; 7% → first rent 4,666.67, first payment 11,333.33. One-year gap before unit sales in lease-back to avoid ‘Inah.","keyPoints":["Delayed payment → rent continues on units still owned by the bank.","Valuation on bulk purchase, bank shares appreciation up to ~3–4%.","Floating rent needs floor and cap.","A delaying client keeps paying rent on the bank’s units, so the bank loses less income than it would under Murabaha or simple leasing (p. 342).","Security is normally an equitable mortgage of the financed property (p. 340)."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Box 12.5 housing DM","kind":"textbook","text":"Bank share 800,000 in 120 units of 6,666.67; month 1 rent 800,000 × 7% / 12 = 4,666.67; total 11,333.33; month 120 rent 38.89; total 6,705.56 (pp. 341–342)."},{"title":"Box 12.4 construction","kind":"textbook","text":"Bank buys 8 of 10 land units (800,000), funds construction in four instalments, leases its share once habitable, and starts selling units one year after the last instalment (p. 341)."},{"title":"Box 12.4 — building on the client’s land","kind":"textbook","text":"Land worth 1,000,000: the bank buys 8 of 10 units (800,000) and pays in four instalments; the client builds. Rent starts when the house is habitable. Unit sales begin one year after the last instalment, a gap scholars suggest to avoid Bai‘ al ‘Inah (p. 341)."},{"title":"Box 12.4 — renovation","kind":"textbook","text":"The client sells, say, four units of his house to the bank and renovates with the proceeds. Rent starts in the first month because the client already lives there. Buy-back starts a year after the last tranche (p. 341)."},{"title":"Early purchase of units","kind":"textbook","text":"If the client buys, say, five units at once, the bank values the house and takes appreciation of only up to 3–4 % on its investment, leaving the rest to the client (pp. 342–343)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Floating rent in DM may move with a benchmark without limit.","right":"Floating rental must have a proper floor and cap to avoid Gharar (p. 343)."}],"relatedTopics":["t12.8","t11.5.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In Box 12.5, what is the rent component in month 1?","options":["6,666.67","4,666.67","11,333.33","466.67"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"800,000 × 7% ÷ 12 = 4,666.67 (p. 342)."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"processSteps":["Musharakah agreement — joint ownership (sale and lease-back if client holds title)","Rent agreement for the bank's undivided share (Ijarah rules)","Unilateral undertaking to purchase units (price schedule in Shirkatulmilk) and security","Monthly: rent on outstanding units + purchase of one unit","Title transfers after final unit"],"calc":{"type":"dm-schedule","note":"Reproduces Box 12.5: cost 1.0m, bank 80%, 120 months, 7% rent → first payment 11,333.33; change the inputs to generate a full schedule (variants are practice examples)."}}],"questions":[{"id":"q12.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which form of Shirkah is supported only by Hanafi jurists and is practically nonexistent?","options":["Shirkah al ‘Inan","Shirkah al-Mufawadah","Mudarabah","Shirkatulmilk"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 310.","topicId":"t12.2.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall kinds of Shirkah","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","pages":[309,312]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.2","type":"tf","prompt":"In Musharakah, partners may agree to share loss 50:50 even though they contributed capital 70:30.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Loss must follow capital ratio (p. 317).","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply loss rule","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.3","type":"scenario","prompt":"A contributes 40% of capital and works actively; B contributes 60% and has declared himself a sleeping partner. Under the Hanafi view, which profit split is NOT allowed?","options":["A 50% : B 50%","A 45% : B 55%","A 30% : B 70%","A 60% : B 40%"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"A sleeping partner cannot take more than his capital ratio (60%), so B at 70% is not allowed (p. 316).","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply sleeping-partner rule","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.4","type":"multi","prompt":"Which conditions apply to a third-party guarantee of Musharakah capital? (Select all.)","options":["Guarantor must not own more than 50% of the venture","The Shirkah must not be conditional on the guarantee","The guarantor must be paid a fee","The venture must not own more than half of the guarantor","It must be given by a co-partner"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3],"explanation":"p. 318.","topicId":"t12.3.5","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall guarantee conditions","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","pages":[318,318]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.5","type":"match","prompt":"Match each form of partnership to its subject matter.","pairs":[["Shirkatulamwal","Capital"],["Shirkatula‘mal","Labour/skills"],["Shirkatul Wujooh","Creditworthiness"],["Shirkatulmilk","Joint ownership"]],"explanation":"Section 12.2.","topicId":"t12.2.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Classify forms","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","pages":[309,312]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.6","type":"application","prompt":"Depositors provide 4,000 and the bank 2,000 to a Mudarabah pool; Mudarib ratio 50:50; profit 600. How much does the bank receive in total?","options":["200","300","400","500"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Bank's capital share = 2,000/6,000 × 600 = 200; remaining 400 split 50:50 → bank 200 as Mudarib. Total 400. (Practice variant of the pp. 323–324 example — generated for learning.)","topicId":"t12.4.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.4.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Compute mixed Mudarabah","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.1","pages":[323,324]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.7","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which statement correctly contrasts Musharakah and Mudarabah?","options":["In both, only the financier bears loss","In Musharakah assets are jointly owned and appreciation is shared; in Mudarabah assets belong to the Rabbul-mal and the Mudarib gains only on profitable sale","In Mudarabah the Rabbul-mal's liability is unlimited","Musharakah requires liquidation before any distribution"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 328.","topicId":"t12.5","chapter":12,"section":"12.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare modes","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.5","pages":[327,328]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.8","type":"identify","prompt":"A bank and client buy a house jointly; the bank leases its share to the client, who buys the bank's units monthly at a pre-agreed schedule. This is:","options":["Musharakah in trade (Shirkatul‘aqd)","Diminishing Musharakah in Shirkatulmilk","Mudarabah","Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 338, 340.","topicId":"t12.8","chapter":12,"section":"12.8","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify DM","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.8","pages":[337,339]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.9","type":"tf","prompt":"Preference shares carrying a fixed dividend are acceptable under Shari’ah principles as described in Chapter 12.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"They violate the principle of no gain without risk (p. 329).","topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"section":"12.6","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Evaluate shares","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[328,330]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.10","type":"order","prompt":"Order the documentation in a housing Diminishing Musharakah (Section 12.9.2).","items":["Musharakah agreement creating joint ownership","Rent agreement for the bank's undivided share","Unilateral undertaking to purchase units (with security)","Periodic purchase of units with falling rent","Title transferred to client"],"explanation":"pp. 340–341.","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Sequence DM","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.11","type":"application","prompt":"Box 12.5 variant: bank share 600,000; 120 monthly units; rent 6% p.a. on outstanding investment. First month's total payment is:","options":["5,000","8,000","3,000","5,300"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Unit 600,000/120 = 5,000; rent 600,000 × 6% / 12 = 3,000; total 8,000. (Practice variant — generated for learning.)","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Compute DM payment","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.12","type":"short","prompt":"Why can the unit price be pre-agreed in Diminishing Musharakah based on Shirkatulmilk but not in DM based on Shirkatul‘aqd?","keywords":["guarantee","capital","market value","joint ownership","rent"],"correctAnswer":"In contractual (trade) Musharakah, a promise to buy the financier's units at a pre-agreed or face value price would guarantee the financier's capital, which is prohibited; units must be bought at market value or a price agreed at the time. In Shirkatulmilk, the objective is joint ownership rather than profit through business; the bank earns rent on its leased share and a pre-agreed price schedule is permissible under Shari’ah rules.","explanation":"pp. 338–339.","topicId":"t12.8","chapter":12,"section":"12.8","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain DM pricing rule","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.8","pages":[337,339]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.13","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which cash flows are credited to a Running Musharakah account?","options":["Long-term finance for the project","Proceeds from the sale of fixed assets","Sale proceeds of finished goods","All of the above"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 333.","topicId":"t12.7.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.7.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall running Musharakah","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.2","pages":[332,334]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.14","type":"definition","prompt":"Ujratul-mithl in Mudarabah refers to:","options":["The Mudarib's share of profit","A fair wage paid to the Mudarib when the contract is invalid","The financier's capital","A penalty"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 321.","topicId":"t12.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define terms","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4","pages":[320,323]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.15","type":"scenario","prompt":"Sitara's operating profit for a year is Rs.50 million. The Level I profit entitlement rate is:","options":["12%","6%","2%","24%"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"50/100 × 12% = 6% (Box 12.3 formula).","topicId":"t12.7.3","chapter":12,"section":"12.7.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Sitara formula","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.3","pages":[334,337]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.16","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the Hanafi school, Mudarabah is a form of Shirkah because the Mudarib is liable for losses from the start.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"The Hanafis do NOT treat Mudarabah as Shirkah, precisely because the Mudarib is not liable for losses and is only an agent until profit arises (p. 312).","topicId":"t12.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the Hanafi view on the nature of Mudarabah","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2","pages":[308,312]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.17","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following disqualify a person from being a partner? (Select all.)","options":["Insanity","Being placed under court inhibition for insolvency","Being a non-Muslim","Minority, unless the guardian permits"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3],"explanation":"Musharakah with non-Muslims is allowed for lawful operations (pp. 312–313).","topicId":"t12.3.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify conditions for partners","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","pages":[312,313]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.18","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank joins a syndicate of conventional banks financing a large power project. What must it ensure?","options":["Nothing; syndicates are exempt","Its own portfolio consists of valid contracts such as Ijarah, with assurances that Shari’ah rules will be observed","It must receive the same interest rate as the others","It must withdraw"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 313.","topicId":"t12.3.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply rules on partnership with conventional banks","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","pages":[312,313]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.19","type":"comparison","prompt":"How do Abu Hanifa and later Hanafi law (Abu Yusuf and Muhammad) differ on an indebted person?","options":["No difference","Abu Hanifa: he cannot be inhibited (only imprisoned for non-payment); Abu Yusuf and Muhammad: he may be inhibited if creditors demand","Abu Hanifa: always inhibited","Both forbid imprisonment"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 313.","topicId":"t12.3.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare views on inhibition of debtors","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","pages":[312,313]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.20","type":"tf","prompt":"A Muslim and a non-Muslim may validly form a partnership to trade in liquor in an Islamic state, since liquor is prohibited only for Muslims.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"A non-Muslim may trade in such goods, but a partnership in that trade is void if any partner is a Muslim (p. 313).","topicId":"t12.3.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Distinguish goods prohibited for all from those prohibited for Muslims","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","pages":[312,313]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.21","type":"mcq","prompt":"On whether the amount of capital must be known at the time of contract, the Hanafis hold that:","options":["It must be known exactly at contract","It need not be known at contract but must be agreed before business begins","It never needs to be known","Only goods need valuation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Maliki, Hanbali and Shafi‘i jurists require it to be known at contract (p. 313).","topicId":"t12.3.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall school views on capital","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.2","pages":[313,314]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.22","type":"scenario","prompt":"Partner A contributed a shop worth 2 million to a Musharakah. Its value has since risen to 3 million. A claims the whole gain. What is the rule?","options":["A gets the gain because he contributed the shop","The gain belongs to all partners in their capital ratio, because the capital has been commingled","The gain is ignored","It goes to charity"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 314.","topicId":"t12.3.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the commingling rule","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.2","pages":[313,314]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.23","type":"identify","prompt":"When someone joins a running business as a partner, how are cash and receivables valued?","options":["At a discount for time value","At face value; foreign currency at the rate on the contract date","At the historical cost of the business","Excluded from capital"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Fixed assets are taken at an agreed value (p. 314).","topicId":"t12.3.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.2","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall valuation rules for a running business","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.2","pages":[313,314]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.24","type":"tf","prompt":"Partners in a Musharakah may make grants or loans out of partnership property without the consent of the other partners.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Only minor customary short-term loans are allowed. Grants or loans otherwise need all partners’ consent (p. 315).","topicId":"t12.3.3","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall limits on partners’ powers","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.3","pages":[314,316]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.25","type":"mcq","prompt":"In Shirkah al ‘Inan a partner is:","options":["Kafil but not Wakil","Wakil but not Kafil — agent, not indemnifier","Neither agent nor indemnifier","Both agent and full guarantor"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Al-Kasani adds that usage (‘Urf) can make indemnity a presumed condition (p. 315).","topicId":"t12.3.3","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the agency position in ‘Inan","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.3","pages":[314,316]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.26","type":"scenario","prompt":"An outside manager is appointed from the start to run a Musharakah for 30 % of profit. He also asks for a monthly salary. Is this allowed?","options":["Yes, both","No — as a Mudarib-type manager he is entitled only to his profit share","Only the salary","Yes, if the partners agree afterwards"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"A manager hired for a fixed salary (a Shirkah expense) may also get a good-management bonus, but a profit-share manager gets only the profit share (p. 315).","topicId":"t12.3.3","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply rules on managers’ remuneration","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.3","pages":[314,316]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.27","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of these are among the rights of partners listed by al-Sarakhsi (unless agreed otherwise)? (Select all.)","options":["Invest Shirkah capital in Mudarabah","Mortgage Shirkah property","Travel for the business at the Shirkah’s expense","Guarantee the other partners’ capital"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"Capital guarantees between partners are not allowed (pp. 315, 318).","topicId":"t12.3.3","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.3","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall partners’ rights","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.3","pages":[314,316]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.28","type":"order","prompt":"Order these steps in allocating profit when a Musharakah is valued at year-end.","items":["Carry out actual or constructive liquidation (valuation of assets)","Value receivables at cash value, net of doubtful debts","Deduct operating costs and expenses to arrive at net earnings","Apply the pre-agreed percentages to net earnings"],"explanation":"Box 12.1, rules 2 and 11 (pp. 319–320).","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Sequence profit allocation","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.29","type":"tf","prompt":"A Musharakah partner may agree to cap his profit at a fixed amount, with any excess going to the other partner.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Box 12.1, rule 6 (p. 320).","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply Box 12.1 profit rules","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.30","type":"identify","prompt":"Under the AAOIFI Standard, when may one partner bear the whole of a loss?","options":["Never","When he undertakes it at the time of the loss, without any prior condition","When it is written into the contract","Only if he is the managing partner"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 12.1, loss rule 2 (p. 320).","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Recall the voluntary loss rule","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.31","type":"order","prompt":"List the three cases in which negligence is established, in the order the book gives them.","items":["Not abiding by the contract’s terms and conditions","Working against the norms of the business concerned","Established ill-intention of the partner"],"explanation":"p. 318.","topicId":"t12.3.5","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the cases of negligence","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","pages":[318,318]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.32","type":"scenario","prompt":"A third party guaranteed B’s capital in a Musharakah, then refused to pay after a loss. B says the Musharakah is void and he need not meet his obligations. Is B right?","options":["Yes","No — the guarantee is only a promise to guarantee; the Shirkah cannot be tied to its fulfilment","Yes, if the guarantor owns more than 50 %","Only for profit, not capital"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 318.","topicId":"t12.3.5","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the third-party guarantee rule","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","pages":[318,318]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.33","type":"multi","prompt":"Which events terminate a traditional Shirkah? (Select all.)","options":["The specific purpose is achieved","All the capital is lost","A partner is barred from exercising legal powers over his property","A partner’s share rises above 50 %"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"Also withdrawal with notice and death (pp. 318–319).","topicId":"t12.3.6","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.6","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall termination events","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.6","pages":[318,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.34","type":"tf","prompt":"When one of three partners withdraws with due notice, the partnership automatically ends for the other two.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Withdrawal does not end the partnership among the remaining partners (pp. 318–319).","topicId":"t12.3.6","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Understand withdrawal rules","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.6","pages":[318,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.35","type":"mcq","prompt":"Qirad and Muqaradah are:","options":["Types of Murabaha","Terms for Mudarabah used in the Hijaz (Maliki and Shafi‘i usage)","Forms of Ijarah","Names of deposit pools"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 320–321.","topicId":"t12.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Mudarabah terminology","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4","pages":[320,323]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.36","type":"short","prompt":"In what two situations can a guarantee taken from a Mudarib to return the funds be enforced?","keywords":["negligen","breach"],"correctAnswer":"Only if the Mudarib is negligent in using the funds or breaches the terms of the contract.","explanation":"p. 322.","topicId":"t12.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall limits on guarantees from a Mudarib","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4","pages":[320,323]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.37","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which schools allow the Rabbul-mal to work alongside the Mudarib?","options":["All schools","The Hanbalis and, to some extent, the Hanafis; the majority do not","Only the Shafi‘is","None"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The majority fear it limits the Mudarib’s discretion (p. 325).","topicId":"t12.4.3","chapter":12,"section":"12.4.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Compare views on the financier working","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.3","pages":[325,325]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.38","type":"application","prompt":"A Rabbul-mal says: “If you trade in wheat you get 50 % of profit; in cloth, 40 %.” Is this valid?","options":["No, one ratio only","Yes — different ratios may be agreed for different situations","Only if the wheat is delivered","Only in Musharakah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 325.","topicId":"t12.4.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Mudarabah profit rules","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","pages":[325,327]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.39","type":"scenario","prompt":"A Mudarabah contract stated no profit ratio, and there is no customary practice. What follows?","options":["Profit is shared 50:50","The contract is void ab initio and the Mudarib gets the market wage for his work","The Mudarib takes all the profit","The court fixes a ratio"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 326.","topicId":"t12.4.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule on a missing ratio","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","pages":[325,327]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.40","type":"identify","prompt":"Which jurist allowed the Mudarib to draw daily food expenses from the Mudarabah account?","options":["Imam Shafi‘i","Imam Ahmad","Imam Malik","Imam Zufar"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The Hanafis restrict the claim to expenses on business journeys (p. 326).","topicId":"t12.4.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall views on Mudarib expenses","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","pages":[325,327]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.41","type":"application","prompt":"Mudarabah deals: A makes 50,000 profit, B makes 20,000 loss. The ratio is Rabbul-mal 60 : Mudarib 40. What does the Mudarib receive?","options":["20,000","12,000","28,000","0"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Profit first offsets the loss: net 30,000 × 40 % = 12,000 (pp. 326–327).","topicId":"t12.4.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Calculate Mudarabah profit after offsetting losses","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","pages":[325,327]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.42","type":"multi","prompt":"When can a Mudarabah NOT be terminated unilaterally? (Select all.)","options":["After the Mudarib has commenced business, until liquidation","Within an agreed fixed duration, except by mutual consent","At any time before the capital is handed over","Whenever the Rabbul-mal is dissatisfied with profit"],"correctAnswer":[0,1],"explanation":"p. 327.","topicId":"t12.4.5","chapter":12,"section":"12.4.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Recall termination rules for Mudarabah","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.5","pages":[327,327]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.43","type":"match","prompt":"Match each feature to Musharakah or Mudarabah.","pairs":[["Liability normally unlimited","Musharakah"],["Liability limited to investment","Mudarabah"],["All gain from asset appreciation","Musharakah"],["Assets valued in money before distribution on dissolution","Mudarabah"]],"explanation":"pp. 327–328.","topicId":"t12.5","chapter":12,"section":"12.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Musharakah and Mudarabah","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.5","pages":[327,328]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.44","type":"tf","prompt":"An underwriter of a share issue may charge a fee for the underwriting commitment itself.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Underwriting must be without consideration; fees are allowed only for other services (p. 330).","topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"section":"12.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall rules on underwriting","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[328,330]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.45","type":"comparison","prompt":"How do classical Shafi‘i and Hanafi jurists differ on selling a business of mixed liquid and tangible assets?","options":["Both forbid it","Shafi‘i: tangible assets must be separated and sold independently; Hanafi: the whole may be sold for more than its liquid part","Hanafi: at face value only","Shafi‘i: allowed if liquid assets exceed 50 %"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Most contemporary scholars require non-liquid assets above 50 %; some say 33 % (p. 330).","topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"section":"12.6","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare views on trading mixed-asset shares","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[328,330]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.46","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank adds its own equity to a deposit pool three months after the pool was formed. What is its relationship with the pool members?","options":["Musharakah","Mudarabah — the bank invests like any other depositor","Loan","Wakalah only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Equity added at creation would make it Musharakah (p. 331).","topicId":"t12.7.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply rules on bank equity in pools","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","pages":[331,332]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.47","type":"multi","prompt":"Which practices in a Mudarabah deposit pool are consistent with the book? (Select all.)","options":["Higher weightage for longer tenors","Preferential weightage for pensioners","Charging head-office expenses to the pool","Changing weightages only at the start of an accounting period"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3],"explanation":"General and head-office expenses fall on the bank (p. 331).","topicId":"t12.7.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply deposit-pool rules","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","pages":[331,332]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.48","type":"tf","prompt":"On the Hanafi view, a pool may allow early withdrawal if its non-liquid assets exceed 10 % of total worth.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Otherwise the pool should hold more than 50 % Ijarah or fixed assets (p. 332).","topicId":"t12.7.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall liquidity thresholds for pools","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","pages":[331,332]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.49","type":"comparison","prompt":"Leasing an undivided share (Musha‘a) to a third party is allowed by:","options":["Abu Hanifa and Zufar","Malik, Shafi‘i and Abu Yusuf","All four Imams","No one"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Leasing it to a co-owner is agreed by all (pp. 337–338).","topicId":"t12.8","chapter":12,"section":"12.8","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Compare views on leasing Musha‘a","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.8","pages":[337,339]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.50","type":"identify","prompt":"According to OIC Fiqh Academy Resolution No. 2, in DM for trade the sale undertaking can be binding on:","options":["The client only","The financier only, at market prices at the time of sale","Both at a pre-agreed price","Neither"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 340.","topicId":"t12.9.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the OIC ruling on trade DM","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.1","pages":[339,340]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.51","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank finances house construction on the client’s own land through DM. When should it start selling its units to the client?","options":["Immediately","One year after the last instalment, to avoid Bai‘ al ‘Inah","After ten years","When the house is sold"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Box 12.4 (p. 341).","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Box 12.4 sequencing","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.52","type":"application","prompt":"Box 12.5: bank share 800,000; 120 units; rent 7 % p.a. on outstanding investment. After month 1 the outstanding investment is 793,333.3. What is the month-2 rent?","options":["4,666.67","4,627.78","6,666.67","4,588.89"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"793,333.3 × 7 % ÷ 12 = 4,627.78 (p. 342).","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Calculate DM rent","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.53","type":"tf","prompt":"In DM housing finance, if a client delays unit purchases, the bank’s income falls further than under Murabaha.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Rent continues on the bank’s units, so the loss of income is far less than under Murabaha or simple leasing (p. 342).","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain DM’s built-in risk mitigation","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q12.54","type":"mcq","prompt":"In Mudarabah accounting (Box 12.6), a loss that occurs at the inception of work is:","options":["Borne by the Mudarib","Borne by the Islamic bank","Deferred to profits","Split equally"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Later losses do not affect the measurement of capital; total loss without misconduct ends the Mudarabah (pp. 344–345).","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall accounting treatment of Mudarabah","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch12-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"What distinguishes Shirkah-based participatory modes from other Islamic financing modes?","explanation":"Risk-bearing is a precondition for profit legitimacy in ANY Islamic mode; what distinguishes Shirkah-based modes specifically is DIRECT participation in both profit and loss.","topicId":"t12.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.1","topicLabel":"Participatory Modes and the Concept of Shirkah","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Shirkah and distinguish Shirkatulmilk from Shirkatul'aqd.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.1","pages":[307]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Only participatory modes involve any business risk","Direct participation in both profit AND loss by the parties","Participatory modes never require capital","Participatory modes are always short-term"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-2","type":"tf","prompt":"In Shirkatulmilk (partnership by ownership), partners are automatically agents of each other, just as in Shirkatul'aqd.","explanation":"False - in Shirkatulmilk, partners are explicitly NOT agents of each other (each is a legal 'stranger' to the other's share); mutual agency applies only in Shirkatul'aqd.","topicId":"t12.2.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.2.1","topicLabel":"Shirkatulmilk vs Shirkatul'aqd","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Shirkatulmilk from Shirkatul'aqd.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.1","pages":[309]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch12-3","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which two forms of Shirkah enjoy universal Ijma'a (jurist consensus), recognized as lawful by even the strictest schools (Shafi'e, Jafari, Zahirite)?","explanation":"Shirkah al 'Inan and Mudarabah are the only two forms recognized by Shafi'e, Jafari, and Zahirite (Ibn Hazm) jurists as lawful joint-venture forms.","topicId":"t12.2.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","topicLabel":"Sub-types of Contractual Partnership","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish the various sub-types of contractual partnership.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","pages":[311]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Mufawadah and Wujooh","Shirkah al 'Inan and Mudarabah","A'mal and Mufawadah","Wujooh and A'mal"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-4","type":"scenario","prompt":"Two partners agree to jointly purchase goods on credit using their personal creditworthiness (without immediate payment), then sell them and split the profit. Imam Shafi'e views this as unlawful, but which schools validate it?","explanation":"This describes Shirkatul Wujooh (partnership in creditworthiness); Hanafi and Hanbali jurists validate it, while Shafi'e deems it unlawful and Maliki accepts it only with conditions clarified upfront.","topicId":"t12.2.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","topicLabel":"Sub-types of Contractual Partnership","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Distinguish the various sub-types of contractual partnership.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","pages":[310,311]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Hanafi and Hanbali","Maliki and Jafari","Only Zahirite","No school validates it"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch12-5","type":"tf","prompt":"A partnership between a Muslim and a non-Muslim to trade in alcohol (lawful for the non-Muslim alone) would be considered void under Shari'ah.","explanation":"True - alcohol is prohibited for Muslims only; a non-Muslim can lawfully trade it, but a partnership in such trade becomes void if any party involved is Muslim.","topicId":"t12.3.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","topicLabel":"Conditions with Respect to Partners in Musharakah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the conditions governing partners in Musharakah.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","pages":[313]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch12-6","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does 'commingling' of Musharakah capital require?","explanation":"Commingling means individual ownership is replaced by collective ownership; the merger can be actual or constructive, based on agreed valuation, not necessarily physical pooling at contract time.","topicId":"t12.3.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.2","topicLabel":"Rules Relating to Musharakah Capital","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the rules governing Musharakah capital.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.2","pages":[313,314]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Physical, indistinguishable pooling of identical cash at contract signing only","Individual ownership replaced by collective joint-venture ownership - which can be actual or constructive (agreed valuation)","Each partner must retain exclusive ownership of the specific asset he contributed","Commingling is not required in Musharakah at all"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-7","type":"scenario","prompt":"Partner A invests 40% of Musharakah capital and Partner B invests 60%. A works full-time managing the business; B is a sleeping partner who declared this status upfront. Per Abu Hanifa's view, can A's profit share exceed 40%, and can B's profit share exceed 60%?","explanation":"Profit ratio can differ from investment ratio to reflect work contribution, so A (working partner) can get more than 40%. But per Abu Hanifa, a partner who explicitly declares himself sleeping cannot get MORE than his capital ratio - so B is capped at 60%.","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","topicLabel":"Treatment of Profit and Loss in Musharakah","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the profit/loss rules of Musharakah.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Neither can exceed their capital ratio","A's profit share CAN exceed 40% (reflecting his work); B's profit share CANNOT exceed 60% (he declared himself sleeping)","Both can exceed their capital ratios freely","Only B's share can exceed his capital ratio"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-8","type":"tf","prompt":"In Musharakah, loss can be shared in a ratio different from the partners' capital investment, if mutually agreed.","explanation":"False - there is complete jurist unanimity that loss must be shared EXACTLY in proportion to capital invested; only the profit ratio can be varied by agreement.","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","topicLabel":"Treatment of Profit and Loss in Musharakah","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Apply the profit/loss rules of Musharakah.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[317]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch12-9","type":"mcq","prompt":"Under what circumstance can a partner's capital/profit in Shirkah be effectively protected by another party?","explanation":"A qualifying independent third party can guarantee capital loss subject to the four conditions given (ownership caps, non-conditionality, no consideration) - but co-partners themselves cannot guarantee each other's capital/profit.","topicId":"t12.3.5","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","topicLabel":"Guarantees in Shirkah Contracts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the rules on guarantees in Shirkah contracts.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","pages":[318]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["A co-partner can directly guarantee another partner's capital","A genuine third party (not owning >50% of the venture, and not >50%-owned by it) can guarantee capital loss, provided the guarantee is not a condition of the Shirkah contract and not for consideration","No form of guarantee is ever permitted in Shirkah","Only the bank's own shareholders can provide such a guarantee"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-10","type":"mcq","prompt":"Is Musharakah, by default, a binding or non-binding contract?","explanation":"Musharakah is basically a non-binding contract - any partner can withdraw at will, though the partners can agree on a specific timeframe.","topicId":"t12.3.6","chapter":12,"section":"12.3.6","topicLabel":"Maturity and Termination of Musharakah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the termination rules of Musharakah.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.6","pages":[318]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Binding - no partner can ever withdraw","Non-binding - any partner can withdraw at will, unless the partners have agreed on a specific timeframe","It depends solely on regulatory rules","Musharakah has no concept of termination"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-11","type":"short","prompt":"In Mudarabah, who bears the financial loss, and what does the Mudarib 'lose' if the venture is unprofitable?","explanation":"This reflects the core Mudarabah loss-allocation rule described in section 12.4.","topicId":"t12.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.4","topicLabel":"The Concept and Rules of Mudarabah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain Mudarabah's structure and loss treatment.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4","pages":[320,321]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The financier (Rabbul-mal) bears all financial loss (erosion of his invested capital). The Mudarib's only 'loss' is his already-expended time and effort, for which he receives no remuneration if the venture is unprofitable."},{"id":"q-ch12-12","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why is it prohibited to convert an existing debt owed by the Mudarib into Mudarabah capital?","explanation":"The prohibition specifically guards against a financier converting an interest-bearing loan into a Mudarabah, effectively securing both principal recovery and an illegal return camouflaged as profit share.","topicId":"t12.4.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.4.1","topicLabel":"The Nature of Mudarabah Capital","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the nature of Mudarabah capital.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.1","pages":[322]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because debts are always worth less than their face value","Because it would guard against disguising a usurious loan as Mudarabah, where the financier secures both debt recovery and an illegal return under cover of profit-sharing","Because debts cannot legally exist in Islamic finance","Because the Mudarib would then own the capital outright"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-13","type":"scenario","prompt":"A financier tells a Mudarib: 'You must sell the goods immediately whenever a bargain is profitable, even if you'd prefer to wait for a better price.' Is this a permissible condition?","explanation":"The chapter notes that according to some jurists, a financier may compel the worker to sell goods if profitable, while the worker wants to hold - this is one of the permissible conditions a financier can impose.","topicId":"t12.4.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.4.2","topicLabel":"Types of Mudarabah and Conditions","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify the conditions a financier can validly impose on a Mudarib.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.2","pages":[324]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No, this is never permissible under any school","According to some jurists, yes - a financier may compel the worker to sell if the bargain is profitable, even against the worker's preference to hold","Only if the Mudarib agrees to forfeit his entire profit share","This automatically converts the contract into Musharakah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-14","type":"tf","prompt":"A Mudarabah contract can validly stipulate a lump-sum dollar amount of profit for the Mudarib.","explanation":"False - like Musharakah, a lump-sum amount as profit/return for either party is not allowed; profit must be a percentage/ratio of actual accrued profit.","topicId":"t12.4.4","chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","topicLabel":"Treatment of Profit/Loss in Mudarabah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain profit treatment rules in Mudarabah.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","pages":[325]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch12-15","type":"mcq","prompt":"In which two situations does an otherwise non-binding Mudarabah become binding?","explanation":"Mudarabah becomes binding once the Mudarib has commenced business (binding until actual/constructive liquidation), or when the parties agreed on a specific duration (binding until expiry, absent mutual consent to end early).","topicId":"t12.4.5","chapter":12,"section":"12.4.5","topicLabel":"Termination of a Mudarabah Contract","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the termination rules of Mudarabah.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.5","pages":[326]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["When the Mudarib is a family member, or when the capital exceeds a certain amount","Once the Mudarib has commenced business (binding until liquidation), or when the parties agreed to a specific contract duration","Only if a government regulator mandates it","Mudarabah is always binding from day one"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-16","type":"mcq","prompt":"Match each feature to Musharakah or Mudarabah: (1) All partners can participate in management; (2) Assets solely owned by the capital provider; (3) Liability normally unlimited for partners.","explanation":"Management rights and unlimited liability characterize Musharakah; sole asset ownership by the capital provider characterizes Mudarabah.","topicId":"t12.5","chapter":12,"section":"12.5","topicLabel":"Mudarabah Distinguished from Musharakah","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Distinguish Mudarabah from Musharakah.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.5","pages":[327,328]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"matching","options":["1-Musharakah, 2-Mudarabah, 3-Musharakah","1-Mudarabah, 2-Musharakah, 3-Mudarabah","1-Musharakah, 2-Musharakah, 3-Mudarabah","1-Mudarabah, 2-Mudarabah, 3-Musharakah"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch12-17","type":"scenario","prompt":"Company X's capital consists entirely of cash raised from a recent share issue, not yet invested in any assets. An investor wants to buy Company X's shares at a price above face value. Is this permissible?","explanation":"While a company's capital remains liquid (cash/receivables), shares represent only money and can only be sold at face value - selling above face value would be Riba (money exchanged for more money).","topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"section":"12.6","topicLabel":"Modern Corporations: Joint Stock Companies","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the rules on trading shares of joint stock companies.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[329,330]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, share prices are always freely negotiable","No - while capital remains purely liquid (cash), shares can only be sold at face value, since selling above face value would be exchanging money for more money (Riba)","Yes, provided the buyer is a Muslim","No, shares of any company can never be traded"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-18","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why are preference shares (giving fixed dividend priority) not Shari'ah-compliant?","explanation":"If a partner is privileged to share in profits only without sharing losses, the partnership contract is deemed void due to the element of interest, since this breaks the fundamental risk-sharing link.","topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"section":"12.6","topicLabel":"Modern Corporations: Joint Stock Companies","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the rules governing preference shares.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[329]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["They are too complex administratively","The core principle 'no gain without loss risk' is violated - sharing profit without sharing loss makes the partnership contract void due to an interest element","They are only illegal in certain countries","Preference shares are actually fully compliant"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-19","type":"short","prompt":"On the deposits side of Islamic banking, why does the overall depositor-bank relationship remain Mudarib/Rabbul-mal even when the bank technically forms a Musharakah relationship by contributing its own equity to the pool?","explanation":"This reflects the nuanced treatment in section 12.7.1 of deposit pool structuring.","topicId":"t12.7.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","topicLabel":"Modern Application of Shirkah: Deposits Side","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe how Shirkah is applied on the deposits side of Islamic banking.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","pages":[331]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Because depositors function as sleeping partners - unlike true Musharakah partners, they have no right to participate in the business's overall management or appreciation. So even though the bank's equity contribution at pool creation technically forms a Musharakah relationship within the pool, the overall depositor-bank dynamic remains that of Mudarib and Rabbul-mal."},{"id":"q-ch12-20","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the chapter say Musharakah is particularly well-suited to international trade finance?","explanation":"The chapter states Musharakah can be applied in trade finance without complexities since fraud/negligence/other-problem risks are relatively lower in international trade than in other Musharakah-based projects.","topicId":"t12.7.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.7.2","topicLabel":"Modern Application of Shirkah: Assets Side","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe how Shirkah is applied on the assets side of Islamic banking.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.2","pages":[332]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because international trade involves no documentation","Because fraud, negligence, and other problem risks are relatively lower in international trade than in other Musharakah-based projects","Because international trade never involves currency risk","Because banks are legally required to use Musharakah for trade"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-21","type":"tf","prompt":"The author attributes Musharakah's limited use by Islamic banks primarily to an inherent Shari'ah defect in the mode.","explanation":"False - the author attributes it to lack of documentation, defective taxation systems, and insufficient banker effort, arguing banks have favored the easier, less risky Murabaha/leasing alternatives rather than any Shari'ah limitation.","topicId":"t12.7.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.7.2","topicLabel":"Modern Application of Shirkah: Assets Side","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's critique of Musharakah's underuse.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.2","pages":[334]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch12-22","type":"mcq","prompt":"How many subcontracts does a Diminishing Musharakah arrangement typically involve for a leasable asset like a house?","explanation":"For leasable assets, DM involves three subcontracts: partnership by ownership, leasing by one partner of its share to the other(s), and selling by one partner of its share to the other(s).","topicId":"t12.8","chapter":12,"section":"12.8","topicLabel":"Diminishing Musharakah: Concept","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Walk through the Diminishing Musharakah structure.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.8","pages":[337]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["One","Two","Three (partnership, lease, sale)","Five"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch12-23","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank structures a Diminishing Musharakah where the partnership, lease, and sale are all stipulated collectively as one interdependent package, such that each is conditional on the others. Is this Shari'ah-compliant?","explanation":"The chapter is explicit: contemporary jurist opinion approves DM only when the subcontracts are separately enforceable and not conditional on one another; collective/interdependent stipulation is not approved.","topicId":"t12.8","chapter":12,"section":"12.8","topicLabel":"Diminishing Musharakah: Concept","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Walk through the Diminishing Musharakah structure and its Shari'ah requirements.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.8","pages":[338]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, this is the standard and preferred structure","No - jurists require the subcontracts to be entered separately, remain independent, and not be conditional on one another; a collectively-stipulated interdependent package is not approved","Yes, provided all parties are aware of the bundling","No, because DM can never involve leasing at all"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-24","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank finances a housing purchase via Diminishing Musharakah on a Shirkatulmilk basis. Can the client's promise to purchase the bank's ownership units specify a PRE-AGREED FIXED price?","explanation":"This is the crucial distinction: in DM via Shirkatulmilk (as typically used for housing finance), a pre-agreed price IS permissible - unlike DM via Shirkatul'aqd (trade), where only market value or a price fixed at actual sale time is allowed.","topicId":"t12.8","chapter":12,"section":"12.8","topicLabel":"Diminishing Musharakah: Concept","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Distinguish DM via Shirkatulmilk from DM via Shirkatul'aqd.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.8","pages":[338,339]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No, this is never allowed in any form of DM","Yes - in DM via Shirkatulmilk (ownership partnership, e.g. housing), one partner CAN purchase the co-partner's units at a pre-agreed price","Only if the bank waives all profit","Only with central bank approval"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-25","type":"mcq","prompt":"In trade-financing Diminishing Musharakah, whose promise (financier's or entrepreneur's) is made binding, per OIC Fiqh Academy Resolution No. 2?","explanation":"Per OIC Fiqh Academy Resolution No. 2 and IRTI research, the sale provision can be made binding ONLY on the financier partner; the entrepreneur may voluntarily buy but is never obligated to.","topicId":"t12.9.1","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.1","topicLabel":"Diminishing Musharakah as a Financing Mode: Housing, Trade, Documentation","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply the Diminishing Musharakah structure to trade finance.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.1","pages":[340]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Only the entrepreneur partner's promise to buy","Only the financier partner's promise to sell - the entrepreneur is never obligated to buy","Both parties' promises are equally binding","Neither party's promise is binding"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-26","type":"mcq","prompt":"Arrange the standard housing-finance Diminishing Musharakah documentation sequence: (A) Undertaking to purchase units of the bank's share; (B) Musharakah agreement creating joint ownership; (C) Rent agreement (Ijarah rules).","explanation":"The sequence is: (1) Musharakah agreement creating joint ownership, (2) rent agreement (signed after the Musharakah agreement), (3) undertaking to purchase units.","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","topicLabel":"Diminishing Musharakah as a Financing Mode: Housing, Trade, Documentation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Walk through the Diminishing Musharakah documentation procedure.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"ordering","options":["B, C, A","A, B, C","C, B, A","B, A, C"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch12-27","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank buys a portion of a customer's own land to fund construction of a house on it via Diminishing Musharakah, disbursing the last instalment in month 4. When can the bank begin selling its ownership units back to the client?","explanation":"Shari'ah scholars suggest a one-year gap after the last disbursement before the bank begins selling units back, specifically to avoid Bai' al 'Inah - during this period the client pays only rent (no unit purchases, no rent decrease).","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","topicLabel":"Diminishing Musharakah as a Financing Mode: Housing, Trade, Documentation","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Diminishing Musharakah rules to construction financing.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[341]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Immediately after the house is complete","One year after the disbursement of the LAST instalment, to avoid Bai' al 'Inah","Only after 5 years","The bank can never sell units back to the client"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch12-28","type":"tf","prompt":"If a Diminishing Musharakah housing client delays paying rent, the bank's income loss is generally MORE severe than under Murabaha or simple leasing.","explanation":"False - the bank's loss is far LESS severe than in Murabaha or simple leasing, since the customer continues paying rental on the bank-owned units regardless of the delay (the rent just doesn't decrease).","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","topicLabel":"Diminishing Musharakah as a Financing Mode: Housing, Trade, Documentation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the risk-mitigation features of Diminishing Musharakah.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[342]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch12-29","type":"short","prompt":"If a Diminishing Musharakah housing client wants to purchase multiple ownership units at once, how do Islamic banks typically handle any capital gain on the property's value?","explanation":"This reflects the multi-unit purchase and valuation-sharing mechanism described in section 12.9.2.","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","topicLabel":"Diminishing Musharakah as a Financing Mode: Housing, Trade, Documentation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain risk-mitigation mechanisms in Diminishing Musharakah.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[342,343]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Banks typically conduct a valuation of the house and share in the capital gain appreciation, generally up to a pre-stipulated rate (e.g. 3-4%) on their part of the investment, giving the remainder to the client - a built-in risk-mitigation mechanism."},{"id":"q-ch12-30","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is a 'Balance Transfer Facility (BTF)' in the context of Diminishing Musharakah?","explanation":"The chapter describes BTF as a Diminishing Musharakah facility specifically for replacing interest-based housing loans with a Shari'ah-compliant arrangement.","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","topicLabel":"Diminishing Musharakah as a Financing Mode: Housing, Trade, Documentation","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify practical applications of Diminishing Musharakah.","source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["A way to transfer money between bank branches","A Diminishing Musharakah arrangement used to replace an existing interest-based housing loan with a Shari'ah-compliant structure","A type of Salam contract","A penalty for late Ijarah payment"],"correctAnswer":1}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f12.1","front":"Shirkah","back":"Two or more persons combining capital, labour or creditworthiness, with similar rights and liabilities, to share profit and bear loss by proportionate ownership.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t12.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2","pages":[308,312]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.2","front":"Shirkatulmilk vs Shirkatul‘aqd","back":"Partnership in ownership (joint ownership, not for profit; co-owners not agents) vs partnership by contract (offer and acceptance for profit; partners mutual agents).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t12.2.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.1","pages":[309,309]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.3","front":"Shirkah al ‘Inan","back":"General partnership: each partner is Wakil (agent) not Kafil (surety); profit by agreement, loss by capital; consensus of all jurists.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t12.2.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","pages":[309,312]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.4","front":"Shirkatul Wujooh","back":"Partnership in creditworthiness: joint credit purchase and sale; loss by liability undertaken. Valid for Hanafi/Hanbali; Shafi‘i unlawful.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t12.2.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","pages":[309,312]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.5","front":"Golden rule of Shirkah P&L","back":"‘Profit is based on agreement of the parties, but loss is always subject to the ratio of investment.’","category":"Principles","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.6","front":"Sleeping partner's profit share (Hanafi)","back":"Cannot exceed his capital ratio; a non-sleeping partner may take more than his capital ratio by agreement.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.7","front":"Prohibited profit stipulations in Musharakah/Mudarabah","back":"A lump sum for any partner, or a rate tied to capital invested.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.8","front":"Third-party guarantee in Musharakah — conditions","back":"Guarantor ≤50% owner of venture; venture ≤50% owner of guarantor; Shirkah not conditional on it; no fee.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t12.3.5","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","pages":[318,318]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.9","front":"Buy-out promise in contractual Musharakah","back":"At market value or price agreed on the date of buying — not at a pre-agreed price or face value (capital guarantee).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.3.6","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.6","pages":[318,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.10","front":"Mudarabah","back":"Capital from Rabbul-mal, management from Mudarib; profit by agreed ratio; loss on capital owner only; void → Ujratul-mithl.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t12.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4","pages":[320,323]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.11","front":"Can a debt be used as Mudarabah capital?","back":"No — it could disguise a usurious loan earning a return under cover of profit share.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t12.4.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.1","pages":[323,324]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.12","front":"Restricted vs unrestricted Mudarabah","back":"Restricted: financier specifies business, items, place or time. Unrestricted: Mudarib invests as he deems fit within custom.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t12.4.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.2","pages":[324,324]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.13","front":"Musharakah vs Mudarabah (seven points)","back":"Capital source; management; loss; liability (unlimited vs limited); profit timing; asset ownership/appreciation; valuation on dissolution.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t12.5","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.5","pages":[327,328]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.14","front":"Preference shares — Shari’ah status","back":"Non-compliant: fixed dividends or priority without equal risk of loss.","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[328,330]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.15","front":"Trading shares of a company whose assets are still liquid","back":"Only at face value; otherwise money is exchanged for more money (Riba).","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[328,330]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.16","front":"Deposit-side Mudarabah pool","back":"Depositors = Rabbul-mal, bank = Mudarib; direct expenses to pool, HQ costs to bank; disclosed weightages; loss by investment ratio.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t12.7.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","pages":[331,332]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.17","front":"Running Musharakah","back":"Client's operating sale proceeds credited to a running Musharakah account; investing/financing flows excluded; bank's share of operating profit paid periodically.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t12.7.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.2","pages":[332,334]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.18","front":"Diminishing Musharakah","back":"Partnership + lease of financier's share + periodic sale of units — separate contracts; rent falls as units are bought.","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t12.8","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.8","pages":[337,339]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.19","front":"Box 12.5 DM first payment","back":"Bank 800,000; unit 6,666.67; rent 800,000 × 7% ÷ 12 = 4,666.67; total 11,333.33.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.20","front":"Sitara TFCs (Box 12.3)","back":"Rs.360m, 5-year, 2002; Level I 12% on first Rs.100m operating profit; Level II 2% per extra Rs.100m, ¼ to Takaful reserve; paid 15–24%.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t12.7.3","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.3","pages":[334,337]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.21","front":"Wakil but not Kafil","back":"In Shirkah al ‘Inan a partner is agent of the others but not the indemnifier of their obligations to outsiders (p. 315).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t12.3.3","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.3","pages":[314,316]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.22","front":"Can capital be contributed in goods?","back":"Yes (Usmani): the goods’ market value at contract fixes the partner’s share. Contemporary jurists require valuation in money (p. 314).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.3.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.2","pages":[313,314]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.23","front":"Three cases of negligence in Shirkah","back":"(i) Breaching the contract terms; (ii) acting against the business’s norms; (iii) established ill-intention (p. 318).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.3.5","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","pages":[318,318]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.24","front":"Five ways a Shirkah ends","back":"Purpose achieved; withdrawal with notice; death (heirs may replace); capital lost; a partner barred from legal powers over property (pp. 318–319).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.3.6","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.6","pages":[318,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.25","front":"Qirad / Muqaradah","back":"The Hijazi (Maliki and Shafi‘i) names for Mudarabah (p. 321).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t12.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4","pages":[320,323]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.26","front":"Can the Rabbul-mal work in the Mudarabah?","back":"Majority and AAOIFI: no, only supervise. Hanbalis (and some Hanafis): yes. The author suggests leaving it to the parties today (p. 325).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t12.4.3","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.3","pages":[325,325]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.27","front":"Mudarabah with no ratio and no custom","back":"Void ab initio; the Mudarib gets the market wage for his work (p. 326).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.4.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","pages":[325,327]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.28","front":"When is a Mudarabah binding?","back":"Once the Mudarib has begun business (until liquidation), or during an agreed fixed term (except by mutual consent) (p. 327).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.4.5","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.5","pages":[327,327]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.29","front":"Underwriting in Shari’ah","back":"Must be free of charge. The underwriter may charge only for other services, e.g. feasibility studies or marketing (p. 330).","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[328,330]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.30","front":"Bank equity in a Mudarabah pool","back":"Musharakah if added at the pool’s creation; Mudarabah if added later. Head-office expenses are the bank’s own (p. 331).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t12.7.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","pages":[331,332]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.31","front":"Pool liquidity thresholds for early withdrawal","back":"More than 50 % Ijarah or fixed assets; on the Hanafi view, non-liquid assets above 10 % suffice (p. 332).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t12.7.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","pages":[331,332]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.32","front":"Musha‘a","back":"Undivided ownership of an asset. Leasing it to a co-owner is agreed; leasing to a third party is disputed (pp. 337–338).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t12.8","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.8","pages":[337,339]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.33","front":"DM in trade — unit price","back":"Cannot be fixed in the promise. It follows valuation at each sale; only the financier’s promise to sell can bind (OIC Res. 2) (p. 340).","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t12.9.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.1","pages":[339,340]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.34","front":"Why a one-year gap before unit sales (Box 12.4)?","back":"Scholars suggest it so that the sale-and-lease-back does not become a buy-back (Bai‘ al ‘Inah) (p. 341).","category":"Financing modes","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f12.35","front":"Floating rent in DM","back":"Allowed with a proper floor and cap to avoid Gharar (p. 343).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-1","front":"What is the key structural difference between Musharakah and Mudarabah?","back":"In Musharakah, all parties contribute funds and work; in Mudarabah, one party contributes funds (Rabbul-mal) while the other acts as entrepreneur/manager (Mudarib), with the financier bearing all financial loss.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t12.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.1","pages":[308]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-2","front":"What is the core difference between Shirkatulmilk and Shirkatul'aqd regarding agency?","back":"In Shirkatulmilk (ownership partnership), partners are NOT agents of each other. In Shirkatul'aqd (contract partnership), partners ARE mutual agents.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t12.2.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","pages":[309]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-3","front":"Name the three sub-types of Shirkatulamwal/Shirkatul'aqd by subject matter.","back":"Shirkatulamwal (capital-based), Shirkatula'mal (labour/service-based), and Shirkatulwujooh (creditworthiness-based).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t12.2.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","pages":[309,310]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-4","front":"Which two forms of Shirkah enjoy full, universal Ijma'a (unanimous jurist consensus)?","back":"Shirkah al 'Inan (General Partnership) and Mudarabah.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t12.2.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.2.2","pages":[311]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-5","front":"Can Musharakah be formed with non-Muslims or conventional banks?","back":"Yes - Musharakah can be concluded with non-Muslims and even interest-based banks to carry out Shari'ah-acceptable operations, provided proper assurances/guarantees ensure Shari'ah compliance.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.3.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.1","pages":[313]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-6","front":"What is the core rule that distinguishes Musharakah's treatment of profit vs loss?","back":"Profit ratio can differ from the capital investment ratio (by mutual agreement, reflecting work contribution), but loss MUST always be shared exactly in proportion to each partner's capital investment.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,317]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-7","front":"What is the governing maxim on profit/loss sharing in Shirkah, attributed to Caliph Ali (RA)?","back":"\"Profit is based on agreement of the parties, but loss is always subject to the ratio of investment.\"","category":"Principles","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[317]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-8","front":"Per Imam Abu Hanifa's view, what is the profit cap for a partner who explicitly declares himself a 'sleeping' (non-working) partner?","back":"His profit share cannot exceed his capital investment ratio (though it can be less). Hanbali jurists, however, allow even a sleeping partner to get more than his investment ratio.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-9","front":"Can a partner's profit or capital be guaranteed by co-partners in Shirkah?","back":"No - neither profit nor capital can be guaranteed by co-partners, since Shirkah assets are held as a trust; a co-partner can only demand security against proven misconduct or negligence.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t12.3.5","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","pages":[318]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-10","front":"Under what four conditions is a third-party guarantee of Musharakah capital loss permitted?","back":"The guarantor must not own more than 50% of the venture; the venture must not own more than 50% of the guarantor; the Shirkah contract must not be conditional on the guarantee; and the guarantee must not be for any consideration.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t12.3.5","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","pages":[318]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-11","front":"Is a promise to buy a Musharakah partner's assets at a PRE-FIXED price allowed?","back":"No - a promise to buy at a pre-agreed fixed price or face value is not allowed (constitutes a prohibited capital guarantee); a binding promise at MARKET VALUE or a price agreed at the time of purchase IS allowed.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.3.6","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.6","pages":[319]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-12","front":"Why can't an existing debt be used as Mudarabah capital?","back":"Converting debt into Mudarabah capital is prohibited to guard against disguising a usurious loan as a Mudarabah, where the financier would secure both debt recovery and an illegal return under cover of a profit share.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t12.4.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.1","pages":[322]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-13","front":"What is a Mudarib entitled to if the Mudarabah contract becomes invalid for any reason?","back":"Ujratul-mithl (fair market wage) for his work - not a profit share.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t12.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4","pages":[321]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-14","front":"Distinguish restricted from unrestricted Mudarabah.","back":"Restricted: the financier specifies a particular business, items, and time for the Mudarib. Unrestricted: the Mudarib is free to invest in any business he deems fit.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t12.4.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.2","pages":[324]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-15","front":"What happens if a Mudarib violates a restriction or contravenes a beneficial condition set by the financier?","back":"He becomes a 'usurper' and is responsible for the capital to the capital owner.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t12.4.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.2","pages":[324]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-16","front":"Can the financier (Rabbul-mal) work in the Mudarabah business, per the majority view?","back":"No - the majority of traditional jurists disallow it (it jeopardizes the Mudarib's discretion); only Hanbali (and partly Hanafi) jurists allow it. He always retains the right to oversee the Mudarib's fiduciary conduct.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t12.4.3","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.3","pages":[325]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-17","front":"What does 'loss' specifically mean in Mudarabah?","back":"Erosion of capital - no profit can be recognized or claimed unless the Mudarabah's original capital remains fully intact.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t12.4.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.4","pages":[325]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-18","front":"When does Mudarabah become a binding (non-terminable-at-will) contract?","back":"Once the Mudarib has actually commenced business (binding until liquidation), or where the parties agreed on a specific contract duration upfront (binding until expiry, absent mutual consent).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.4.5","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.4.5","pages":[326]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-19","front":"How does asset ownership differ between Musharakah and Mudarabah?","back":"In Musharakah, assets are jointly owned by all partners (each benefits from value appreciation). In Mudarabah, purchased assets are solely owned by the Rabbul-mal; the Mudarib only profits through a profitable sale.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t12.5","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.5","pages":[327,328]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-20","front":"Are preference shares (guaranteed dividend/liquidation priority) Shari'ah-compliant?","back":"No - the basic principle is 'no gain without undertaking loss risk'; a partner privileged to share profit without sharing loss makes the partnership contract void due to the interest element.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[329]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-21","front":"When can shares of a joint stock company be traded at any market price (not just face value)?","back":"Once the raised capital has been invested in nonliquid assets (land, buildings, machinery). While capital remains purely liquid (cash/receivables), shares can only be sold at face value.","category":"Capital markets","topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[329,330]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-22","front":"What nonliquid-asset threshold do most contemporary scholars require for trading shares of a mixed-asset company?","back":"Nonliquid assets must exceed 50% of the business (some scholars use a lower floor of 33%).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[330]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-23","front":"On an Islamic bank's deposits side, when does the relationship become Musharakah vs Mudarabah?","back":"Musharakah if the bank contributes its own equity to the pool AT pool creation; Mudarabah if contributed SUBSEQUENTLY. Overall, the relationship remains Mudarib/Rabbul-mal since depositors are effectively sleeping partners.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t12.7.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","pages":[331]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-24","front":"Why does the author say Musharakah has been underused by Islamic banks in practice?","back":"Mainly due to lack of documentation, defective taxation systems, and insufficient banker effort - banks have favored the easier, less risky alternatives of Murabaha and leasing, not any inherent Shari'ah limitation.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t12.7.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.2","pages":[334]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-25","front":"What is Musha'a, and why does it matter for Diminishing Musharakah?","back":"Undivided ownership - all co-owners own every part of the joint property pro rata; no partner can claim a specific separable portion. It's the underlying concept that makes progressive unit-by-unit buyout in DM possible.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t12.8","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.8","pages":[337]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-26","front":"What is the critical Shari'ah-compliance difference between DM via Shirkatul'aqd and DM via Shirkatulmilk regarding sale price?","back":"In Shirkatul'aqd (trade) DM, the sale price CANNOT be pre-fixed (market value/price-at-sale-time only). In Shirkatulmilk (ownership, e.g. housing) DM, one partner CAN purchase the other's units at a PRE-AGREED price.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t12.8","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.8","pages":[338,339]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-27","front":"In trade-financing Diminishing Musharakah, whose promise to sell/buy is binding, per OIC Fiqh Academy Resolution No. 2?","back":"Only the FINANCIER partner's promise to sell is binding; the entrepreneur partner is never obligated to buy, and any sale occurs at prices prevailing in the market at the time of actual sale.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t12.9.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.1","pages":[340]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch12-28","front":"In construction/renovation Diminishing Musharakah, why must there be a one-year gap after the last disbursement before the bank sells units back to the client?","back":"To avoid Bai' al 'Inah (prohibited buy-back) - the gap must be long enough that the leased property or its value might genuinely have changed.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[341]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e12.1","kind":"long","prompt":"Explain the rules of profit and loss sharing in Musharakah and Mudarabah, with reference to the views of the major schools and Box 12.1.","expectedStructure":["Golden rule and its source","Views of Malik/Shafi‘i vs Abu Hanifa/Ahmad","Sleeping partner rule","No lump sum or % of capital","Drawings and constructive liquidation","Mudarabah: loss on financier, capital intact before profit","Box 12.1 key rules"],"keyConcepts":["profit ratio","capital ratio","sleeping partner","constructive liquidation"],"essentialPoints":["Work as a factor of profit justifies differing ratios"],"commonMistakes":["Sharing loss by profit ratio","Allowing a fixed return"],"topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316,320]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e12.2","kind":"short","prompt":"Distinguish Mudarabah from Musharakah.","expectedStructure":["Capital","Management","Loss","Liability","Profit distribution","Ownership and appreciation","Dissolution"],"keyConcepts":["Rabbul-mal","Mudarib","limited liability"],"essentialPoints":["Use a table"],"commonMistakes":["Saying the Mudarib bears capital loss"],"topicId":"t12.5","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.5","pages":[327,328]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e12.3","kind":"scenario","prompt":"A client wants a home through Diminishing Musharakah. Structure the transaction and compute the first instalment for a 1.0m house, 80% bank share, 10 years, 7% rental rate.","expectedStructure":["Shirkatulmilk joint ownership","Rent agreement","Unilateral purchase undertaking and price schedule","Security","Payment computation (unit + rent)","Late payment and prepayment effects"],"keyConcepts":["Musha‘a","unit purchase","floor and cap"],"essentialPoints":["First payment 11,333.33"],"commonMistakes":["Making the three contracts conditional on each other","Pre-agreed price in trade DM"],"topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[340,346]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e12.4","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"Why have Islamic banks made limited use of Musharakah on the assets side? Discuss risks, obstacles and the author's view.","expectedStructure":["Risks cited by banks","Mitigation: security for negligence, certificates","Obstacles: documentation, taxation, banker effort","Author's view: Murabaha and leasing easier","Potential in trade and project finance"],"keyConcepts":["moral hazard","running Musharakah","TFCs"],"essentialPoints":["Sitara as a successful example"],"commonMistakes":["Presenting the author's view as consensus"],"topicId":"t12.7.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.2","pages":[332,334]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e12.5","kind":"viva","prompt":"Can a Musharakah partner guarantee the other partner's capital? Can anyone?","expectedStructure":["Trust principle","Security against negligence","Third-party guarantee conditions"],"keyConcepts":["Amanah","third-party guarantee"],"essentialPoints":["Guarantee is a promise, not a condition"],"commonMistakes":["Allowing co-partner guarantee"],"topicId":"t12.3.5","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.5","pages":[318,318]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e12.6","kind":"short","prompt":"State the Shari’ah rules for trading shares of joint stock companies.","expectedStructure":["Shirkah basis and limited liability","Halal business and purification","Liquid vs nonliquid assets","Preference and redeemable shares","Short-selling and pledging","Underwriting"],"keyConcepts":["50% rule","Riba"],"essentialPoints":["Liquid-capital shares trade at par"],"commonMistakes":["Allowing short sales"],"topicId":"t12.6","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.6","pages":[328,330]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e12.7","kind":"long","prompt":"Explain Diminishing Musharakah, distinguishing its use in Shirkatulmilk (housing) from Shirkatul‘aqd (trade). Why may a unit price be pre-agreed in one but not the other?","expectedStructure":["Definition and the three separate contracts","Ownership DM: rent, pre-agreed price schedule","Contractual DM: profit ratio, loss by equity, market-value sale","Why a fixed price in trade DM guarantees capital","OIC Res. 2 and the one-sided promise","Box 12.4 and 12.5 practice"],"keyConcepts":["Musha‘a","Shirkatulmilk","Shirkatul‘aqd","Guarantee of capital","Bai‘ al ‘Inah"],"essentialPoints":["Contracts must not be conditional on one another (p. 338)","In Shirkatulmilk a pre-agreed price is allowed (p. 338)","In trade DM the price follows valuation at each sale (pp. 339–340)","One-year gap in construction cases (p. 341)"],"commonMistakes":["Stipulating all three contracts as one package","Allowing a fixed buy-back price in trade DM","Ignoring the floor and cap on floating rent"],"topicId":"t12.9.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.1","pages":[339,340]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e12.8","kind":"short","prompt":"State the rules for managing a Mudarabah deposit pool in an Islamic bank.","expectedStructure":["Relationship","Expenses","Weightages","Bank equity","Losses","Liquidity"],"keyConcepts":["Pool","Weightage","Constructive liquidation"],"essentialPoints":["Direct expenses to the pool, head-office expenses to the bank","Weightages disclosed and changed only at the start of a period","Bank equity: Musharakah at creation, Mudarabah later","No Mudarib share if the pool makes a loss; loss shared by investment",">50 % fixed assets for early withdrawal (Hanafi >10 %)"],"commonMistakes":["Charging head-office costs to the pool","Weighting large deposits more as a matter of course"],"topicId":"t12.7.1","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.7.1","pages":[331,332]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-11","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain the rules governing profit and loss distribution in a Musharakah.","expectedStructure":["State the profit-ratio flexibility rule","State the loss-ratio rule (always per capital)","Note the exception for a purely sleeping partner","Cite the maxim from Ali (RA)"],"keyConcepts":["Musharakah","Profit-sharing ratio","Loss-sharing ratio"],"essentialPoints":["The profit-sharing ratio can be freely agreed between partners, and need not match the capital-contribution ratio, to reward a partner's labor/management.","Loss must always be shared strictly in proportion to each partner's capital contribution — there is complete jurist consensus on this point.","According to Imam Abu Hanifa, if a partner has expressly declared himself a sleeping (non-working) partner, his profit share cannot exceed his investment ratio.","The maxim attributed to Caliph Ali (RA): 'Profit is based on agreement of the parties, but loss is always subject to the ratio of investment.'"],"commonMistakes":["Assuming profit must also track the capital ratio — only loss is fixed to capital by consensus.","Forgetting the sleeping-partner exception on the profit side."],"topicId":"t12.3.4","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.3.4","pages":[316]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-12","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Distinguish Mudarabah from Musharakah on at least four grounds.","expectedStructure":["Cover source of capital","Cover management rights","Cover loss-bearing","Cover asset ownership and/or liability"],"keyConcepts":["Mudarabah","Musharakah","Rabbul-mal","Mudarib"],"essentialPoints":["In Musharakah, all partners contribute capital; in Mudarabah, capital comes only from the Rabbul-mal, not the Mudarib.","In Musharakah, all partners may take part in management; in Mudarabah, the Rabbul-mal has no right to participate in management.","In Musharakah, loss is shared per the capital ratio of all partners; in Mudarabah, financial loss is borne exclusively by the Rabbul-mal, unless the Mudarib was negligent or dishonest.","In Musharakah, assets are jointly owned by all partners in proportion to investment; in Mudarabah, assets purchased belong solely to the Rabbul-mal."],"commonMistakes":["Treating Mudarabah as just 'a Musharakah where one partner doesn't work' — the ownership and loss-liability differences are structural, not just about effort.","Missing the profit-distribution timing difference (periodic in Musharakah vs generally only on liquidation in Mudarabah)."],"topicId":"t12.5","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.5","pages":[327]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-13","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain how Diminishing Musharakah is structured for Shari'ah-compliant housing finance, and why the three contracts involved must stay separate.","expectedStructure":["Describe the three contracts (partnership, lease, unit purchase)","Explain the sequencing","Explain why bundling them would be problematic"],"keyConcepts":["Diminishing Musharakah","Shirkatulmilk","Musha'a"],"essentialPoints":["A Musharakah agreement first creates joint ownership between the bank and the client (Shirkatulmilk).","A separate rent (Ijarah) agreement lets the bank lease its undivided share to the client for rent.","A separate undertaking lets the client periodically purchase units of the bank's share per an agreed schedule, with rent decreasing as the client's ownership grows.","The three contracts must remain independent and separately enforceable; the Hanbali jurists, who otherwise permit some conditions in a sale, still do not approve a contract whose enforcement depends upon another contract."],"commonMistakes":["Describing Diminishing Musharakah as a single combined mortgage-like contract rather than three sequential, independent agreements.","Assuming the unit-purchase price can always be pre-fixed — this is only valid for DM by ownership (e.g. housing), not DM in trade."],"topicId":"t12.9.2","chapter":12,"source":{"chapter":12,"section":"12.9.2","pages":[339]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
